1
00:00:00,014 --> 00:00:03,360
Every week, there's some part of your work you still do by hand.

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:12,160
Whether it's renaming dozens of files one by one before you upload them, or digging through a data export manually to find important data,

3
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:25,280
or even rebuilding a chart over and over again every time data changes. A simple Python program could solve each one of these tasks. All it takes is someone to build the tool. That someone is you.

4
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:34,640
I'm Allie and I'll be leading you through this course along with our other presenter, Summer. In this course, we'll walk through the most important programming basics.

5
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:46,320
You'll get a chance to practice each one of these topics or group of topics with exercises we created and linked in the description. Then we'll walk through these exercises together, step by step.

6
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:52,880
By the end of this course, you'll have the skills you need to start writing the tools you've always wanted. Sound good?

7
00:00:53,200 --> 00:01:00,400
If you're ready to start your journey, the first thing we'll need is somewhere to write our Python code. Summer will help you set that up.

8
00:01:01,262 --> 00:01:11,040
Have you ever wanted to learn Python, but always felt like it was complicated and you didn't know where to start? Here's the good news: you don't have to install or configure anything.

9
00:01:11,374 --> 00:01:17,316
Stay with us, and in the next 90 seconds, you'll learn how to write and run your first line of Python code.

10
00:01:17,998 --> 00:01:20,080
We can do that thanks to Google Colab.

11
00:01:21,070 --> 00:01:27,300
It is a free tool that lets you write and run Python code directly in your browser. So let's jump in.

12
00:01:28,142 --> 00:01:33,156
To get started, we open our browser and make sure we're signed into our Google account.

13
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:40,720
Then we'll type Google Colab in the search bar and choose Colab in the search results.

14
00:01:41,646 --> 00:01:46,160
Once the page loads, at the bottom left of the window, we click New Notebook.

15
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:51,440
Perfect! Now we have our first Python notebook ready to use.

16
00:01:52,334 --> 00:01:53,760
See this empty box?

17
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:59,600
This is called a code cell, and it's where we'll write our first line of Python code.

18
00:02:00,494 --> 00:02:06,084
Let's start simply by telling Python to display on screen the text Hello World.

19
00:02:06,926 --> 00:02:12,640
We tell Python to display text by writing print, followed by a set of parentheses.

20
00:02:13,550 --> 00:02:19,040
Inside the parentheses, we write the text we want to print, between quotation marks.

21
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:23,748
Awesome! We've now written our first line of Python code.

22
00:02:24,590 --> 00:02:27,716
The next step is to tell Python to run the code.

23
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:32,740
To do that, we click the play button on the left side of the cell.

24
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:41,828
Nice! We can see from the green check next to the cell and the output that our code ran successfully and very quickly.

25
00:02:42,670 --> 00:02:48,196
In under 90 seconds, we've gone from Python feels complicated to actually coding.

26
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:56,132
Now, this is pretty basic stuff, but it's the first step in your Python journey. There's so much more we can do.

27
00:02:56,974 --> 00:03:01,668
For example, let's see how to use Python to do some calculations for us.

28
00:03:02,542 --> 00:03:04,292
We'll start by clearing our cell.

29
00:03:05,134 --> 00:03:08,996
Notice that when we do this, the cell output doesn't change.

30
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:13,280
To update the cell output, we need to run the cell again.

31
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:16,516
Great! Now we have a clean slate.

32
00:03:17,358 --> 00:03:21,252
Let's now do a simple calculation, say 5 plus 3.

33
00:03:22,126 --> 00:03:26,212
Running the cell, Python performs a calculation and displays 8.

34
00:03:27,054 --> 00:03:32,132
Notice that here we didn't use print and Python still outputs the answer.

35
00:03:32,974 --> 00:03:37,956
That's because Colab automatically shows the results of the last line in a cell.

36
00:03:38,798 --> 00:03:44,868
To see what we mean, let's add another calculation above our current one, say 2 plus 3.

37
00:03:45,710 --> 00:03:50,080
Running the cell, we only see 8, the result of the last line.

38
00:03:50,926 --> 00:03:55,332
The first calculation still runs, but its result isn't displayed.

39
00:03:56,174 --> 00:03:58,800
To see both results, we need to print them.

40
00:04:00,430 --> 00:04:04,164
Now, when we run the cell, both results appear.

41
00:04:05,006 --> 00:04:09,028
Awesome! To keep experimenting, let's add more cells.

42
00:04:09,870 --> 00:04:14,372
When we hover at the bottom or top of a cell, a few buttons appear.

43
00:04:15,214 --> 00:04:20,356
Clicking plus code adds a brand new code cell that's ready for more code.

44
00:04:21,198 --> 00:04:25,380
Let's try a different calculation this time. 12 times 7.

45
00:04:26,222 --> 00:04:28,548
Running the cell gives us 84.

46
00:04:29,422 --> 00:04:39,204
Each cell works independently, which means we can edit and rerun them whenever we want. This makes it really easy to experiment and try different things.

47
00:04:40,078 --> 00:04:46,116
But as we add more cells to our notebook, we might want to leave ourselves some notes about what our code does.

48
00:04:46,958 --> 00:04:51,940
To do this, we can add a different type of cell to the notebook, a text cell.

49
00:04:52,782 --> 00:04:57,188
Inside the text cell, we can leave ourselves a note or explain our code.

50
00:04:58,030 --> 00:05:05,188
They also allow us to format our text using something called markdown. These asterisks are markdown formatting.

51
00:05:06,062 --> 00:05:12,996
The text cells shows the raw text and markdown on the left and then previews what it will look like on the right.

52
00:05:13,838 --> 00:05:21,348
You don't need to know markdown to use text cells, but if you're curious about it, we left a link to learn more in the description.

53
00:05:22,190 --> 00:05:27,076
When we select close on the text cell, we see what was in the preview before.

54
00:05:27,918 --> 00:05:33,316
To edit this text cell, we simply double-click it and change the text to something new.

55
00:05:35,566 --> 00:05:40,580
Awesome! Now we know how to work with both code and text cells.

56
00:05:41,454 --> 00:05:48,068
But the current organization of our notebook, where the text cell follows the calculations, is not ideal.

57
00:05:48,910 --> 00:05:52,992
It would make more sense if the text cell was above the calculations.

58
00:05:53,838 --> 00:06:01,668
To move the cell above our two calculation cells, we can click the arrow on the upper right of the cell and move it to the top.

59
00:06:02,512 --> 00:06:03,556
Looks better.

60
00:06:04,398 --> 00:06:11,684
But as useful as text cells are, sometimes we also might want to add notes inside of our code cells.

61
00:06:12,526 --> 00:06:15,204
We can do this with code comments.

62
00:06:16,046 --> 00:06:19,236
To write a comment, we start with the hashtag symbol.

63
00:06:20,078 --> 00:06:22,692
Then we can write any text we want.

64
00:06:23,534 --> 00:06:27,748
When we run this cell, Python will ignore the comment. It's just for us.

65
00:06:28,622 --> 00:06:34,628
To confirm this, let's add our super secret calculation code below it and run the cell.

66
00:06:35,470 --> 00:06:38,692
Great! We only get the result of the calculation.

67
00:06:39,534 --> 00:06:43,844
Comments are helpful for reminding us of our code's purpose when we come back to it.

68
00:06:44,686 --> 00:06:50,052
Awesome! Now, as we work, we might end up with cells we no longer need.

69
00:06:50,894 --> 00:06:52,420
Let's see how to remove them.

70
00:06:53,262 --> 00:06:59,332
We can delete a cell by clicking on it and then clicking the trash icon in the top right corner of the cell.

71
00:07:00,206 --> 00:07:02,724
And poof! The cell is gone!

72
00:07:03,566 --> 00:07:11,044
Great! Now that we're comfortable running, adding, and removing cells, we're almost done building our first notebook.

73
00:07:11,886 --> 00:07:16,964
To finish up, let's give the notebook a useful name, like calculations practice.

74
00:07:18,542 --> 00:07:26,404
Great! This notebook is automatically saved in Google Drive. We can confirm this by clicking File, Locate, and Drive.

75
00:07:27,246 --> 00:07:31,844
We are then taken to a new tab where this notebook is saved in our drive.

76
00:07:32,686 --> 00:07:37,796
Perfect! Before we wrap up, let's head back to our notebook to learn a handy shortcut.

77
00:07:38,670 --> 00:07:44,708
To run a cell, instead of clicking the play button, we can press Shift plus Enter on our keyboard.

78
00:07:45,550 --> 00:07:52,132
This runs the cell and automatically moves us to the next one. Really useful when working through a notebook.

79
00:07:52,974 --> 00:07:59,172
To see what other shortcuts Colab offers, we can go to Tools and Keyboard Shortcuts.

80
00:07:59,982 --> 00:08:04,996
There are lots of options here. Feel free to explore and customize them how you'd like.

81
00:08:05,872 --> 00:08:13,348
Awesome! We've got Colab set up now and we know our way around a notebook. We even ran our first calculations adding two numbers together.

82
00:08:13,632 --> 00:08:23,392
But that's only the start of what Python can calculate. In the next section, we'll work through Python's other math operators and learn how to use Python as a calculator.

83
00:08:24,238 --> 00:08:28,272
Ready to learn how to use these operators to make Python do math for you?

84
00:08:28,512 --> 00:08:36,272
By the end of this video, you'll know how to add, subtract, multiply, and divide in Python, along with a few more operations.

85
00:08:36,942 --> 00:08:41,252
We'll start off simple with the plus, minus, star, and slash operators.

86
00:08:42,126 --> 00:08:49,604
In Python, these operators let us add, subtract, multiply, and divide numbers, just like you learned in grade school.

87
00:08:50,414 --> 00:08:58,468
For example, to calculate the sum of 2 and 3, we first write the expression 2 plus sign 3 in a new cell.

88
00:08:59,470 --> 00:09:03,396
Then, to evaluate this expression, we need to execute the cell.

89
00:09:04,366 --> 00:09:08,964
To execute a cell in Google Colab, click the run button on the left of the code cell.

90
00:09:09,966 --> 00:09:11,780
Or we can use a keyboard shortcut.

91
00:09:12,814 --> 00:09:16,160
To run a cell on Windows, press Control plus Enter.

92
00:09:16,718 --> 00:09:19,108
On a Mac, press Command plus Return.

93
00:09:20,622 --> 00:09:24,292
Great, as you probably expected, 2 plus 3 is 5.

94
00:09:25,230 --> 00:09:30,180
Similarly, to calculate 2 minus 3, we use the subtraction operator.

95
00:09:31,054 --> 00:09:34,800
To calculate 2 times 3, we use the star operator.

96
00:09:35,886 --> 00:09:39,876
And to calculate 2 divided by 3, we use the slash operator.

97
00:09:40,878 --> 00:09:46,052
When we run these cells, Python does each calculation and we get the results we expected.

98
00:09:47,022 --> 00:09:53,124
Awesome! Now that we know how to do basic math in Python, we'll explore a few more advanced operators.

99
00:09:54,094 --> 00:09:56,292
Let's start with the double star operator.

100
00:09:57,134 --> 00:10:00,356
In Python, this is the exponentiation operator.

101
00:10:01,358 --> 00:10:07,140
For example, to calculate 10 to the power of 3, we write 10 star star 3.

102
00:10:08,110 --> 00:10:12,228
Running the cell, we get that 10 to the power of 3 is 1000.

103
00:10:13,230 --> 00:10:17,636
Another useful operator is the double slash, or floor division operator.

104
00:10:18,638 --> 00:10:26,080
Unlike regular division, which returns a precise result including decimals, floor division returns only the whole number part.

105
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:30,916
It calculates how many times the right hand value fits evenly into the left hand value.

106
00:10:31,822 --> 00:10:39,108
For example, to calculate the number of times three goes evenly into 13, we write 13 double slash 3.

107
00:10:39,982 --> 00:10:41,924
Running the cell, we get 4.

108
00:10:42,766 --> 00:10:46,660
This is because 3 goes into 13 four times evenly

109
00:10:49,870 --> 00:10:51,300
with a remainder of 1.

110
00:10:52,142 --> 00:10:58,948
If we want to directly calculate this remainder, we can use another operator, the percent sign or modulus operator.

111
00:10:59,982 --> 00:11:06,916
For example, we calculate the remainder of 13 divided by 3 by writing 13% percent sign 3.

112
00:11:07,950 --> 00:11:10,788
Running the cell, we get 1 as expected.

113
00:11:11,790 --> 00:11:16,260
Awesome! Now you know how to use the most important arithmetic operators in Python.

114
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:26,240
Now let's head to the practice notebook linked in the description and try some math ourselves. If you want to try the practice exercises on your own, pause the video here.

115
00:11:26,480 --> 00:11:32,996
Then in the next section, we'll work through the solutions step by step. Alright, let's jump into problem one.

116
00:11:33,838 --> 00:11:45,252
In this problem, we're shopping for a birthday party. We buy three packs of balloons at $4 each, two cakes at $12 each, and a pack of streamers for $6. Also, we have a $5 coupon.

117
00:11:46,094 --> 00:11:52,356
We need to calculate and print the total cost before the coupon, after the coupon, and the cost per guest for eight people.

118
00:11:53,198 --> 00:12:00,996
The key thing to notice here is that we need to print three separate values from a single cell, which means we'll need to use the print function three times.

119
00:12:01,838 --> 00:12:04,964
Let's start by calculating the total before the coupon is applied.

120
00:12:05,806 --> 00:12:09,796
For each item we buy more than one of, we multiply the quantity by the price.

121
00:12:10,606 --> 00:12:15,204
For the balloons, which are three packs at $4 each, we write three times four.

122
00:12:16,046 --> 00:12:20,644
And for the cakes, which are two at twelve doll each, we add two times twelve.

123
00:12:21,454 --> 00:12:24,260
Then we add the flat six dollars for the streamers.

124
00:12:25,070 --> 00:12:28,228
To display the result, we wrap the whole thing in the print function.

125
00:12:29,038 --> 00:12:35,140
For the total after the coupon is applied, we can reuse the same expression and subtract five at the end.

126
00:12:35,982 --> 00:12:40,580
And for the cost per guest, we can use that post-coupon total and divide by eight.

127
00:12:41,390 --> 00:12:49,988
Since we're adding and subtracting before dividing, we wrap this part in parentheses first, which tells Python to evaluate the addition and subtraction before the division.

128
00:12:50,830 --> 00:12:55,524
Running the cell, we get 42, 37, and 4.625.

129
00:12:56,366 --> 00:13:04,320
So the party costs us $42 before the coupon, $37 after, and split eight ways, that's $4.63

130
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:05,316
per person.

131
00:13:06,158 --> 00:13:10,320
Great! Using the print function allows us to output multiple results in the same

132
00:13:10,320 --> 00:13:12,324
cell. Without it,

133
00:13:13,872 --> 00:13:15,588
We'd only see the last result.

134
00:13:16,430 --> 00:13:18,116
Okay, let's move on to problem two.

135
00:13:18,958 --> 00:13:24,036
Here we're calculating the area of a square photo backdrop that's seven feet on each side.

136
00:13:24,878 --> 00:13:27,556
We also need to add a comment above the calculation.

137
00:13:28,366 --> 00:13:35,076
To find the area of a square, we multiply the side by itself, or in other words, we raise the side length to the power of 2.

138
00:13:35,886 --> 00:13:43,588
In Python, we do that with the double star operator. So the area of a 7 foot backdrop is 7 double star 2.

139
00:13:44,430 --> 00:13:47,300
To describe what this line does, we write a comment above it.

140
00:13:49,806 --> 00:13:51,876
Running the cell, we get 49.

141
00:13:52,718 --> 00:13:58,756
The comment sitting above our code is ignored by Python, but it's helpful for anyone reading the notebook later, including ourselves.

142
00:13:59,566 --> 00:14:02,340
Also notice that we didn't use print to display the output.

143
00:14:03,182 --> 00:14:07,268
Since there's only one line in the cell, it's up to you whether or not to use the print function.

144
00:14:08,110 --> 00:14:17,636
Just keep in mind that the auto display behavior is specific to notebooks like this. So if you ever run Python outside of a notebook, you need to use the print statement to see its output.

145
00:14:18,478 --> 00:14:20,484
Great, let's finish up with problem three.

146
00:14:21,326 --> 00:14:25,796
For this problem, we're packing 50 cupcakes into boxes that hold six cupcakes each.

147
00:14:26,606 --> 00:14:31,012
We need to figure out how many full boxes we can fill and how many cupcakes are left over.

148
00:14:31,854 --> 00:14:36,356
We also need to add a text cell with the heading packing cupcakes above our code.

149
00:14:37,198 --> 00:14:38,660
Let's start with the text cell.

150
00:14:39,470 --> 00:14:44,452
We hover over the top of the code cell until the plus text button appears, and then we click it.

151
00:14:45,294 --> 00:14:48,868
Inside it, we type a single hashtag Packing Cupcakes.

152
00:14:49,710 --> 00:14:53,956
The single hashtag here is Markdown, and it makes our text into a heading.

153
00:14:54,798 --> 00:14:59,492
If you want to learn more about markdown syntax, we left a link to some documentation in the description.

154
00:15:00,334 --> 00:15:01,348
Now for the code.

155
00:15:02,190 --> 00:15:11,364
To find how many full boxes we'll have, we can use floor division. That gives us how many times 50 can be evenly divided by 6, ignoring any remainder.

156
00:15:12,206 --> 00:15:19,748
And to find the leftovers, we use the modulus operator, which gives us the remainder after 50 is evenly divided by 6.

157
00:15:20,590 --> 00:15:23,204
Running the cell, we get 8 and 2.

158
00:15:24,046 --> 00:15:28,196
This means that we can fully fill eight boxes with two cupcakes left over.

159
00:15:29,006 --> 00:15:34,052
And that makes sense. 8 times 6 is 48, plus the two leftovers gets us back to 50.

160
00:15:34,894 --> 00:15:37,796
Floor division and modulus always work together like this.

161
00:15:38,750 --> 00:15:43,588
FloridaVision tells you how many times something evenly fits, and modulus tells you what's left over.

162
00:15:44,432 --> 00:15:52,352
At this point, we've used all of Python's math operators. But if you do enough calculations, you might start to notice an interesting mystery.

163
00:15:52,512 --> 00:15:57,472
In the next section, we'll see what that mystery is and get to the bottom of why it happens.

164
00:15:58,254 --> 00:16:05,952
If we add 2 and 3 in Python, we get 5. But if we divide 10 by 2, we get 5.0.

165
00:16:06,752 --> 00:16:18,110
Why does the second result show a decimal while the first one doesn't? To understand this, we need to learn the two ways that Python represents numbers. integers, and floats.

166
00:16:19,310 --> 00:16:23,780
In Python, an integer is any positive or negative whole number.

167
00:16:24,672 --> 00:16:29,956
For example, 13, negative 5, and 0 are all integers.

168
00:16:30,766 --> 00:16:38,820
Integers are the numbers we use for counting discrete items, things that come in whole units, like people or books.

169
00:16:39,662 --> 00:16:45,060
But we can't always count things in whole units. What about prices or someone's height?

170
00:16:45,902 --> 00:16:50,628
For this, Python uses a different type of number called floats.

171
00:16:51,470 --> 00:16:58,116
A float, short for floating point number, is any positive or negative number with a decimal point.

172
00:16:58,958 --> 00:17:06,564
For example, 3.14, negative 2.7, and 0.5 are all floats.

173
00:17:07,406 --> 00:17:10,980
Floats are the numbers we use when we need decimal precision.

174
00:17:11,822 --> 00:17:25,828
Awesome! Now that we understand integers and floats, let's get into some code to answer our original question. Why does 10 divided by 2 give us 5.0, but 2 plus 3 give us 5?

175
00:17:26,670 --> 00:17:31,236
Python works this way because division doesn't always produce whole numbers.

176
00:17:32,046 --> 00:17:36,090
For example, 7 divided by 2 gives us 3 and a half.

177
00:17:37,198 --> 00:17:39,940
This couldn't be represented as an integer.

178
00:17:40,782 --> 00:17:48,196
So, to keep things consistent, Python treats all division results as floats, even if the result doesn't have a decimal.

179
00:17:49,038 --> 00:17:53,060
We can verify this by using a built-in function called type.

180
00:17:53,902 --> 00:18:02,788
When we write type and surround our expression in parentheses, Python will tell us whether the result of our expression is an integer or a float.

181
00:18:03,598 --> 00:18:12,420
Running the 2 plus 3 cell gives us int, short for integer, since adding two whole numbers always gives us another whole number.

182
00:18:13,294 --> 00:18:19,108
Running the 7 divided by 2 cell gives us float, since 3 and a half needs a decimal point.

183
00:18:20,096 --> 00:18:27,492
And running the 10 divided by 2 cell also gives us a float, even though the answer is mathematically a whole number.

184
00:18:28,336 --> 00:18:37,220
Okay, but keeping track of which calculations result in an integer and which ones result in a float might seem like a lot of work.

185
00:18:38,062 --> 00:18:46,416
But don't worry, we can mix floats, like the result of 10 divided by 2, and integers like 4 in our calculations,

186
00:18:46,576 --> 00:18:49,380
and Python handles the conversions automatically.

187
00:18:50,254 --> 00:18:56,260
For most purposes, we can simply think of both as numbers and let Python take care of the details.

188
00:18:57,102 --> 00:19:05,348
That said, if we ever need an integer result from division, say when calculating how many full boxes we can fill,

189
00:19:06,190 --> 00:19:10,084
Python provides a special operator, double slash.

190
00:19:10,926 --> 00:19:18,016
Writing 10 double slash 2 gives us 5, an integer, instead of 5.0.

191
00:19:18,894 --> 00:19:24,420
This is called integer division, and it always rounds down to the nearest whole number.

192
00:19:25,262 --> 00:19:30,510
For example, 7 double slash 2 gives us 3, not 3 and a half.

193
00:19:31,662 --> 00:19:39,140
Awesome! Zooming out, it's important to understand that integers and floats are two examples of data types in Python.

194
00:19:39,982 --> 00:19:48,004
A data type tells Python what kind of information we're working with, and Python has several other data types beyond numbers.

195
00:19:48,878 --> 00:19:52,836
The differences between these other data types really do matter.

196
00:19:53,616 --> 00:20:02,976
We'll get to each of Python's other data types as the course goes on. But first, let's head to the notebook linked in the description to get some practice with integers and floats.

197
00:20:03,216 --> 00:20:09,056
Pause the video here if you want to try it out yourself, and then we'll walk through the solutions step by step.

198
00:20:09,646 --> 00:20:12,132
Alright, let's walk through the solutions together.

199
00:20:12,974 --> 00:20:19,044
In the first problem, we use the type function to check whether different numbers are integers or floats.

200
00:20:19,854 --> 00:20:22,820
We're given four numbers whose types we want to check.

201
00:20:23,662 --> 00:20:28,740
To determine the types, we call the type function, passing in the number as an argument.

202
00:20:29,582 --> 00:20:32,676
Then we'll output the result using the print function.

203
00:20:33,518 --> 00:20:38,416
Starting with 42, we check its type and print the result.

204
00:20:39,278 --> 00:20:43,588
Running the cell, we get class int, which is short for integer.

205
00:20:44,430 --> 00:20:50,660
The word class here is just part of how Python displays type information. We can ignore it for now.

206
00:20:51,502 --> 00:20:57,860
What matters is that 42 is an int, which makes sense since it's a whole number with no decimal point.

207
00:20:58,702 --> 00:21:01,860
Next, let's check the type of 3.14.

208
00:21:02,702 --> 00:21:10,148
Running the cell again, we see that 3.14 is afloat. Makes sense. 3.14 has a decimal point.

209
00:21:10,990 --> 00:21:14,916
To check 0 and 0.0, we'll do the same thing.

210
00:21:17,646 --> 00:21:23,236
Running the cell, we can see that zero is an int, and 0.0 is a float.

211
00:21:24,078 --> 00:21:32,228
Even though 0 and 0.0 represent the same value mathematically, the decimal point makes 0.0 afloat.

212
00:21:33,070 --> 00:21:35,204
Great, let's move on to problem 2.

213
00:21:36,046 --> 00:21:43,684
In this problem, we'll practice using different division operators by figuring out how to distribute 17 books among four shelves.

214
00:21:44,526 --> 00:21:55,044
We need to calculate three things. How many books each shelf gets if we divide them equally, how many whole books each shelf gets, and how many books are left over.

215
00:21:55,886 --> 00:22:03,856
To calculate how many books each shelf gets with regular division, we write 17 forward slash four

216
00:22:04,416 --> 00:22:05,572
and print the result.

217
00:22:06,414 --> 00:22:12,850
Running the cell, we get 4.25, meaning each shelf should get 4. and a quarter books.

218
00:22:13,934 --> 00:22:20,868
But we can't split books into a quarter, so it would be more useful to find out how many whole books each shelf gets.

219
00:22:21,710 --> 00:22:29,696
To do this, on the next line, we use integer division by writing 17, double forward slash 4,

220
00:22:30,336 --> 00:22:31,696
and printing the result.

221
00:22:32,558 --> 00:22:37,092
Running the cell again, we see that each shelf should get four whole books.

222
00:22:37,966 --> 00:22:43,268
Now, if each shelf gets four books out of 17, there should be some left over.

223
00:22:44,110 --> 00:22:50,596
To find out how many, on the next line, we use the modulo operator and print the result.

224
00:22:51,438 --> 00:22:57,348
Running the cell one more time, we see that there's one book left over after putting four books on each shelf.

225
00:22:58,190 --> 00:22:58,816
Nice!

226
00:22:59,758 --> 00:23:08,736
Now we've seen how regular division gives us the exact decimal answer, integer division gives us just the whole number part, and modulo

227
00:23:08,896 --> 00:23:10,148
gives us the remainder.

228
00:23:10,990 --> 00:23:13,252
Okay, let's move on to the final problem.

229
00:23:15,150 --> 00:23:19,844
Here we'll explore what happens when we mix integers and floats in calculations.

230
00:23:20,686 --> 00:23:23,140
We're given four expressions to evaluate.

231
00:23:23,950 --> 00:23:28,388
For each one, we'll print both the result and its type.

232
00:23:29,198 --> 00:23:32,336
Let's start by comparing the two addition operations.

233
00:23:33,326 --> 00:23:38,948
We print the result of the first expression followed by its type.

234
00:23:39,854 --> 00:23:42,896
Running the cell, we get eight followed by int.

235
00:23:43,950 --> 00:23:48,068
This makes sense because both numbers in the expression are integers.

236
00:23:48,910 --> 00:23:54,852
But on the next line, if we add a decimal point to 3.0 and run the cell again,

237
00:23:55,696 --> 00:24:00,420
we get that the second expression produces 8.0, which is a float.

238
00:24:01,296 --> 00:24:08,388
So even though we're still adding 5 and 3, including the decimal point on the 3, change the result to a float.

239
00:24:09,230 --> 00:24:14,596
This pattern holds for other operations too. For example, integer division.

240
00:24:15,438 --> 00:24:21,924
To confirm this, we print the result of the third expression followed by its type.

241
00:24:23,150 --> 00:24:25,860
Running the cell, we get 5 as an integer.

242
00:24:26,798 --> 00:24:34,564
But in contrast, if we write print, the fourth expression and its type, and run the cell again,

243
00:24:35,406 --> 00:24:37,700
we get 5.0 as a float.

244
00:24:38,542 --> 00:24:46,980
So here's the pattern. Whenever we mix an integer and a float in a calculation, Python automatically converts the result to a float.

245
00:24:47,822 --> 00:24:54,500
This means we don't have to worry too much about keeping track of integers versus floats. Python handles it for us.

246
00:24:55,216 --> 00:25:06,336
Awesome! Now we understand which type of calculations give back an integer and which give back a float. But integers and floats aren't the only type of data we want to work with in Python.

247
00:25:06,496 --> 00:25:11,296
In the next section, we'll see the data type that Python uses to represent text.

248
00:25:12,256 --> 00:25:18,976
Text is everywhere in programming, in user input, file names, and even large language models.

249
00:25:19,296 --> 00:25:28,176
In Python, to represent text, we use strings, which are simply sequences of characters like ABC, cat, or hello

250
00:25:28,336 --> 00:25:28,896
world.

251
00:25:30,158 --> 00:25:34,896
To create a string in Python, we write the text we want to represent, for example cat,

252
00:25:35,696 --> 00:25:37,936
inside either single quotation marks,

253
00:25:38,976 --> 00:25:40,576
or double quotation marks.

254
00:25:41,422 --> 00:25:45,536
To display these strings, we can use Python's built in print function.

255
00:25:50,030 --> 00:25:54,276
Notice that in both cases, the quotes don't appear in the output.

256
00:25:55,118 --> 00:26:02,308
That's because the quotation marks aren't part of the string itself. They simply tell Python where the string starts and ends.

257
00:26:03,150 --> 00:26:10,308
So you might be wondering if both approaches produce the same result, why does Python offer two ways to create strings?

258
00:26:11,182 --> 00:26:14,212
Good question. You'll see why later in the video.

259
00:26:15,054 --> 00:26:18,788
Awesome! Now we know how to create a string to store a word.

260
00:26:19,662 --> 00:26:22,116
But strings are extremely flexible.

261
00:26:22,958 --> 00:26:32,516
We can use them to represent any sequence of characters, letters, punctuation, symbols, and even numbers.

262
00:26:33,376 --> 00:26:36,356
Wait, strings can store numbers?

263
00:26:37,198 --> 00:26:42,628
That might seem surprising, since Python already has integers and floats for that.

264
00:26:43,470 --> 00:26:48,068
So why does Python offer multiple ways for us to represent numbers?

265
00:26:48,910 --> 00:26:53,444
To see why, suppose we want to calculate the sum of one and two.

266
00:26:54,286 --> 00:27:02,756
In code, if we write these numbers as integers and add them, Python gives us three, just as we expect.

267
00:27:03,696 --> 00:27:04,016
But

268
00:27:04,816 --> 00:27:12,256
if we represent the same numbers as strings and try to add them, we get 12?

269
00:27:13,102 --> 00:27:16,708
That's some new age math right there. What happened?

270
00:27:17,696 --> 00:27:24,836
Well, when we use the plus operator, Python looks at the data types of the values on each side of the operator.

271
00:27:25,710 --> 00:27:32,100
If both sides are numbers, either an integer or float, the plus sign performs addition.

272
00:27:33,056 --> 00:27:38,500
But if both sides are strings, the plus operator ties the strings together.

273
00:27:39,342 --> 00:27:42,308
This operation is called concatenation.

274
00:27:43,296 --> 00:27:51,536
And if one side is a number while the other is a string, Python doesn't know whether to add or concatenate,

275
00:27:51,696 --> 00:27:53,572
so it throws an error instead.

276
00:27:54,414 --> 00:28:00,132
This example shows how a value's data type can determine what an operator actually does.

277
00:28:00,976 --> 00:28:05,732
This is why the best way to represent a number depends on the situation.

278
00:28:06,606 --> 00:28:12,708
If you're doing math, like calculating totals or averages, integers and floats are the right choice.

279
00:28:13,550 --> 00:28:21,156
But if you're working with numeric identifiers, things like zip codes or bank account numbers, strings might make more sense.

280
00:28:22,030 --> 00:28:25,412
For example, a zip code might start with zeros.

281
00:28:26,256 --> 00:28:28,016
Storing it as an integer

282
00:28:28,816 --> 00:28:33,060
causes an error since Python doesn't allow integers to start with zero.

283
00:28:33,902 --> 00:28:39,972
But storing it as a string works and preserves the zip code exactly as written.

284
00:28:40,782 --> 00:28:49,444
Awesome! Now you know why Python lets us represent numbers as both text and numeric types and when to choose one over the other.

285
00:28:50,286 --> 00:28:55,044
Earlier in the video, remember how we said strings can use single or double quotes?

286
00:28:55,886 --> 00:28:59,460
Let's wrap up by clarifying when it's best to use each one.

287
00:29:00,334 --> 00:29:05,828
As we said before, for simple strings, both options behave the same way.

288
00:29:07,502 --> 00:29:12,228
But when your string's content is more complex, one might work better than the other.

289
00:29:13,070 --> 00:29:21,348
For example, if your text includes an apostrophe, like it's a sunny day, using single quotes causes an error.

290
00:29:22,190 --> 00:29:25,316
Python thinks the apostrophe ends the string early.

291
00:29:26,176 --> 00:29:29,988
In this case, using double quotes fixes the issue.

292
00:29:30,830 --> 00:29:39,236
Similarly, if your text includes double quotes, like to represent what someone says, defining the string with double quotes will break it.

293
00:29:40,112 --> 00:29:42,032
Wrapping it in single quotes

294
00:29:42,992 --> 00:29:44,192
avoids that problem.

295
00:29:45,038 --> 00:29:49,636
So as a rule of thumb, use single quotes when your text contains double quotes.

296
00:29:50,672 --> 00:29:54,884
And use double quotes when your text contains single quotes.

297
00:29:55,726 --> 00:29:57,828
Beyond that, it's your choice.

298
00:29:58,670 --> 00:30:06,308
Awesome! Now you know how to create strings, handle text and numbers, and decide when to use single or double quotes.

299
00:30:06,798 --> 00:30:16,112
Now let's head to the practice notebook linked in the description to get some experience working with strings. If you want to try these exercises on your own, pause the video here.

300
00:30:16,352 --> 00:30:20,192
Then in the next section, we'll walk through the solutions step by step.

301
00:30:20,750 --> 00:30:22,832
Alright, let's jump into problem one.

302
00:30:23,790 --> 00:30:30,500
In this problem, we need to print three strings using print, choosing the right type of quotes for each one.

303
00:30:31,342 --> 00:30:37,476
The challenge here isn't the printing. It's knowing which quote type to use, so each string is valid.

304
00:30:38,318 --> 00:30:46,340
For the first string, hello world, doesn't contain any quotes or apostrophes, so either single or double quotes work fine.

305
00:30:47,182 --> 00:30:48,580
We'll go with double quotes.

306
00:30:49,422 --> 00:30:53,508
The second string, it's a great day, contains an apostrophe.

307
00:30:54,350 --> 00:31:06,596
Since a single quote and an apostrophe are the same character, wrapping this in single quotes would break the string. Python would see the apostrophe in its as the end of the string.

308
00:31:07,438 --> 00:31:18,276
If you accidentally use the wrong quote type like we did here, you'll notice the syntax highlighting looks off. That's a good signal that something is wrong before you even run the code.

309
00:31:19,152 --> 00:31:23,236
So to fix this string, we'll wrap it in double quotes instead.

310
00:31:24,078 --> 00:31:28,356
The third string, she said wow, contains double quotes.

311
00:31:29,166 --> 00:31:37,700
This time, using double quotes to wrap the string would confuse Python. It would see the first inner double quotes as the end of the string.

312
00:31:38,542 --> 00:31:40,900
So instead, we use single quotes.

313
00:31:41,742 --> 00:31:44,740
Running the cell, all three strings print correctly.

314
00:31:45,582 --> 00:31:51,364
So remember, if your text contains an apostrophe or single quotes, use double quotes.

315
00:31:52,272 --> 00:31:56,260
But if your text contains double quotes, use single quotes.

316
00:31:57,102 --> 00:31:58,992
Great, let's go on to problem two.

317
00:31:59,982 --> 00:32:04,708
In this problem, we have four pre-written expressions that use the plus operator.

318
00:32:05,552 --> 00:32:09,956
For each one, we need to predict what it will output before running it.

319
00:32:10,798 --> 00:32:15,812
The trick is that the plus operator behaves differently depending on the types on either side.

320
00:32:16,654 --> 00:32:21,764
In the first expression, three and seven are strings, not numbers.

321
00:32:22,606 --> 00:32:29,156
When both sides are strings, plus joins or concatenates the characters end to end.

322
00:32:29,998 --> 00:32:32,192
This means that we get 37.

323
00:32:33,232 --> 00:32:39,300
But if in the second expression we don't use quotes, 3 and 7 are integers.

324
00:32:40,142 --> 00:32:46,212
When the plus operator is used between two integers, Python performs addition instead.

325
00:32:47,054 --> 00:32:50,372
This means that the second expression produces 10.

326
00:32:51,214 --> 00:32:55,780
The third expression also uses plus to concatenate strings.

327
00:32:56,622 --> 00:32:59,844
But this time it's joining three strings instead of two.

328
00:33:00,686 --> 00:33:07,140
We can use the plus operator to join as many strings as we'd like, so this is a totally valid expression.

329
00:33:07,982 --> 00:33:12,644
Running the cell, we get the concatenated string hello space world.

330
00:33:13,552 --> 00:33:19,908
So far, all three expressions have had matching data types on each side of the plus operators.

331
00:33:20,750 --> 00:33:26,820
When the types match, it's easy for Python to know what to do when it encounters the plus operator.

332
00:33:27,744 --> 00:33:31,024
But when the types don't match,

333
00:33:32,078 --> 00:33:36,836
like in the fourth expression, Python can't decide whether to add or concatenate.

334
00:33:37,678 --> 00:33:41,156
In this case, Python throws a type error.

335
00:33:41,998 --> 00:33:48,356
The main takeaway here is that the plus operator's behavior depends on the types of data on either side.

336
00:33:49,166 --> 00:33:51,748
Awesome, let's finish up with problem three.

337
00:33:52,590 --> 00:33:59,588
In this problem, we're given three broken print statements, and our goal is to fix all three so they run without errors.

338
00:34:00,430 --> 00:34:06,180
Starting with the first print statement, we see it uses single quotes to wrap the strings.

339
00:34:07,022 --> 00:34:15,104
The problem is that the string contains an apostrophe, the same character as a single quote, so Python reads the apostrophe

340
00:34:15,264 --> 00:34:16,452
as the end of the string.

341
00:34:17,262 --> 00:34:23,620
To fix this issue, we switch to double quotes, so Python doesn't get confused by the apostrophe inside.

342
00:34:24,430 --> 00:34:33,092
The second string has the opposite problem. The string is created with double quotes, but the text itself also contains double quotes.

343
00:34:33,934 --> 00:34:39,268
This means that Python sees the first of those inner double quotes as the closing quote.

344
00:34:40,110 --> 00:34:44,068
To fix this error, we swap the outer wrapping to single quotes.

345
00:34:44,910 --> 00:34:49,892
Now Python properly treats the quotes inside the string as regular characters.

346
00:34:50,734 --> 00:34:59,424
The third line tries to concatenate a string and an integer, which means we have the same type mixing issue as we discussed in problem

347
00:34:59,744 --> 00:34:59,984
two.

348
00:35:00,750 --> 00:35:07,428
The fix here is to make both sides of the operator into strings so that Python can concatenate them together.

349
00:35:08,270 --> 00:35:12,836
Running this line, Python joins the two sides together without an error.

350
00:35:13,904 --> 00:35:17,572
Great, those are the solutions to our three practice problems.

351
00:35:18,864 --> 00:35:30,384
Now we know how to create strings and join text together. But every value we've worked with so far, whether it was a string or an integer or a float, has been used once and then forgotten.

352
00:35:30,704 --> 00:35:35,664
In the next section, we'll learn how to store data so that we can use it later in our programs.

353
00:35:36,494 --> 00:35:48,704
Almost every Python program you'll ever write needs to store information. A username, a running total, or the results of a calculation. To do this in Python, you'll need variables.

354
00:35:49,646 --> 00:35:53,668
To see how that works, think of a variable as a labeled storage jar.

355
00:35:54,542 --> 00:36:00,996
That jar's label is like a variable name, and whatever we put inside the jar is the variable's value.

356
00:36:01,838 --> 00:36:09,060
To see how this works in Python, suppose we're writing a program that needs to remember a username so we can use it later.

357
00:36:09,902 --> 00:36:14,180
To create a variable for the username, we start by writing the variable's name.

358
00:36:15,054 --> 00:36:22,244
It's best practice to pick a name that is descriptive of what the variable will store, so we'll go with the name username.

359
00:36:23,086 --> 00:36:26,692
Creating a variable name is like giving a label to our storage jar.

360
00:36:27,598 --> 00:36:31,024
After the variable name, we write an equal sign.

361
00:36:32,304 --> 00:36:35,460
The equal sign is called the assignment operator.

362
00:36:36,398 --> 00:36:41,988
It tells Python to assign the value we'll specify on the right to the variable name on the left.

363
00:36:42,830 --> 00:36:47,344
Finally, after the equal sign, we write the value we want to store.

364
00:36:47,584 --> 00:36:49,744
In this case, the string summer.

365
00:36:50,606 --> 00:36:55,108
The string summer is what gets put inside our storage jar when the code is run.

366
00:36:55,982 --> 00:36:58,660
Awesome! We've now created our first variable.

367
00:36:59,502 --> 00:37:04,324
From this point forward in our program, the variable username holds the string summer.

368
00:37:05,198 --> 00:37:11,716
This means that, in practice, when we write username, Python will substitute in the string summer.

369
00:37:12,558 --> 00:37:19,332
When working in a Jupyter notebook, we can output the variable's value just by writing it on the last line of a cell.

370
00:37:20,238 --> 00:37:29,988
But to output a variable's value in a normal Python program, or anywhere in a Jupyter notebook that's not the final line of a cell, we need to use the print function.

371
00:37:30,830 --> 00:37:39,204
To see how the print function works with variables, in a new cell, we can call the print function and pass in the username variable.

372
00:37:40,110 --> 00:37:42,464
Running the cell, we also get summer.

373
00:37:43,438 --> 00:37:49,700
Notice that the Jupyter output shows summer with quotes, but print gives us summer without them.

374
00:37:50,542 --> 00:37:57,348
That's because print formats the output for human reading, not raw Python, so the quotes get dropped.

375
00:37:58,224 --> 00:38:05,104
Great! In addition to storing a username, we might also want to store the user's password. 1234.

376
00:38:06,030 --> 00:38:12,004
Even though this password is an integer, we can store it in a variable just like we store the username string.

377
00:38:12,846 --> 00:38:18,180
To confirm the assignment was successful, we can print the variable and run the cell.

378
00:38:19,022 --> 00:38:27,344
In addition to strings and numeric data, variables can store any type of data lists, booleans, dictionaries,

379
00:38:27,584 --> 00:38:28,196
and more.

380
00:38:29,038 --> 00:38:34,468
If you want to learn more about these data types, we left a link to our videos on these topics in the description.

381
00:38:35,310 --> 00:38:41,024
Awesome! Now you understand how to create variables to store values and use them later in your program.

382
00:38:41,870 --> 00:38:47,492
But suppose the user later wants to update their password stored in the password variable.

383
00:38:48,334 --> 00:38:57,572
We can change a variable's value at any point in our program. This is called reassignment, and we can do it as many times as we'd like.

384
00:38:58,414 --> 00:39:05,700
To reassign the password variable, we write its name, followed by the assignment operator and the new value.

385
00:39:06,542 --> 00:39:12,464
After this point, the password variable now contains the value 4567.

386
00:39:13,358 --> 00:39:22,532
But before this point, password is still 1234. Great! Now we know how to store and update the values in our variables.

387
00:39:23,374 --> 00:39:32,388
So far we've focused on what variables hold. But variable names matter too, and Python has rules about what counts as a valid name.

388
00:39:33,230 --> 00:39:35,744
Break them, and your code won't run.

389
00:39:36,064 --> 00:39:41,988
To see how these rules work, let's create a new variable that stores a user's three favorite desserts.

390
00:39:43,470 --> 00:39:47,610
You might think a natural variable name would be three fave desserts.

391
00:39:48,704 --> 00:39:51,556
But when we run this, Python throws an error.

392
00:39:52,462 --> 00:39:55,588
This is because variable names can't start with a number.

393
00:39:56,494 --> 00:40:05,348
However, numbers are allowed in all other parts of a variable name, so a variable name like my threefave desserts

394
00:40:05,744 --> 00:40:06,564
will work.

395
00:40:07,438 --> 00:40:09,024
Okay, that's fine.

396
00:40:09,344 --> 00:40:13,284
But my threefave desserts isn't very easy to read.

397
00:40:14,126 --> 00:40:17,504
A natural fix would be to add spaces between the words.

398
00:40:17,744 --> 00:40:20,580
But running the cell, we get another error.

399
00:40:21,422 --> 00:40:24,996
That's because Python doesn't allow spaces in variable names.

400
00:40:25,998 --> 00:40:33,412
Instead, to make variable names more readable, Python programmers often use underscore characters to represent spaces.

401
00:40:34,350 --> 00:40:36,868
When we run the cell now, it works.

402
00:40:37,774 --> 00:40:42,788
One final rule to keep in mind is that variable names are case sensitive.

403
00:40:43,630 --> 00:40:55,012
This means that my three fave desserts and my three favedesserts with a capital M are two completely separate variables as far as Python is concerned.

404
00:40:55,854 --> 00:41:00,004
So when creating and using variables, it's important to be consistent.

405
00:41:00,846 --> 00:41:04,144
Awesome! Now you know how to store any value in a variable.

406
00:41:04,430 --> 00:41:09,668
Use it anywhere in your program, update it when needed, and name it correctly.

407
00:41:10,464 --> 00:41:19,104
Now let's head to the practice notebook linked in the description to get some experience working with variables. If you want to try these exercises on your own, pause the video here.

408
00:41:19,344 --> 00:41:23,664
Then in the next section, we'll walk through the solutions together step by step.

409
00:41:24,462 --> 00:41:26,596
Alright, let's jump into problem one.

410
00:41:27,502 --> 00:41:33,284
In this problem, we create two variables and then print a single sentence that uses both of them.

411
00:41:34,094 --> 00:41:39,972
We'll need a string variable called plant name and an integer variable called watering days.

412
00:41:40,814 --> 00:41:50,244
To store the plant name, we write the variable plant name and the assignment operator and the name of the plant in between quotes.

413
00:41:51,104 --> 00:41:59,584
Similarly, for the number of days between waterings, we write the variable name, watering days, the assignment operator,

414
00:42:00,144 --> 00:42:03,972
and a whole number to represent the number of days between waterings.

415
00:42:04,814 --> 00:42:13,636
To print one line that mentions both, we call print and pass in the sentence text and variables as separate arguments.

416
00:42:18,574 --> 00:42:25,284
When we run the cell, we see our sentence with the plant name and the watering schedule filled in.

417
00:42:26,126 --> 00:42:32,484
Great! For problem two, we'll practice updating a variable's value after it's initially assigned.

418
00:42:33,326 --> 00:42:41,764
In this problem, we'll keep a watering log for the Monstera using a variable called water ounces to track how many ounces are in the watering can.

419
00:42:42,638 --> 00:42:55,748
We'll start with 64 ounces, print the amount, and subtract what we use to water the plant. Print again, and then add back what we refill from the tap, and print one last time.

420
00:42:56,622 --> 00:43:05,744
The important part is that each time the amount changes, we reassign water ounces using the current value of water ounces on the right side,

421
00:43:05,984 --> 00:43:08,356
not a total we calculated off to the side.

422
00:43:09,198 --> 00:43:12,484
We begin by assigning 64 to water ounces.

423
00:43:13,294 --> 00:43:16,548
To log the starting amount, we print water ounces.

424
00:43:17,390 --> 00:43:20,224
Running the cell, we see 64.

425
00:43:21,104 --> 00:43:21,744
Next,

426
00:43:23,054 --> 00:43:26,372
we water the Monstera and use 20 ounces.

427
00:43:27,214 --> 00:43:33,828
To pull that out of what we already have, we reassign water ounces to water ounces minus 20.

428
00:43:34,670 --> 00:43:38,404
We can see the updated total by printing water ounces again.

429
00:43:39,246 --> 00:43:46,340
Running the cell, the second number is 44, which means our water ounces variable was successfully updated.

430
00:43:47,182 --> 00:43:52,804
After that, we refill the can from the tab and add 32 ounces back.

431
00:43:53,678 --> 00:44:00,196
We reassign water ounces again, this time adding 32 to whatever is currently in water ounces.

432
00:44:01,070 --> 00:44:05,508
To see the updated amount of water, we print water ounces one more time.

433
00:44:06,350 --> 00:44:09,904
Running the cell, the third line shows 76.

434
00:44:10,958 --> 00:44:17,796
Awesome! Reassigning variables like this is common whenever data changes and we need to keep track of the latest value.

435
00:44:18,670 --> 00:44:20,420
Let's finish with problem three.

436
00:44:21,294 --> 00:44:27,300
In this problem, we're given code that won't run because the variable names break Python's naming rules.

437
00:44:28,142 --> 00:44:35,844
We'll fix the names by adding underscores to the names, and we'll make sure we don't start a name with a digit or put a space inside it.

438
00:44:36,718 --> 00:44:42,852
The first invalid name, second underscore plant, starts with a number, which Python doesn't allow.

439
00:44:43,726 --> 00:44:51,172
To fix this, we replace the number with the word, second, which keeps the same idea without the leading digit.

440
00:44:52,014 --> 00:45:00,388
The other variable name, plant space type, tries to use a space, which Python reads as two separate words instead of one identifier.

441
00:45:01,230 --> 00:45:05,220
To fix this issue, we simply add an underscore between the words.

442
00:45:06,062 --> 00:45:10,340
Finally, we update the print call so it uses the new variable names.

443
00:45:12,814 --> 00:45:20,708
When we run the cell, we see cactus and succulent printed on one line, separated by a space, and the code runs cleanly.

444
00:45:21,550 --> 00:45:24,804
Great, those are the solutions to all three practice problems.

445
00:45:25,824 --> 00:45:30,384
Now we know how to store values in variables and reuse them later in our program.

446
00:45:30,704 --> 00:45:37,744
But every time we printed a variable inside of a sentence, we had to separate the words and the variable names with commas.

447
00:45:38,224 --> 00:45:44,064
In the next section, we'll learn a better way to directly embed variable names inside of strings.

448
00:45:44,784 --> 00:45:47,104
Why is there an f before this string?

449
00:45:47,264 --> 00:45:49,476
And what's going on with the brackets?

450
00:45:50,318 --> 00:45:54,788
In Python, this syntax creates a formatted string, or f string for short.

451
00:45:55,310 --> 00:46:00,996
By the end of this video, you'll know how to format text with f strings and make your outputs easier to read.

452
00:46:02,190 --> 00:46:08,784
To get familiar with the f string syntax, let's write a simple program to display the temperature assigned to the variable ftemp.

453
00:46:10,254 --> 00:46:16,644
Specifically, our program should generate a string that reads, the temperature is 67 degrees Fahrenheit.

454
00:46:17,710 --> 00:46:24,900
But for flexibility, we don't want to hard code the value 67 in the string in case the f temp variable changes.

455
00:46:25,902 --> 00:46:30,532
Instead, we'd like to embed the value of ftp directly in the string.

456
00:46:31,374 --> 00:46:33,508
This is easy to do with an f string.

457
00:46:34,574 --> 00:46:41,284
We can convert this regular string into an f string by simply writing f before the first quotation mark.

458
00:46:42,126 --> 00:46:48,932
Now that we have an f string, we can insert the value of ftp by writing the variable's name between curly braces.

459
00:46:49,838 --> 00:46:56,484
This syntax tells Python to insert the value of f temp and not the literal string f underscore temp.

460
00:46:57,486 --> 00:47:05,252
Running the cell, we can see that the f temp variable inside the f string was indeed replaced by its value, 60 sep.

461
00:47:06,126 --> 00:47:10,852
This is already useful on its own, but f string allows us to do much more.

462
00:47:11,854 --> 00:47:16,388
In particular, we can display the result of any expression between the braces.

463
00:47:17,264 --> 00:47:23,204
For example, suppose we want our program to report the temperature in Celsius instead of Fahrenheit.

464
00:47:24,110 --> 00:47:33,412
We can do the conversion from Fahrenheit to Celsius directly inside the F string by subtracting 32 from F temp and dividing by 1.8.

465
00:47:34,382 --> 00:47:41,540
When this line is executed, Python first evaluates the conversion expression and then inserts the result in the string.

466
00:47:42,542 --> 00:47:50,500
Running the cell, we get that the temperature is 19.4444443 degrees Celsius.

467
00:47:51,438 --> 00:47:55,172
Okay, that did the job, but that's way too many decimals.

468
00:47:56,206 --> 00:48:02,148
We should probably improve the format of the output by rounding the temperature to one or two decimal places.

469
00:48:03,150 --> 00:48:06,756
Luckily, with F strings, rounding numbers is easy.

470
00:48:07,822 --> 00:48:12,932
Anytime we want to format the expression between brackets, we start by writing a colon.

471
00:48:13,902 --> 00:48:18,308
From here, we have a lot of options to customize how our result is displayed.

472
00:48:19,246 --> 00:48:29,636
To format the decimal places in the expression, we write a period, the number of decimal places we want to display, one, and the letter F to display the result as a float.

473
00:48:30,734 --> 00:48:34,628
Running the cell, we get a Celsius value rounded to one decimal place.

474
00:48:35,598 --> 00:48:40,420
If we wanted to round two decimal places, we'd just replace the one with a two.

475
00:48:42,414 --> 00:48:48,228
This works fine, but you might be wondering, why do we need to write the f after the number of places?

476
00:48:49,166 --> 00:48:55,364
Well, it's because f strings offer multiple ways to display decimal numbers, and floats are just one option.

477
00:48:56,366 --> 00:49:02,544
For example, we could instead display the results in scientific notation by replacing f with e.

478
00:49:04,046 --> 00:49:07,972
Or we could format the value as a percentage using a percent sign.

479
00:49:09,486 --> 00:49:16,068
But since we're working with temperatures, formatting the value as a float makes the most sense, so we'll stick with F.

480
00:49:16,910 --> 00:49:17,504
Great!

481
00:49:17,744 --> 00:49:23,556
As we've seen so far, F strings give us the flexibility to format how decimal numbers are displayed.

482
00:49:24,622 --> 00:49:26,692
But F strings can do more.

483
00:49:27,694 --> 00:49:31,908
In particular, we can also use them to make big numbers easier to read.

484
00:49:32,942 --> 00:49:42,116
To see how this works, let's represent the temperature at the sun's core, which is 27 million degrees Fahrenheit, and update the variable name accordingly.

485
00:49:43,022 --> 00:49:50,116
When we run the cell, it's hard to tell at a glance whether the result is almost 150 million or just 15 million.

486
00:49:50,958 --> 00:49:53,664
We'd have to count the places manually to be

487
00:49:53,664 --> 00:50:01,380
sure. To improve the readability of large numbers like this, f-strings allow us to add commas as thousands separators.

488
00:50:02,382 --> 00:50:05,988
To add the separators, we simply write a comma before the period.

489
00:50:06,958 --> 00:50:10,820
When we run the cell, our Celsius value now has comma separators.

490
00:50:11,822 --> 00:50:12,900
Much clearer.

491
00:50:13,838 --> 00:50:20,804
So far, we've used F string to improve the readability of an expression's result with rounding and comma separators.

492
00:50:21,646 --> 00:50:29,796
But F strings offer even more customization for situations where we want to control the spacing, alignment, and padding around our expression.

493
00:50:30,766 --> 00:50:39,268
For example, let's say we want our program to display both the temperature at the core of the sun and the average temperature in Fahrenheit on Earth's surface.

494
00:50:40,270 --> 00:50:44,260
To convert this value to Celsius and display it, we'll use another F string.

495
00:50:45,230 --> 00:50:50,020
Since we want to display both strings at the same time, we'll pass them into print statements.

496
00:50:51,022 --> 00:50:54,500
When we run the cell, we get both temperatures as expected.

497
00:50:55,502 --> 00:51:00,452
But the layout of these strings is a bit messy, since the results take up different amounts of space.

498
00:51:01,422 --> 00:51:08,644
To fix that, F-strings let us control the space that a result will take up by writing a number in front of the decimal options.

499
00:51:09,486 --> 00:51:16,132
In our example, let's specify 15 to make sure each result takes up exactly 15 characters.

500
00:51:17,134 --> 00:51:22,084
When we run the cell, each result is displayed in a space that is 15 characters wide.

501
00:51:22,926 --> 00:51:28,260
Since neither number is 15 characters long, Python pushes the result all the way to the right.

502
00:51:29,422 --> 00:51:35,300
That works fine, but we can change the alignment of our results by adding an alignment symbol after the colon.

503
00:51:36,302 --> 00:51:40,672
To left align the values, we add the less than symbol right after the colon.

504
00:51:41,678 --> 00:51:47,204
When we run the cell again, we'll see the numbers are now pushed to the left, just as we expected.

505
00:51:48,206 --> 00:51:53,412
Or if we want the values to sit in the center of the 15 characters, we can use the caret.

506
00:51:54,990 --> 00:52:01,124
Now, you might have noticed when the value doesn't take up the full width, something fills in the extra space.

507
00:52:02,126 --> 00:52:05,732
By default, those leftover spots are filled with space characters.

508
00:52:06,798 --> 00:52:08,912
But we're not limited to spaces.

509
00:52:09,166 --> 00:52:11,300
We can choose a different character instead.

510
00:52:12,334 --> 00:52:16,772
We can do this by writing the character we want to use before the alignment symbol.

511
00:52:17,774 --> 00:52:23,460
For example, to make the result stand out more, we can fill the space with asterisks instead.

512
00:52:24,430 --> 00:52:25,152
Awesome!

513
00:52:25,518 --> 00:52:29,380
As you've seen, there are a lot of options for formatting F strings.

514
00:52:30,112 --> 00:52:41,312
Now we can drop values straight into sentences and control exactly how they're displayed. But so far we can't show different sentences depending on the values in our programs, like the temperature.

515
00:52:41,552 --> 00:52:52,432
In the next section, we'll learn how to direct our program down different paths based on different decision criteria. The apps you use every day constantly make decisions.

516
00:52:53,134 --> 00:52:57,668
Is this email spam? Is it late enough to switch to dark mode?

517
00:52:58,574 --> 00:53:01,092
Is the battery low enough to show a warning?

518
00:53:02,030 --> 00:53:05,636
Each of these questions can be answered with a simple yes or no.

519
00:53:06,542 --> 00:53:11,364
Let's see how we can teach our Python programs to make the same kinds of decisions.

520
00:53:12,206 --> 00:53:16,932
Suppose we want to build a program that recommends what to wear based on the temperature.

521
00:53:17,774 --> 00:53:23,044
If the temperature is below 50 degrees Fahrenheit, the recommendation is to wear a jacket.

522
00:53:23,886 --> 00:53:28,388
If the temperature is 50 or higher, the recommendation is to not wear a jacket.

523
00:53:29,230 --> 00:53:33,924
To write the code that makes decisions like this, we need two basic ingredients.

524
00:53:34,766 --> 00:53:39,492
First, we need a way to ask a yes or no question and represent its answer.

525
00:53:40,352 --> 00:53:44,452
Second, we need a way to run different code based on the answer.

526
00:53:45,358 --> 00:53:47,972
Let's start by looking at the first ingredient.

527
00:53:48,910 --> 00:53:55,332
In Python, the answer to a yes or no question is represented using a data type called a Boolean.

528
00:53:56,270 --> 00:54:01,252
A Boolean can only have one of two values, true or false.

529
00:54:02,094 --> 00:54:05,828
For example, let's create a variable called is cold.

530
00:54:06,670 --> 00:54:11,940
To represent a yes answer to the question, is it cold outside? we can set it to true.

531
00:54:12,782 --> 00:54:17,028
To represent a no answer, we would set is cold to false.

532
00:54:17,952 --> 00:54:20,996
Fine, but there's a problem with this approach.

533
00:54:21,966 --> 00:54:28,452
We had to decide for ourselves whether it's cold, and then manually type in true or false.

534
00:54:29,294 --> 00:54:34,660
What if we have the actual temperature and we want Python to determine whether it's cold?

535
00:54:35,520 --> 00:54:42,244
For example, let's say the current temperature is 45, and anything below 50 counts as cold.

536
00:54:43,086 --> 00:54:50,724
To ask Python, is the current temperature less than 50? We write temperature less than sign 50.

537
00:54:51,566 --> 00:54:56,196
When we run the cell, Python evaluates this expression and outputs true.

538
00:54:57,038 --> 00:55:00,580
This means that, yes, the temperature is below 50.

539
00:55:01,422 --> 00:55:07,044
If we instead change the temperature to 55 and evaluate the same condition again,

540
00:55:07,886 --> 00:55:12,900
Python now produces false, meaning the temperature is not below 50.

541
00:55:13,742 --> 00:55:17,680
This less than sign is called a comparison operator.

542
00:55:18,542 --> 00:55:25,572
It compares two values and gives us back a Boolean. True if the relationship holds, false if it doesn't.

543
00:55:26,446 --> 00:55:33,060
Running a comparison and seeing the result is useful, but what if we want to use that answer later in our code?

544
00:55:33,998 --> 00:55:37,924
Well, we can store it in a variable just like any other value.

545
00:55:38,800 --> 00:55:45,828
For example, let's assign the result of temperature less than 50 to a variable called iscold.

546
00:55:46,670 --> 00:55:49,988
When Python runs this line, it works from right to left.

547
00:55:50,894 --> 00:55:54,500
First, it evaluates the comparison and gets false.

548
00:55:55,374 --> 00:55:58,532
Then it stores that Boolean in iscold.

549
00:55:59,374 --> 00:56:07,140
If this right to left order feels unclear, we can add parentheses around the comparison to make the evaluation order more obvious.

550
00:56:08,942 --> 00:56:14,020
The less than operator is just one of several comparison operators Python gives us.

551
00:56:14,830 --> 00:56:22,960
We can check if a value is greater than, less than or equal to, greater than or equal to, equal to,

552
00:56:23,200 --> 00:56:25,348
or not equal to another value.

553
00:56:26,190 --> 00:56:32,164
One thing to watch out for, when checking if two values are equal, we use a double equal sign.

554
00:56:33,006 --> 00:56:37,860
This is different from the single equal sign, which is reserved for variable assignment.

555
00:56:38,702 --> 00:56:47,300
Great, so now we have Booleans and comparison operators in our toolkit, which means we can ask questions and get true or false answers.

556
00:56:48,142 --> 00:56:51,492
But right now, our program just knows the answer.

557
00:56:52,366 --> 00:56:55,556
It doesn't actually do anything based on it.

558
00:56:56,462 --> 00:57:02,692
That's where the second ingredient comes in. We need a way to tell Python, if this is true, then do this.

559
00:57:03,534 --> 00:57:06,084
Let's put our isCold variable to work.

560
00:57:06,926 --> 00:57:12,484
Say we want our program to print wear a jacket, but only if isCold is true.

561
00:57:13,326 --> 00:57:16,196
In Python, we do this with an if statement.

562
00:57:17,038 --> 00:57:20,240
The header of an if statement starts with the keyword if.

563
00:57:21,262 --> 00:57:26,560
This keyword is followed by a condition. Here, is cold and ends with a colon.

564
00:57:27,502 --> 00:57:35,360
On the next line, indented, we write what should happen when the condition is true. In this case, printing wear a jacket.

565
00:57:36,238 --> 00:57:43,844
That indentation isn't just for readability. It signals to Python that this line is part of the if block.

566
00:57:44,686 --> 00:57:52,676
To see the difference, let's add an unindented line outside of the if block to mark when the program finishes.

567
00:57:53,518 --> 00:57:57,280
This line will run regardless of the value of iscold.

568
00:57:58,158 --> 00:58:01,572
Great! Now let's see this if statement in action.

569
00:58:02,414 --> 00:58:09,360
With the temperature set to 48, we run the code and get wear a jacket followed by end of program.

570
00:58:10,414 --> 00:58:18,020
Here's why. 48 is less than 50, so the comparison returns true, which is then assigned to isCold.

571
00:58:18,862 --> 00:58:24,292
When Python reaches the if statement, it first checks whether isCold is true or false.

572
00:58:25,134 --> 00:58:31,460
In this case, isCold is true. So Python runs the indented code and outputs where a jacket.

573
00:58:32,334 --> 00:58:38,276
After the if block, Python moves on to the unindented code and outputs end of program.

574
00:58:39,118 --> 00:58:44,068
Great, but suppose it warms up and the temperature is now 55 degrees.

575
00:58:44,928 --> 00:58:49,088
Now when we run the code, we only get end of program.

576
00:58:49,968 --> 00:58:55,844
This is because 55 is not below 50, so isCold was set to false.

577
00:58:56,688 --> 00:59:02,436
Then, like before, Python evaluated the condition, but this time it was false.

578
00:59:03,278 --> 00:59:08,388
So Python skipped the indented block entirely and moved on to the unindented code.

579
00:59:09,262 --> 00:59:15,460
Great! An if statement gives us one decision path. If the condition is true, run this code.

580
00:59:16,302 --> 00:59:22,596
But in many cases, we also want our program to do something only when the condition is false.

581
00:59:23,534 --> 00:59:28,388
In our example, we want to suggest no jacket if the condition is false.

582
00:59:29,262 --> 00:59:32,608
To do this, we'll need another keyword, else.

583
00:59:33,614 --> 00:59:41,252
To add an else block, we'll write the keyword else at the same indentation level as the if followed by a colon.

584
00:59:42,094 --> 00:59:48,164
Unlike the if statement, the else statement doesn't require a condition. It is the fallback option.

585
00:59:49,006 --> 00:59:53,508
On the next line, we indent the code we want to run when the condition is false.

586
00:59:54,446 --> 00:59:57,380
In this case, we'll print no jacket needed.

587
00:59:58,318 --> 01:00:01,028
Awesome, let's see this else block in action.

588
01:00:01,934 --> 01:00:07,940
Since the temperature is currently set to 55, when we run the code, is cold will be false.

589
01:00:08,750 --> 01:00:13,028
This means Python will skip the if block and run the else block instead.

590
01:00:13,902 --> 01:00:20,740
Running the cell, we see no jacket needed, followed by end of program, exactly as expected.

591
01:00:21,614 --> 01:00:26,928
And if the temperature falls back down to 45 degrees, isCold will be

592
01:00:26,928 --> 01:00:33,568
true. This means Python will run the if block just like we saw earlier and print wear a jacket.

593
01:00:34,542 --> 01:00:39,460
The else block will get skipped this time, because the if condition is satisfied.

594
01:00:40,302 --> 01:00:45,648
Then Python will continue on to the unindented code and print end of program.

595
01:00:46,510 --> 01:00:49,540
Running the cell, we get the expected output.

596
01:00:50,414 --> 01:00:53,700
Awesome! Now you know how to make decisions in Python.

597
01:00:54,542 --> 01:00:57,924
We started with Booleans to represent yes or no answers.

598
01:00:58,766 --> 01:01:03,972
Then we used comparison operators to ask questions and get those answers automatically.

599
01:01:04,878 --> 01:01:11,300
Finally, we brought it all together with if and else statements to run different code based on those answers.

600
01:01:12,046 --> 01:01:21,568
Now let's go to the notebook linked in the description to get some practice writing programs that make decisions. Pause the video here if you want to work through the exercises yourself.

601
01:01:22,048 --> 01:01:25,700
Let's walk through the solutions together, starting with problem one.

602
01:01:26,542 --> 01:01:32,420
In this problem, we're given a user's age and we'll need to check if they qualify for a movie discount.

603
01:01:33,294 --> 01:01:37,508
If the age is 12 or under, we'll print you get a child discount.

604
01:01:38,382 --> 01:01:42,020
Otherwise, we'll print regular ticket price applies.

605
01:01:42,862 --> 01:01:48,580
We start by checking if the age is 12 or under using the less than or equal to operator.

606
01:01:49,422 --> 01:01:56,580
This comparison will result in a Boolean, true, if the age is 12 or less, false otherwise.

607
01:01:57,422 --> 01:02:02,276
We'll need this result later, so we store it in a variable called isChild.

608
01:02:03,168 --> 01:02:08,260
Then we use this variable in an if statement to choose which message to print.

609
01:02:09,070 --> 01:02:14,340
Since isChild is already a Boolean, we can simply write the variable name.

610
01:02:15,182 --> 01:02:19,236
When the condition is true, we print the child discount message.

611
01:02:20,846 --> 01:02:24,804
When the condition is false, we run the code in the else block.

612
01:02:25,646 --> 01:02:29,028
In this else block, we print the regular price message.

613
01:02:29,870 --> 01:02:33,732
Running the code, we get the child discount message.

614
01:02:34,542 --> 01:02:41,092
This makes sense because 10 is less than or equal to 12. So isChild is true.

615
01:02:41,934 --> 01:02:49,188
If we change the age to 15 and run it again, we get regular ticket price applies instead.

616
01:02:50,030 --> 01:02:52,804
In this case, isChild was false.

617
01:02:53,614 --> 01:02:55,524
Great! On to problem 2.

618
01:02:56,366 --> 01:03:01,348
In this problem, we're given a password, and we need to check if it's strong enough.

619
01:03:02,158 --> 01:03:06,148
A password is considered strong if it has eight or more characters.

620
01:03:06,958 --> 01:03:10,308
If it's strong, we'll print password is strong.

621
01:03:11,118 --> 01:03:14,212
Otherwise, we'll print password is too weak.

622
01:03:15,054 --> 01:03:18,884
To check the length of the password, we use Python's len function.

623
01:03:19,726 --> 01:03:23,172
The len function returns the number of characters in a string.

624
01:03:23,982 --> 01:03:30,756
We can check the password's length directly in the if statement, comparing it to our threshold of eight characters.

625
01:03:31,598 --> 01:03:34,948
If it's eight or more, we print password is strong.

626
01:03:36,718 --> 01:03:39,684
Otherwise, we print password is too weak.

627
01:03:41,230 --> 01:03:49,668
Running the code, we got password is too weak, which is correct because ABC123 has only six characters.

628
01:03:50,510 --> 01:03:58,736
If we change the password to something longer, like secure pass 2024 and run it again, we get password is

629
01:03:58,896 --> 01:04:01,252
strong since it has 14 characters.

630
01:04:02,094 --> 01:04:04,496
Awesome, let's move on to the final problem.

631
01:04:05,390 --> 01:04:12,036
In this problem, we're given a current temperature and a desired temperature, and we need to build a simple thermostat program.

632
01:04:12,910 --> 01:04:19,940
If the current temperature is below the desired temperature, we'll print turning on heater and then print the current temperature.

633
01:04:20,816 --> 01:04:26,372
If not, we'll print temperature is comfortable and then print the current temperature.

634
01:04:27,182 --> 01:04:31,844
After the if-else block, we'll always print thermostat check complete.

635
01:04:32,686 --> 01:04:39,716
To check if it's too cold, we compare the current temperature to the desired temperature using the less than operator.

636
01:04:40,558 --> 01:04:43,716
We'll assign the result of this comparison to a variable.

637
01:04:44,558 --> 01:04:49,508
Then we'll use that variable in an if statement to decide what action to take.

638
01:04:50,350 --> 01:04:53,296
If it's too cold, we print turning on heater,

639
01:04:54,222 --> 01:04:57,092
then print the current temperature using an F string.

640
01:04:59,854 --> 01:05:04,900
If you want to learn more about how F strings work, check out our video linked in the description.

641
01:05:05,742 --> 01:05:09,376
For the other case, we print temperature is comfortable,

642
01:05:10,416 --> 01:05:13,188
and then the current temperature in the else block.

643
01:05:15,022 --> 01:05:24,736
Finally, we need to output the message thermostat check complete, no matter the value of the iscold variable, so we add a print statement outside the if-else

644
01:05:25,216 --> 01:05:25,444
block.

645
01:05:26,286 --> 01:05:37,412
Running the code, we got turning on heater followed by the temperature and thermostat check complete, which makes sense because 65 is less than 72.

646
01:05:38,254 --> 01:05:46,212
If we change the current temperature to 75 and run it again, we get temperature is comfortable instead.

647
01:05:47,136 --> 01:05:58,096
Awesome. Now we can ask a yes or no question and act directly on the answer. But a yes or no question only has two outcomes, and so we can only direct our program down two paths.

648
01:05:58,256 --> 01:06:07,536
In the next section, we'll see how to direct our program down more decision paths. We learned how to use if and else to make decisions in Python.

649
01:06:07,856 --> 01:06:15,856
This structure allows our program to choose between two paths, one if the condition is true, and one if it's false.

650
01:06:16,942 --> 01:06:20,452
But the if-else structure we've built has some limitations.

651
01:06:21,296 --> 01:06:27,780
It only gives us two possible paths and it only allows us to test one condition.

652
01:06:28,622 --> 01:06:34,340
Let's see how to move past those limits, starting with how to handle more than two possibilities.

653
01:06:35,214 --> 01:06:40,228
Let's go back to our example program that recommends what to wear based on the temperature.

654
01:06:41,102 --> 01:06:45,668
At the moment, the program first checks if the temperature is below 50.

655
01:06:46,510 --> 01:06:49,860
If the answer is yes, it prints wear jacket.

656
01:06:50,704 --> 01:06:55,044
Otherwise, the program prints no jacket needed.

657
01:06:55,886 --> 01:07:02,724
This works, but it only handles two scenarios, cold or not cold.

658
01:07:03,534 --> 01:07:05,412
What if we want a third option?

659
01:07:06,254 --> 01:07:16,676
For example, suppose temperatures between 50 and 65 count as mild, and we want our program to suggest bring a light layer in this situation.

660
01:07:17,518 --> 01:07:21,220
With the current structure, there's no place for this middle option.

661
01:07:22,062 --> 01:07:29,508
Thankfully, Python provides another keyword, elif, when we want to consider multiple alternatives.

662
01:07:30,318 --> 01:07:35,428
This keyword is short for else if, which gives us a clue about how it works.

663
01:07:36,270 --> 01:07:44,228
Like the else statement, an elif statement is executed only if the condition in the if statement is false.

664
01:07:45,184 --> 01:07:53,764
And, like an if statement, an elif statement is executed only if the condition that follows it is true.

665
01:07:54,606 --> 01:08:02,704
This means that if our program evaluates the elif condition during execution, it must be the case that is cold

666
01:08:02,864 --> 01:08:03,524
is false.

667
01:08:04,430 --> 01:08:08,900
In other words, it must be that the temperature is 50 or above.

668
01:08:09,824 --> 01:08:16,580
So, to determine if the weather is mild, we only need to check if the temperature is below 65 degrees.

669
01:08:17,518 --> 01:08:21,988
Then, just like with if and else, we end the line with a colon.

670
01:08:22,830 --> 01:08:30,884
Indented underneath the elif statement, we write the print statement that will run when the condition is true. Bring a light layer.

671
01:08:31,726 --> 01:08:36,324
Great! To see how this new structure works, let's do a few code traces.

672
01:08:37,166 --> 01:08:40,784
For our first code trace, we'll set the temperature to 55.

673
01:08:41,678 --> 01:08:46,596
When Python arrives at the if statement, it checks the value of is cold.

674
01:08:47,438 --> 01:08:51,780
Since 55 is greater than 50, is cold is false.

675
01:08:52,784 --> 01:08:59,108
So, Python skips the code indented under the if block and moves to the elif block.

676
01:08:59,984 --> 01:09:05,284
Here, Python tests whether 55 is less than 65, which is true.

677
01:09:06,126 --> 01:09:12,772
Because the statement is true, Python then executes the code in the elif block and prints the message.

678
01:09:13,614 --> 01:09:22,144
Finally, since the elif condition was satisfied, Python skips the else block entirely and executes the end of the program

679
01:09:22,384 --> 01:09:22,884
message.

680
01:09:23,726 --> 01:09:27,300
Running the code, this is indeed the output we get.

681
01:09:28,174 --> 01:09:32,996
Great! Now suppose it cools down and the temperature drops to 40.

682
01:09:33,838 --> 01:09:39,012
When Python executes the if statement, it checks whether isCold is true.

683
01:09:39,886 --> 01:09:45,024
Since it is, Python will run the if block and print wear a jacket.

684
01:09:45,902 --> 01:09:52,772
Because the if condition was true, Python skips both the elif and else block that follows.

685
01:09:53,614 --> 01:09:57,984
This means that, even though 40 is also less than 65,

686
01:09:58,784 --> 01:10:03,588
the elif block is not run. The condition is not even tested.

687
01:10:04,494 --> 01:10:10,340
Running the code with the temperature at 40, we get wear a jacket as expected.

688
01:10:11,182 --> 01:10:16,100
For the final scenario, suppose the temperature warms up to 75 degrees.

689
01:10:16,942 --> 01:10:23,652
In this case, Python first checks the if condition and sees that is cold is false.

690
01:10:24,494 --> 01:10:29,764
As a result, it moves to the elif statement and tests the associated condition.

691
01:10:30,606 --> 01:10:36,304
Since 75 is not less than 65, this condition is also false.

692
01:10:37,166 --> 01:10:44,388
Since none of the conditions are true, Python executes the else block and prints no jacket needed.

693
01:10:45,230 --> 01:10:48,580
Running the code, that's exactly what we get.

694
01:10:49,422 --> 01:10:56,996
Great! Let's quickly summarize the three general rules that Python follows when executing an if elif else block.

695
01:10:57,902 --> 01:11:01,988
First, Python evaluates the conditions from top to bottom.

696
01:11:02,880 --> 01:11:10,724
Second, Python executes the first statement whose condition is true, or the else statement if no conditions are true.

697
01:11:11,566 --> 01:11:17,412
And third, Python skips all the statements after the first statement with a true condition.

698
01:11:18,254 --> 01:11:26,212
Okay, our if elif else structure now handles three possibilities. But we can take this even further.

699
01:11:27,086 --> 01:11:36,484
While we're limited to one if and one else per conditional block, we can chain together as many elif statements as we want.

700
01:11:37,360 --> 01:11:47,236
For example, we might want more possibilities for cooler temperatures to handle a chilly 40 degrees differently than a freezing 20 degrees.

701
01:11:48,078 --> 01:11:55,524
And for warmer temperatures, we might want different advice for a mild 70 degrees than a scorching 95 degrees.

702
01:11:56,366 --> 01:12:00,932
To handle these additional cases, we can add more L if blocks.

703
01:12:07,918 --> 01:12:10,916
Now we have four conditions plus a fallback.

704
01:12:11,758 --> 01:12:20,196
Even though we've added more conditions, Python still evaluates them from top to bottom and stops at the first true condition.

705
01:12:21,038 --> 01:12:25,252
Awesome! Now our program can handle as many scenarios as we need.

706
01:12:26,126 --> 01:12:29,572
But each condition tests only one thing, temperature.

707
01:12:30,414 --> 01:12:38,372
In practice, we might want our program to consider other criteria besides just the temperature when recommending what to wear.

708
01:12:39,214 --> 01:12:43,200
For example, whether or not it's raining could be important.

709
01:12:44,078 --> 01:12:49,604
To explore how to use multiple decision criteria at once, let's start with a new cell.

710
01:12:50,446 --> 01:12:56,388
Since we want to work with temperature and rain, let's create two variables to represent these states.

711
01:12:57,230 --> 01:13:02,692
For the time being, we'll set temperature to 45 and is raining to true.

712
01:13:03,630 --> 01:13:11,204
Then, let's say we want to recommend wearing something warm and waterproof if it's cold and rainy.

713
01:13:12,046 --> 01:13:18,756
In Python, to test whether two conditions are true at the same time, we join them with the AND keyword.

714
01:13:19,598 --> 01:13:29,040
In code, when we write temperature less than 50 and is raining, we're combining these two separate conditions

715
01:13:29,360 --> 01:13:30,884
into a single expression.

716
01:13:31,726 --> 01:13:35,332
This kind of expression is called a logical expression.

717
01:13:36,174 --> 01:13:43,520
A logical expression connects two individual conditions using a logical operator, in this case, and.

718
01:13:44,782 --> 01:13:53,828
When the and operator is used to connect the conditions, the logical expression evaluates to true only when both conditions are true.

719
01:13:54,670 --> 01:14:02,244
In this example, the temperature is 45, which is less than 50. So the left condition is true.

720
01:14:03,200 --> 01:14:06,000
And is raining is true.

721
01:14:06,800 --> 01:14:09,284
So the right condition is also true.

722
01:14:10,126 --> 01:14:15,460
So when Python evaluates the logical expression as a whole, we get true.

723
01:14:16,302 --> 01:14:20,484
But suppose it stops raining, so isRaining switches to false.

724
01:14:21,390 --> 01:14:26,116
This means the right condition in the logical expression becomes false.

725
01:14:26,958 --> 01:14:31,140
So when we run the cell, the logical expression evaluates to false.

726
01:14:31,982 --> 01:14:41,380
Since and logical expressions are true only when both conditions are true, every other combination of conditions evaluates to false.

727
01:14:42,190 --> 01:14:50,532
Great! Now that we know how and works, let's use this logical expression in an if statement to determine what to wear.

728
01:14:51,374 --> 01:14:57,120
If it's both cold and raining, let's have our program print wear a warm rain jacket.

729
01:14:58,222 --> 01:15:03,236
Outside the conditional block, let's signal the end of the program with a print statement.

730
01:15:04,078 --> 01:15:08,644
Beautiful! Since the temperature is below 50 and it's raining,

731
01:15:09,550 --> 01:15:16,516
when we run the code, Python prints the rain jacket recommendation and the end of the program statement.

732
01:15:17,408 --> 01:15:20,368
But if either condition is false,

733
01:15:21,168 --> 01:15:28,388
the AND logical expression will evaluate to false, and we'll only get the end of the program statement.

734
01:15:29,230 --> 01:15:37,572
Note that, just as we did before, we could add an else statement or even an elif statement to handle more cases.

735
01:15:38,414 --> 01:15:46,276
Great! The AND operator is useful when we want to require both conditions to be true for the logical expression to be true.

736
01:15:47,182 --> 01:15:54,564
But we don't always want to be that strict. Sometimes we want to run code if at least one of the conditions is true.

737
01:15:55,408 --> 01:16:03,684
For example, suppose we want to flag whenever the temperature is extreme, either below 32 or above 95.

738
01:16:04,526 --> 01:16:10,404
In Python, we can test if at least one condition is true by using the OR keyword.

739
01:16:11,246 --> 01:16:16,048
To test out the OR keyword in code, let's add a new cell.

740
01:16:19,278 --> 01:16:24,004
In this cell, we create a temperature variable and set it to 28.

741
01:16:24,846 --> 01:16:34,404
We can then test this variable to determine if it's extreme by writing temperature less than 32 or temperature greater than 95.

742
01:16:35,310 --> 01:16:41,828
An OR logical expression like this evaluates to true when at least one side is true.

743
01:16:42,670 --> 01:16:46,596
With the temperature at 28, the left side is true.

744
01:16:47,534 --> 01:16:51,812
That alone is enough to make the logical expression as a whole true.

745
01:16:52,654 --> 01:16:57,956
Only when both sides are false does an OR logical expression evaluate to false.

746
01:16:58,798 --> 01:17:03,940
For example, if we set the temperature to 70, both sides will be false.

747
01:17:04,782 --> 01:17:09,476
This means that the OR logical expression also evaluates to false.

748
01:17:10,350 --> 01:17:19,684
Great! Like the AND logical expression earlier, we can use this OR logical expression as the condition in an if statement.

749
01:17:20,526 --> 01:17:23,652
If the temperature is extreme, we'll print a warning.

750
01:17:26,606 --> 01:17:31,012
Outside the conditional, we'll signal the end of the program with a print statement.

751
01:17:31,854 --> 01:17:37,540
Running the code with a non-extreme temperature, we get no warnings as expected.

752
01:17:38,382 --> 01:17:42,532
But when the temperature is extreme, we get our weather warning.

753
01:17:43,374 --> 01:17:50,052
Awesome! With the OR and AND logical operators, we can check multiple conditions at the same time.

754
01:17:50,958 --> 01:18:00,036
These operators let us handle lots of scenarios, but there's still something we currently cannot do. Test whether something is false.

755
01:18:01,408 --> 01:18:07,972
For example, we might want to know whether it's not sunny, not cold, or not raining.

756
01:18:08,846 --> 01:18:13,892
To check whether something is not true in Python, we use the NOT operator.

757
01:18:14,702 --> 01:18:18,448
The NOT operator works differently from AND and OR.

758
01:18:19,438 --> 01:18:24,516
Instead of combining two conditions, it simply flips the value of a Boolean.

759
01:18:25,358 --> 01:18:28,100
Let's go back to our code to see how this works.

760
01:18:29,006 --> 01:18:33,444
Suppose we want to know whether an israining variable is false.

761
01:18:34,286 --> 01:18:38,916
To check whether the variable is false, we write not israining.

762
01:18:39,758 --> 01:18:46,884
In this case, since isRaining is false, the NOT operator flips the value to true.

763
01:18:47,808 --> 01:18:53,572
But if israining was true, not is raining would be false.

764
01:18:54,382 --> 01:18:59,684
We can also combine NOT with the AND and OR operators we learned earlier.

765
01:19:00,526 --> 01:19:07,012
For example, suppose we want to know if it's warm and not raining. A good day to go outside.

766
01:19:07,854 --> 01:19:16,048
To do this, let's add a temperature variable set to 75, then we can use this variable to check if it's warm,

767
01:19:16,288 --> 01:19:19,108
above 70, and not raining.

768
01:19:19,982 --> 01:19:27,140
In this logical expression, Python evaluates the condition, temperature greater than 70, and gets true.

769
01:19:28,110 --> 01:19:31,172
Then it evaluates not is raining.

770
01:19:32,014 --> 01:19:36,452
Since is raining is true, not is raining is false.

771
01:19:37,294 --> 01:19:41,732
Finally, Python evaluates true and false, which is false.

772
01:19:42,574 --> 01:19:53,216
And just like with AND and OR on their own, we can use this combined expression as the condition in an if statement to execute specific code based on its result.

773
01:19:54,158 --> 01:19:58,916
Alright, we've done a lot in this video, so let's wrap up with a quick summary.

774
01:19:59,758 --> 01:20:04,388
We started with elif to give our programs more than two possible paths.

775
01:20:05,262 --> 01:20:11,108
By chaining multiple elif statements together, we can handle as many scenarios as we need.

776
01:20:11,936 --> 01:20:17,700
Then we used AND and OR to combine multiple conditions into a single expression.

777
01:20:18,576 --> 01:20:26,656
Finally, we use NOT to flip a condition's value, which we can mix with AND and OR for even more control.

778
01:20:27,726 --> 01:20:31,716
With these tools, our programs can make much more flexible decisions.

779
01:20:32,736 --> 01:20:42,976
Now let's head to the practice notebook linked in the description to practice using ELIF and logical expressions ourselves. If you want to work through the exercises on your own, pause the video here.

780
01:20:44,206 --> 01:20:48,260
Let's walk through all three problems together, starting with problem one.

781
01:20:49,102 --> 01:20:53,828
In this problem, we're given a test score and we need to print the correct letter grade.

782
01:20:54,670 --> 01:21:02,724
The grading scale has five categories A for 90 and above, B for 80 through 89, and so on.

783
01:21:03,566 --> 01:21:08,740
Since there are five possible outcomes, this is a perfect case for an elif chain.

784
01:21:09,550 --> 01:21:16,256
We start with an if statement that checks whether the score is 90 or above. If it is, we print A.

785
01:21:17,296 --> 01:21:21,988
Next, we add an elif that checks whether the score is 80 or above.

786
01:21:22,830 --> 01:21:31,136
At this point in the chain, we already know the score is below 90, since the only way Python would execute this condition is if the if

787
01:21:31,376 --> 01:21:32,804
condition is false.

788
01:21:33,616 --> 01:21:39,716
So if this condition is true, the score must be in the 80s. And we print B.

789
01:21:40,558 --> 01:21:51,856
We follow the same logic for the next two elif blocks. One checking for 70 or above, a C, and one for 60 or above, a D.

790
01:21:52,750 --> 01:21:58,336
Finally, the else block handles every score below 60, where we print F.

791
01:21:59,214 --> 01:22:08,176
Running the code with a score of 85, we get B, which makes sense since 85 falls in the 80 through 89

792
01:22:08,416 --> 01:22:08,708
range.

793
01:22:09,616 --> 01:22:15,456
And if we change the score to 72 and run it, we get C.

794
01:22:16,398 --> 01:22:23,876
Python evaluated the conditions from top to bottom, stopped at the first one that was true, and skipped the rest.

795
01:22:24,686 --> 01:22:28,292
This means that the order of these conditions really matters.

796
01:22:28,814 --> 01:22:39,056
If we had put the greater than or equal to 60 check before the greater than or equal to 70 check, a score of 72 would match it and print D,

797
01:22:39,776 --> 01:22:41,764
even though it should be a C.

798
01:22:42,766 --> 01:22:48,196
The chain only works correctly when we start at the highest threshold and work our way down.

799
01:22:49,038 --> 01:22:57,616
We should also note that you could technically write the same logic using nested if statements an if inside another if

800
01:22:58,016 --> 01:22:59,456
inside another if.

801
01:23:04,846 --> 01:23:15,364
But each layer adds another level of indentation, and by the time you have four or five conditions, the code is pushed so far to the right that it becomes genuinely hard to read.

802
01:23:16,206 --> 01:23:18,176
Using elliph statements,

803
01:23:21,456 --> 01:23:31,396
keeps everything flat, clear, and at the same level, which is almost always the right call when you're choosing between multiple mutually exclusive options.

804
01:23:32,238 --> 01:23:34,336
Great, let's move on to problem two.

805
01:23:35,182 --> 01:23:44,740
In this problem, we're checking whether a roller coaster rider meets both of the ride's requirements, a minimum height of 48 inches and a minimum age of 12.

806
01:23:45,582 --> 01:23:52,132
Since both conditions need to be true at the same time, this is a great use case for the AND operator.

807
01:23:52,974 --> 01:24:01,412
In the if statement, we check if the height in inches is at least 48 and whether the rider's age is at least 12.

808
01:24:02,254 --> 01:24:11,300
This logical expression is only true when both sides are true. The rider is tall enough and old enough.

809
01:24:12,142 --> 01:24:15,780
If the condition is true, we print you can ride.

810
01:24:16,622 --> 01:24:22,564
Otherwise, the else block prints sorry, you don't meet the height or age requirement.

811
01:24:23,406 --> 01:24:32,036
Running the code with a height of 52 inches and an age of 14, we get you can ride. Both conditions are met.

812
01:24:32,878 --> 01:24:39,396
