971
01:41:05,984 --> 01:41:08,784
But not every group of values belongs in a list.

972
01:41:09,024 --> 01:41:13,572
In the next section, we'll see two other ways Python can store a collection of items.

973
01:41:14,670 --> 01:41:19,140
How do you know when to use a list versus a tuple or a set?

974
01:41:20,014 --> 01:41:26,148
In this video, you'll learn the differences between these data types and understand when to use each one.

975
01:41:26,894 --> 01:41:34,864
Let's visualize the syntax differences between lists, tuples, and sets using a collection of four letters, A,

976
01:41:35,104 --> 01:41:36,932
B, C, and A again.

977
01:41:37,774 --> 01:41:42,052
To create a list from these letters, we wrap them in square brackets.

978
01:41:43,534 --> 01:41:47,204
To create a tuple from these letters, we wrap them in parentheses.

979
01:41:48,654 --> 01:41:54,340
And lastly, to create a set from these letters, we'll wrap the items in curly braces.

980
01:41:55,886 --> 01:41:56,464
Awesome!

981
01:41:56,814 --> 01:42:01,924
But notice that the set we just created contains the letter A only once.

982
01:42:02,766 --> 01:42:07,556
That's the first big difference between sets and the other two types of collections.

983
01:42:08,494 --> 01:42:13,060
Sets automatically drop duplicates, while lists and tuples keep them.

984
01:42:13,998 --> 01:42:19,684
This makes sets especially useful when we want to eliminate duplicate values from a collection.

985
01:42:20,590 --> 01:42:26,852
To see why, let's suppose we have a list of numbers and want to get only the unique values.

986
01:42:27,822 --> 01:42:31,812
We can do this by simply converting the list into a set,

987
01:42:32,878 --> 01:42:35,940
which automatically filters out the repeated values.

988
01:42:36,910 --> 01:42:42,884
Then, if we want to work with a list again, we can simply cast the set back into a list.

989
01:42:44,590 --> 01:42:50,084
Another key difference is that sets are unordered, while lists and tuples are ordered.

990
01:42:50,926 --> 01:42:56,068
In an ordered collection, each item has a position, also called an index.

991
01:42:57,006 --> 01:43:03,664
Assigning the list and tuple to a variable, we can use indexes to access any of the items.

992
01:43:10,528 --> 01:43:17,604
Sets, on the other hand, are unordered, which means its items are not assigned a position or index.

993
01:43:18,542 --> 01:43:24,164
If we assign our set to a variable and try to index it, we get an error.

994
01:43:25,678 --> 01:43:33,732
Awesome! Now that we've compared sets to lists and tuples, let's look at what makes lists and tuples different from each other.

995
01:43:34,606 --> 01:43:43,044
The key difference between lists and tuples is whether the collection can be modified after it's created, a trait called mutability.

996
01:43:44,078 --> 01:43:47,812
Lists are mutable, meaning you can modify them after creating them.

997
01:43:48,814 --> 01:43:51,088
Tuples, on the other hand, are immutable.

998
01:43:51,310 --> 01:43:53,796
Once you create them, you cannot change them.

999
01:43:54,734 --> 01:43:59,588
For example, we can call the append method to add the letter D to our list,

1000
01:44:00,750 --> 01:44:04,612
we can call the remove method to delete the letter B from our list,

1001
01:44:05,582 --> 01:44:11,524
or we can update items in the list by reassigning their values at a specific position.

1002
01:44:12,910 --> 01:44:14,980
Tuples on the other hand are immutable.

1003
01:44:15,214 --> 01:44:17,892
They don't support methods like append or remove.

1004
01:44:18,862 --> 01:44:24,036
And if we try to update an item in a tuple by reassigning it, we get an error.

1005
01:44:24,928 --> 01:44:29,408
So what exactly is the point of creating an immutable collection?

1006
01:44:29,728 --> 01:44:31,396
That seems quite limiting.

1007
01:44:32,208 --> 01:44:42,276
Well, the immutability of tuples is actually quite powerful in situations where keeping your data safe from accidental changes is more important than flexibility.

1008
01:44:43,182 --> 01:44:47,684
For example, let's say we want to store a friend's birthday in our program.

1009
01:44:48,622 --> 01:44:56,484
Because a birthday is a piece of information that shouldn't change once it's assigned, storing this type of information in a tuple is a good idea.

1010
01:44:57,422 --> 01:45:02,500
It helps protect the data and prevents it from being accidentally modified later in the program.

1011
01:45:03,246 --> 01:45:05,488
Finally, sets are like lists.

1012
01:45:05,728 --> 01:45:08,292
They can be modified after they are created.

1013
01:45:09,262 --> 01:45:14,020
In particular, we can add new items to a set using the add method,

1014
01:45:14,894 --> 01:45:19,108
and we can remove items using the remove method, just like with lists.

1015
01:45:20,078 --> 01:45:26,532
But since sets are unordered and don't have indexes, we can't directly update a specific item.

1016
01:45:27,328 --> 01:45:35,408
Instead, to change an item in a set, for example the letter B, to the letter D, we remove the unwanted B

1017
01:45:36,558 --> 01:45:37,828
and append the new D.

1018
01:45:38,926 --> 01:45:39,328
Great!

1019
01:45:39,630 --> 01:45:43,620
We've now explored three different ways to store collections in Python.

1020
01:45:43,888 --> 01:45:45,988
Lists, tuples, and sets.

1021
01:45:46,766 --> 01:45:52,128
Each has its own properties, and the appropriate collection depends on the needs of your program.

1022
01:45:53,326 --> 01:45:57,636
By far, lists are the most commonly used type of collection.

1023
01:45:58,542 --> 01:46:03,648
They allow duplicates, are ordered, and are modifiable after creation.

1024
01:46:04,782 --> 01:46:09,168
Tuples and sets are less common, but are useful in specific situations.

1025
01:46:10,126 --> 01:46:14,948
Tuples are similar to lists since they also allow duplicates and are ordered.

1026
01:46:15,854 --> 01:46:19,524
The difference is that tuples are not modifiable after creation.

1027
01:46:20,366 --> 01:46:24,484
This makes them great for storing data that should not change during a program.

1028
01:46:25,294 --> 01:46:29,028
Lastly, sets can be modified after creation.

1029
01:46:29,248 --> 01:46:34,756
But unlike lists and tuples, they don't allow duplicate items and are not ordered.

1030
01:46:35,502 --> 01:46:38,756
Sets are ideal when we want to store unique items.

1031
01:46:39,630 --> 01:46:40,208
Awesome!

1032
01:46:40,526 --> 01:46:45,252
Now you understand the differences between lists, tuples, and sets in Python.

1033
01:46:46,222 --> 01:46:51,236
Now let's head to the notebook linked in the description where we'll practice using the right collection for the job.

1034
01:46:51,568 --> 01:46:55,076
If you want to try the exercises on your own, pause the video here.

1035
01:46:55,488 --> 01:46:58,628
In the next section, we'll walk through the solution step by step.

1036
01:46:59,088 --> 01:47:01,380
Alright, let's jump into problem one.

1037
01:47:02,222 --> 01:47:08,868
In this problem, we're given three different scenarios and need to decide which collection type is best for each one.

1038
01:47:09,710 --> 01:47:14,148
This will test our understanding of when to use lists, tuples, and sets.

1039
01:47:14,990 --> 01:47:17,828
In scenario A, we need to store a birthday.

1040
01:47:18,670 --> 01:47:25,284
The key detail here is that month, day, and year values in a birthday should never change.

1041
01:47:26,126 --> 01:47:30,788
When we have data that shouldn't be modified, a tuple is the right choice.

1042
01:47:31,630 --> 01:47:36,932
Since tuples are immutable, we can't accidentally change them later in the program.

1043
01:47:37,774 --> 01:47:45,008
So we create our birthday tuple using parentheses with the month, day, and year inside,

1044
01:47:45,888 --> 01:47:46,884
and print it.

1045
01:47:48,718 --> 01:47:52,164
Running the code, we see our tuple with all three values.

1046
01:47:53,006 --> 01:48:01,028
Because this is a tuple, if any code later tries to modify these values, Python will throw an error protecting our data.

1047
01:48:02,542 --> 01:48:08,004
In scenario B, we need to store a shopping list that we'll add items to over time.

1048
01:48:08,846 --> 01:48:14,084
A tuple won't work here because tuples are immutable. We can't add items to them.

1049
01:48:14,926 --> 01:48:18,884
A set won't work either because sets don't allow duplicates.

1050
01:48:19,726 --> 01:48:23,684
If we want to buy milk twice, a set would only store it once.

1051
01:48:24,526 --> 01:48:26,724
So a list is the right choice.

1052
01:48:27,566 --> 01:48:32,612
We create our shopping list using square brackets with a few starting items.

1053
01:48:33,454 --> 01:48:39,936
Then we can use the append method to add more items as we think of them and print the final list.

1054
01:48:40,814 --> 01:48:45,220
Running the code, we see our list now includes the new item we added.

1055
01:48:46,062 --> 01:48:50,884
Lists are a very flexible collection type, which is why they're most commonly used.

1056
01:48:51,726 --> 01:48:58,756
In scenario C, we have movie genres with some duplicates, and we want to store only the unique genres.

1057
01:48:59,598 --> 01:49:03,492
When we need unique items, a set is the perfect choice.

1058
01:49:04,334 --> 01:49:10,980
We create our movie genres set using curly braces with all the genres including the duplicates.

1059
01:49:11,822 --> 01:49:19,012
Printing the set and running the code, we see that the set contains only the four unique genres.

1060
01:49:19,854 --> 01:49:24,004
The duplicate, action, and comedy entries were automatically removed.

1061
01:49:24,846 --> 01:49:27,300
Awesome! Let's move on to the second problem.

1062
01:49:28,174 --> 01:49:33,828
In this problem, we have a list of website visitors, but some visitors appear more than once.

1063
01:49:34,670 --> 01:49:39,076
Our goal is to get a list of unique visitors, sorted alphabetically.

1064
01:49:39,918 --> 01:49:44,996
Like in problem one, we'll take advantage of the fact that sets don't allow duplicates.

1065
01:49:45,838 --> 01:49:53,380
To drop the duplicates in our list, we convert our visitors list into a set by wrapping it with the set function.

1066
01:49:54,222 --> 01:49:57,604
This gives us a set containing only the unique names.

1067
01:49:58,446 --> 01:50:01,860
But we can't sort a set because sets are unordered.

1068
01:50:02,702 --> 01:50:08,132
To sort the visitors, we need to convert the set back into a list using the list function.

1069
01:50:08,974 --> 01:50:15,300
Now we have a list of unique visitors. We can call the sort method to arrange them alphabetically.

1070
01:50:16,142 --> 01:50:18,148
Finally, we print the result.

1071
01:50:18,990 --> 01:50:28,100
Running the code, we get a sorted list with just four unique names, Alice, Bob, Charlie, and Diana, now in alphabetical order.

1072
01:50:28,942 --> 01:50:35,876
This pattern of converting a list to a set and back to a list is a common trick for removing duplicates in Python.

1073
01:50:36,718 --> 01:50:38,756
Let's finish up with the third problem.

1074
01:50:39,598 --> 01:50:44,548
In this problem, we're managing a team roster stored in both a list and a set.

1075
01:50:45,390 --> 01:50:51,204
We're comparing how modifications work on each to see which might be better for our use case.

1076
01:50:52,046 --> 01:51:00,484
We need to add a new member, remove an existing member, and understand why updating items works differently in each collection.

1077
01:51:01,326 --> 01:51:04,932
First, let's add Diana to both collections.

1078
01:51:05,774 --> 01:51:08,356
For the list, we use the append method,

1079
01:51:09,198 --> 01:51:11,844
but for the set, we use the add method.

1080
01:51:12,656 --> 01:51:16,004
Next, let's remove Bob from both collections.

1081
01:51:16,846 --> 01:51:20,548
For both the list and the set, we use the remove method.

1082
01:51:21,390 --> 01:51:26,212
This is one of the few methods that works the same way for both collection types.

1083
01:51:27,054 --> 01:51:30,980
To confirm that both collections contain the same names, let's print them.

1084
01:51:35,182 --> 01:51:43,396
Running the code, we see both collections now contain Alice, Charlie, and Diana. Bob has been removed and Diana has been added.

1085
01:51:44,238 --> 01:51:50,660
Lastly, let's discuss why we can't change Charlie to Chuck, the same way in both collections.

1086
01:51:51,502 --> 01:51:56,452
The answer comes down to the fact that lists are ordered and sets are unordered.

1087
01:51:57,294 --> 01:52:02,852
This means that, with a list, we can use indexing to directly update an item.

1088
01:52:03,696 --> 01:52:09,476
For example, we can specify Charlie's position and assign a new value to that index.

1089
01:52:10,318 --> 01:52:14,116
Running the cell, we confirm that Charlie has been changed to Chuck.

1090
01:52:14,976 --> 01:52:18,692
But sets don't have indexes, since they're unordered.

1091
01:52:19,536 --> 01:52:26,372
Instead, to change an item in a set, we have to remove the old value and add the new one.

1092
01:52:27,214 --> 01:52:31,780
Running the code, we see that the set now contains Chuck instead of Charlie.

1093
01:52:32,622 --> 01:52:40,964
This is a key difference between lists and sets. Lists let you update items by position, while sets require you to remove and add.

1094
01:52:41,806 --> 01:52:47,460
Awesome! You've now practiced the key differences between lists, tuples, and sets in Python.

1095
01:52:48,336 --> 01:52:52,656
Now we know when to reach for a list versus a set versus a tuple.

1096
01:52:53,070 --> 01:53:00,772
But so far, if we want to do operations to each of the items in the collections, we need to write an individual line of code for each item.

1097
01:53:01,056 --> 01:53:09,444
In the next section, we'll see how to use a for loop so that we can repeat the same operation for every item in a list with just a few lines of code.

1098
01:53:10,190 --> 01:53:16,176
Have you ever noticed a block of Python code starting with the keyword for, followed by indented lines of code?

1099
01:53:16,416 --> 01:53:21,616
This structure is called a for loop, and programmers often use it to automate repetitive tasks.

1100
01:53:23,406 --> 01:53:25,060
Let's start with a simple example.

1101
01:53:25,966 --> 01:53:30,692
To liquidate inventory, a store owner wants to apply a 10% discount

1102
01:53:31,536 --> 01:53:33,376
to the price of some items.

1103
01:53:35,630 --> 01:53:40,656
To discount the prices using Python, the owner could, one price at a time,

1104
01:53:42,286 --> 01:53:43,616
apply the discount

1105
01:53:44,896 --> 01:53:46,256
and print the results.

1106
01:53:48,078 --> 01:53:52,176
This approach works, we did calculate the discounted prices,

1107
01:53:53,262 --> 01:53:56,496
but writing each calculation manually has some drawbacks.

1108
01:53:57,456 --> 01:54:04,900
Can you imagine how long it would take to implement if, instead of just four prices, the list contained hundreds of them?

1109
01:54:05,742 --> 01:54:14,916
This is exactly where for loops shine. They can efficiently apply the same operations to every item in a list, whether it contains four prices or four hundred.

1110
01:54:15,822 --> 01:54:18,096
A for loop begins with the keyword for.

1111
01:54:19,182 --> 01:54:24,420
This keyword tells Python that we want to repeat some actions for multiple items.

1112
01:54:25,230 --> 01:54:29,028
We follow the for keyword with a variable name of our choice.

1113
01:54:29,870 --> 01:54:34,212
Programmers often call this variable the loop or iteration variable.

1114
01:54:35,054 --> 01:54:41,924
During the execution of the loop, this variable will be sequentially assigned each item in the collection we want to iterate through.

1115
01:54:42,798 --> 01:54:48,228
Since this iteration variable will hold individual price values, let's rename it price.

1116
01:54:49,102 --> 01:54:52,976
Next, we follow the iteration variable with the keyword in.

1117
01:54:53,966 --> 01:55:00,612
The in keyword connects our loop variable to the list we want to loop through. In this case, prices.

1118
01:55:01,518 --> 01:55:05,412
Finally, we complete the first line of our for loop with a colon.

1119
01:55:06,254 --> 01:55:08,804
This first line is called the for loop header.

1120
01:55:09,646 --> 01:55:13,220
Below the header, we write indented code that will run for each item.

1121
01:55:14,222 --> 01:55:16,708
For each price, we apply the discount

1122
01:55:17,614 --> 01:55:19,268
and print the discounted value.

1123
01:55:20,110 --> 01:55:23,076
This indented block is called the loop body.

1124
01:55:23,982 --> 01:55:26,500
And that's it! Our for loop is complete.

1125
01:55:27,342 --> 01:55:31,556
Now let's walk through how Python will execute this loop when the cell is run.

1126
01:55:32,430 --> 01:55:37,444
First, Python executes the for loop header and creates the loop variable price.

1127
01:55:38,286 --> 01:55:47,076
Since this is the first iteration of the loop, Python assigns the first item in the prices list, 1299, to the price variable.

1128
01:55:47,950 --> 01:55:57,252
Python then enters the loop body, calculates 1299 times 0.9, and prints the result 11.691.

1129
01:55:58,094 --> 01:56:01,956
Since there are no more lines in the body, Python returns to the header.

1130
01:56:02,830 --> 01:56:10,496
Now, what happens here is important. The price variable is reassigned to the next value in the list, $1599.

1131
01:56:11,342 --> 01:56:19,396
Python enters the loop body again, calculates $15.99 times 0.9, and prints 14.391.

1132
01:56:20,238 --> 01:56:24,036
This pattern continues as long as there are more prices to discount.

1133
01:56:24,878 --> 01:56:28,096
Python processes the third price, $7.99.

1134
01:56:30,606 --> 01:56:33,092
Then the fourth price, $27.99.

1135
01:56:35,950 --> 01:56:42,724
After the fourth iteration, Python returns to the header but finds no more items in the list, so it exits the loop.

1136
01:56:43,566 --> 01:56:48,260
Since there are no unindented lines of code after the loop, the program ends.

1137
01:56:49,198 --> 01:56:54,180
But if there were more lines of code, Python would move on to executing those.

1138
01:56:55,054 --> 01:56:57,316
To confirm this, let's run our code.

1139
01:56:58,158 --> 01:57:02,660
Great! The discounted prices calculated in our loop match the expected output.

1140
01:57:03,534 --> 01:57:12,804
In addition, notice that the indented code was executed four times, once per price, while the code after the loop was executed only once.

1141
01:57:13,646 --> 01:57:19,396
Alright, let's take stock of what our for loop has done for us by comparing it to our manual approach.

1142
01:57:20,238 --> 01:57:25,732
Originally, we wrote the discounted calculations four separate times, one for each price.

1143
01:57:26,606 --> 01:57:33,636
Using a for loop, we only had to write the discounting operation once, yet Python executed it four times.

1144
01:57:34,510 --> 01:57:37,668
Now imagine if our list was a thousand prices.

1145
01:57:38,510 --> 01:57:41,988
The manual approach would require 1,000 lines of code.

1146
01:57:42,830 --> 01:57:45,700
But the for loop? Still just two lines.

1147
01:57:46,574 --> 01:57:50,980
The loop automatically scales to discount any number of items in the list.

1148
01:57:51,886 --> 01:57:57,732
Awesome! So far we've seen how to build a for loop based on an existing list of items.

1149
01:57:58,574 --> 01:58:03,844
But sometimes we simply need to repeat an action and don't have a list to build from.

1150
01:58:04,686 --> 01:58:09,764
This is also something we can do in Python. Let's see how with a different example.

1151
01:58:10,606 --> 01:58:15,460
Imagine you're organizing a raffle and need to print tickets numbered 0 through 5.

1152
01:58:16,302 --> 01:58:20,900
To create these tickets, we could begin by creating a list with those numbers

1153
01:58:22,734 --> 01:58:25,700
and follow up with a loop that iterates through that list.

1154
01:58:26,624 --> 01:58:26,784
For

1155
01:58:27,904 --> 01:58:36,384
each ticket number in the ticket numbers list, we first print the text Summer Super Raffle,

1156
01:58:36,544 --> 01:58:38,020
followed by the ticket number.

1157
01:58:40,046 --> 01:58:46,724
Notice our loop body now has two lines of code. During each iteration, Python will execute both lines.

1158
01:58:47,566 --> 01:58:51,428
Running this code, Python indeed prints the raffle tickets we wanted.

1159
01:58:52,334 --> 01:58:56,996
This works, but imagine we wanted a hundred raffle tickets instead of just six.

1160
01:58:57,838 --> 01:59:02,372
We would need to manually type out all of those numbers. Seems like a lot of work.

1161
01:59:03,214 --> 01:59:08,004
Luckily, Python has a built-in function that can generate sequential numbers for us.

1162
01:59:08,846 --> 01:59:11,268
This built-in function is called range.

1163
01:59:12,110 --> 01:59:13,412
Here's how we can use it.

1164
01:59:14,350 --> 01:59:22,852
Instead of manually creating a list of numbers and iterating through it, we can call the range function and pass in a 6 as an argument.

1165
01:59:23,726 --> 01:59:30,564
This range function call tells Python to generate numbers starting from 0 and to stop right before 6.

1166
01:59:31,438 --> 01:59:37,060
Running the code, we get the same ticket as before, but this new approach can scale easily.

1167
01:59:37,934 --> 01:59:46,212
For example, if we instead pass 106 into the range function, the range function generates tickets with numbers 0

1168
01:59:47,424 --> 01:59:48,704
to 105

1169
01:59:49,984 --> 01:59:52,452
without any extra work on our end.

1170
01:59:53,294 --> 01:59:57,220
Note that the range function starts generating numbers from 0 by default.

1171
01:59:58,062 --> 02:00:03,844
However, if we want to change this lower bound, we can specify it as the first argument.

1172
02:00:04,686 --> 02:00:13,504
For example, if we want our raffle tickets to be numbered between 1000 and 1005, we can call the range function passing 1000

1173
02:00:13,664 --> 02:00:16,068
and 1006 as arguments.

1174
02:00:16,910 --> 02:00:20,864
Running the code, we get tickets from 1000 to 1005.

1175
02:00:21,742 --> 02:00:30,372
And that's it! In this video, you've learned how to build for loops, both to iterate through existing lists and to repeat actions using the range function.

1176
02:00:31,424 --> 02:00:35,584
Now let's head to the practice notebook to get some experience working with for loops.

1177
02:00:35,744 --> 02:00:39,184
If you want to try the exercises on your own, pause the video here.

1178
02:00:39,424 --> 02:00:43,076
In the next section, we'll work through each of the exercises step by step.

1179
02:00:43,630 --> 02:00:45,252
Alright, let's get into problem one.

1180
02:00:46,126 --> 02:00:52,196
In this problem, we have a list of staff picks from a bookstore, and our goal is to use a for loop to print each title.

1181
02:00:53,102 --> 02:00:57,732
We start our for loop by writing the for keyword, followed by our loop variable.

1182
02:00:58,670 --> 02:01:03,268
Since each item in the list is a book, we'll call the loop variable book.

1183
02:01:04,110 --> 02:01:09,572
After the loop variable, we specify the list to iterate through by writing in staff picks.

1184
02:01:10,414 --> 02:01:12,420
We end the loop header with a colon.

1185
02:01:13,326 --> 02:01:19,300
Indented below the header, we write the loop body, a single print statement that outputs book.

1186
02:01:22,606 --> 02:01:27,300
Running the cell, Python loops through the list and prints each title on its own line.

1187
02:01:28,142 --> 02:01:31,972
Notice that the print statement executed once for every item in the list.

1188
02:01:32,974 --> 02:01:35,556
So four list items means four lines of output.

1189
02:01:36,494 --> 02:01:38,340
Awesome! Let's move on to problem two.

1190
02:01:39,886 --> 02:01:45,092
In this problem, we need to print six book IDs for a set of new arrivals, starting from zero.

1191
02:01:45,902 --> 02:01:49,312
But unlike problem one, we don't have a list to iterate

1192
02:01:49,312 --> 02:01:55,428
through. Instead, we'll use Python's built-in range function to generate a sequence of numbers.

1193
02:01:56,270 --> 02:02:00,132
We can then iterate through this sequence with a for loop just like we would a list.

1194
02:02:01,070 --> 02:02:08,356
To start the loop header, we write for, followed by the loop variable bookID, naming the variable after the ID number it will hold.

1195
02:02:09,390 --> 02:02:16,740
Then, after the in keyword, instead of a list name, we'll call the range function with the arguments 0 and 6.

1196
02:02:17,710 --> 02:02:23,268
These arguments tell Python to generate numbers starting from 0 and stop just before 6.

1197
02:02:24,110 --> 02:02:25,188
In the loop body,

1198
02:02:25,838 --> 02:02:29,636
we output each book ID with a print statement and an F string.

1199
02:02:30,510 --> 02:02:34,308
If you want to learn more about F strings, check out our video linked in the description.

1200
02:02:35,214 --> 02:02:38,980
Running the cell, we get IDs 0 through 5.

1201
02:02:39,950 --> 02:02:42,628
Note that we could have left out the starting argument 0.

1202
02:02:43,502 --> 02:02:47,812
The range function starts at 0 by default when we give only one argument.

1203
02:02:48,782 --> 02:02:50,340
Great, on to the last problem.

1204
02:02:51,310 --> 02:02:55,872
In problem 3, we have a list of clearance book prices and a 15% discount

1205
02:02:55,872 --> 02:03:01,476
rate. Our goal is to print both the original price and the discounted price for each book.

1206
02:03:02,414 --> 02:03:04,548
To loop through the prices, we write the loop header.

1207
02:03:05,550 --> 02:03:08,452
Inside our loop body, we need to write two lines.

1208
02:03:09,390 --> 02:03:14,660
The first line prints the original price using an F string, original followed by price.

1209
02:03:15,502 --> 02:03:18,276
The second line calculates and prints the discounted price.

1210
02:03:18,734 --> 02:03:21,444
Price multiplied by one minus the discount.

1211
02:03:23,950 --> 02:03:27,332
Running the cell, for each price we get two lines of output.

1212
02:03:27,792 --> 02:03:30,820
The original price, then the discounted price right below it.

1213
02:03:31,758 --> 02:03:34,308
Both lines in the body run on every single iteration.

1214
02:03:35,246 --> 02:03:37,508
Awesome, that wraps up all three problems.

1215
02:03:38,512 --> 02:03:42,692
Now we can use a for loop to run the same code on every item in a collection.

1216
02:03:42,992 --> 02:03:49,380
But sometimes we need to know the position of items within collections. A plain for loop doesn't give us that information.

1217
02:03:49,632 --> 02:03:56,740
In the next section, we'll learn about a function that can pair an item with its position and let us access both at once.

1218
02:03:57,872 --> 02:04:02,212
You're given a list of runners in the order they cross the finish line of a race.

1219
02:04:02,798 --> 02:04:05,604
Your job is to print their position and name.

1220
02:04:06,830 --> 02:04:10,980
But if you try doing that with a standard Python loop, there's a problem.

1221
02:04:11,952 --> 02:04:15,684
You only get access to the list items, not their indexes.

1222
02:04:16,718 --> 02:04:18,916
So how should you enumerate the runners?

1223
02:04:19,822 --> 02:04:25,092
Accessing each item's index as we loop through a list is a very common need in programming.

1224
02:04:26,032 --> 02:04:30,564
So common that Python has a built-in way to do it, the enumerate function.

1225
02:04:31,566 --> 02:04:34,532
To use this function, we call it right in our loop header.

1226
02:04:35,534 --> 02:04:38,660
As an argument, we pass in the list we want to iterate through.

1227
02:04:39,470 --> 02:04:41,316
In our example, that's runners.

1228
02:04:42,158 --> 02:04:48,512
Since we don't yet know what enumerate will generate on each pass, let's rename our loop variable x.

1229
02:04:49,632 --> 02:04:52,324
And, to reveal what x holds,

1230
02:04:52,974 --> 02:04:53,636
let's print it.

1231
02:04:54,670 --> 02:04:59,952
Running the code, we see that enumerate produces pairs of indexes and names.

1232
02:05:00,974 --> 02:05:02,564
Each pair is called a tuple.

1233
02:05:03,438 --> 02:05:10,340
If you want to learn more about tuples, including how they compare to lists and sets, check out our video linked in the description.

1234
02:05:11,214 --> 02:05:11,680
Nice!

1235
02:05:11,950 --> 02:05:12,836
We're getting somewhere!

1236
02:05:13,870 --> 02:05:19,428
With enumerate, our loop now walks the list and pairs each runner with a position.

1237
02:05:20,494 --> 02:05:27,716
But to print our information in a more readable format, we need the index and the name as two separate variables.

1238
02:05:28,560 --> 02:05:36,004
We could pull them apart by indexing the tuple, with entry 0 for the index and entry 1 for the name.

1239
02:05:36,846 --> 02:05:43,428
Running the code, we get the right output, but pulling values by index makes the loop harder to read.

1240
02:05:44,270 --> 02:05:49,860
For someone taking a fresh look at this code, x0 and x1 don't say much.

1241
02:05:50,702 --> 02:05:56,164
To make the loop clearer, we can split the tuple's values into their own variables, right in the header.

1242
02:05:57,134 --> 02:06:04,548
To split the tuple, we replace our x variable with two variables, i and runner, separated by a comma.

1243
02:06:05,422 --> 02:06:08,068
i is the conventional name for indexes.

1244
02:06:08,942 --> 02:06:15,172
With this header, the enumerate function still produces a tuple, for example, zero and Alice,

1245
02:06:16,046 --> 02:06:20,964
but that tuple is now pulled apart, and its items are assigned to separate loop variables.

1246
02:06:21,838 --> 02:06:26,980
In Python, splitting a tuple into separate variables is called tuple unpacking.

1247
02:06:28,046 --> 02:06:33,252
From there, we can use i and runner in the loop body like any other variables.

1248
02:06:34,254 --> 02:06:39,492
Running the code, we get each runner's name printed next to their number, nicely formatted.

1249
02:06:40,494 --> 02:06:42,052
There's one small problem though.

1250
02:06:42,480 --> 02:06:46,500
Alice won the race, but the output lists her in zeroth place.

1251
02:06:46,880 --> 02:06:47,780
That's silly.

1252
02:06:48,814 --> 02:06:52,004
The other runners are also listed one place too high.

1253
02:06:52,974 --> 02:06:56,900
That's because the enumerate function counts indexes from zero by default.

1254
02:06:57,870 --> 02:07:02,692
To start the numbering at one instead, the function has an optional start parameter.

1255
02:07:03,534 --> 02:07:10,628
Passing start equals one as an argument tells the function to start generating indexes from one, not zero.

1256
02:07:11,470 --> 02:07:17,124
Running the code, the numbering now begins at one, putting Alice in first place.

1257
02:07:17,646 --> 02:07:18,756
Congrats, Alice!

1258
02:07:19,726 --> 02:07:23,780
By using the start parameter, every runner lines up with the right position.

1259
02:07:24,750 --> 02:07:25,316
Awesome!

1260
02:07:25,520 --> 02:07:30,756
Now you should feel comfortable using enumerate to loop through list items and their indexes.

1261
02:07:31,758 --> 02:07:35,492
But the enumerate function is not the only way to accomplish this task.

1262
02:07:36,494 --> 02:07:42,852
In other people's programs, you may see the same idea implemented using the range and line functions.

1263
02:07:43,694 --> 02:07:51,108
From there, to access each runner, the code has to use manual indexing, accessing the item with brackets.

1264
02:07:52,046 --> 02:07:57,444
And to make sure the places start from one, in the print statement, we need to increment i.

1265
02:07:58,414 --> 02:08:01,316
Running the code, we get the same output as before.

1266
02:08:02,350 --> 02:08:07,972
But indexing by hand makes the loop harder to read, and it leaves the index bookkeeping to us.

1267
02:08:08,942 --> 02:08:12,260
Every one of those manual steps is a chance for bugs to creep in.

1268
02:08:13,166 --> 02:08:19,044
So whenever you need each item together with its index, the best practice is to reach for enumerate.

1269
02:08:20,078 --> 02:08:25,764
It lets Python handle the bookkeeping for us, which is the clearest and safest way to get the job done.

1270
02:08:27,280 --> 02:08:37,668
Great! Now we know how to use the enumerate function to pair an item with its position as we loop through a list. But sometimes the data we need is actually stored across multiple lists.

1271
02:08:37,920 --> 02:08:45,380
If we need to loop through multiple lists together, there's another function we can use, the zip function. We'll see how that works in the next section.

1272
02:08:46,800 --> 02:08:50,468
You're given a list of students and a separate list of their test scores.

1273
02:08:51,054 --> 02:08:56,080
Your job is to print a report containing both the students' names and their test scores.

1274
02:08:56,718 --> 02:08:58,084
But here's the problem.

1275
02:08:59,118 --> 02:09:03,748
By default, a for loop can only iterate through one list at a time.

1276
02:09:04,640 --> 02:09:06,400
So how can we print a report?

1277
02:09:07,438 --> 02:09:12,292
Pairing items from multiple lists at once is a very common need in programming.

1278
02:09:13,264 --> 02:09:17,508
So common that Python has a built-in way to do it, the zip function.

1279
02:09:18,382 --> 02:09:21,412
To use the function, we call it right in our loop header.

1280
02:09:22,350 --> 02:09:27,972
Into that call, we pass the two lists we want to pair, students and their scores.

1281
02:09:28,814 --> 02:09:35,076
Since we don't yet know what zip will generate on each iteration, let's rename our loop variable X.

1282
02:09:36,046 --> 02:09:38,372
To see what X is, we'll print it.

1283
02:09:39,278 --> 02:09:44,548
Running the code, we see that zip produces pairs of students and grades.

1284
02:09:45,518 --> 02:09:47,300
These pairs are called tuples.

1285
02:09:48,302 --> 02:09:55,492
If you want to learn more about tuples, including how they compare to lists and sets, there's a whole video on it linked in the description.

1286
02:09:56,462 --> 02:09:58,436
Okay, we've made good progress.

1287
02:09:59,374 --> 02:10:03,556
Using zip allowed our loop to walk over two lists at the same time.

1288
02:10:04,464 --> 02:10:11,716
But to format our report with a dash in between the name and the grade, we need them both as separate variables.

1289
02:10:12,590 --> 02:10:20,292
We could pull them out of the tuple by indexing it ourselves, with pair 0 for the name and pair 1 for the score.

1290
02:10:21,166 --> 02:10:27,044
Running the code, it works, but indexing manually makes our loop a bit harder to read.

1291
02:10:27,886 --> 02:10:36,292
Someone taking a fresh look at this code would have to refer back to the loop header to see that x0 contains a student name and not a grade.

1292
02:10:37,166 --> 02:10:43,236
Only there can we see that students are first in zip, so x0 must represent a student.

1293
02:10:44,144 --> 02:10:45,444
Not very transparent.

1294
02:10:46,350 --> 02:10:53,444
To improve the readability of our loop, Python lets us assign the tuple's values to separate variables directly in the header.

1295
02:10:54,350 --> 02:11:01,476
To split the tuple, we replace x with two variables, student and score, separated by a comma.

1296
02:11:02,382 --> 02:11:05,700
With this header, the zip function still produces a tuple.

1297
02:11:06,350 --> 02:11:09,380
For example, one with Alice and 85.

1298
02:11:10,414 --> 02:11:14,436
But now, that tuple is pulled apart into two separate variables.

1299
02:11:15,438 --> 02:11:20,452
In Python, splitting a tuple into separate variables is called tuple unpacking.

1300
02:11:21,486 --> 02:11:27,620
Once they've been assigned in the header, we can use these two variables in the loop body just like any other variable.

1301
02:11:28,910 --> 02:11:32,484
Running the code, we get each student's name printed with their score.

1302
02:11:33,518 --> 02:11:33,904
Nice!

1303
02:11:34,286 --> 02:11:39,780
Unpacking gave the name and score their own variables, so we could finally print our report.

1304
02:11:40,782 --> 02:11:46,624
Now, a report with names and grades is useful for a teacher, but students want to know their actual letter

1305
02:11:46,624 --> 02:11:52,356
grade. Can we use zip to pair a third list containing letter grades with the other two?

1306
02:11:53,230 --> 02:11:54,340
The answer is yes.

1307
02:11:54,606 --> 02:11:57,252
The zip function can pair any number of lists.

1308
02:11:58,126 --> 02:12:02,916
To add the third list, we simply pass it as another argument in the zip function.

1309
02:12:03,918 --> 02:12:07,588
Now each tuple generated by zip will hold three values.

1310
02:12:08,558 --> 02:12:12,196
To capture this third value, we add a third loop variable.

1311
02:12:13,198 --> 02:12:16,740
Finally, we can output this variable in the loop body.

1312
02:12:17,614 --> 02:12:23,940
Running the code, we get each student's name, score, and a letter grade, all matched up.

1313
02:12:24,878 --> 02:12:29,252
This pairing works perfectly when all of the lists have the same number of elements.

1314
02:12:30,144 --> 02:12:31,556
But sometimes they don't.

1315
02:12:32,494 --> 02:12:39,844
Suppose Bob missed the test, so students still has three elements, but scores and grades only have two.

1316
02:12:40,864 --> 02:12:43,460
Running the code, we only get two pairs.

1317
02:12:44,302 --> 02:12:49,476
That's because zip stops creating pairs the moment it reaches the end of the shortest list.

1318
02:12:50,144 --> 02:12:52,388
Here, both scores and grades.

1319
02:12:53,262 --> 02:12:57,988
Looking a bit closer, Bob was assigned a score even though he didn't take the test.

1320
02:12:58,830 --> 02:13:02,820
This happened because zip matches items purely by position.

1321
02:13:03,264 --> 02:13:06,436
It has no idea which ones truly belong together.

1322
02:13:07,374 --> 02:13:12,964
Worst of all, Python didn't raise an error or give us any warning about either of these issues.

1323
02:13:13,806 --> 02:13:20,548
So the lesson here is: when you're zipping several lists that all have to stay in sync, you need to be careful.

1324
02:13:21,550 --> 02:13:26,180
If you can change how the data is stored, it would probably be safer to use a dictionary here.

1325
02:13:27,182 --> 02:13:31,524
If you want to learn more about dictionaries, there's a whole video on them linked in the description.

1326
02:13:32,590 --> 02:13:38,532
But if you're just combining a few lists, and you know they're the same length, zip is a great choice.

1327
02:13:39,406 --> 02:13:39,904
Awesome!

1328
02:13:40,142 --> 02:13:43,908
By now you should feel comfortable using zip to pair lists together.

1329
02:13:44,814 --> 02:13:46,244
But it's not the only way.

1330
02:13:47,182 --> 02:13:53,860
Especially in older code, you'll see the same job done by looping over the indexes with range and length.

1331
02:13:54,894 --> 02:13:59,812
Then, to access the list items, the code has to use manual indexing.

1332
02:14:00,718 --> 02:14:04,356
This does work, and it produces the same output.

1333
02:14:05,198 --> 02:14:11,236
But as we discussed earlier, using manual indexing makes the loop harder to read and more error prone.

1334
02:14:12,142 --> 02:14:15,332
So if you find code like this, reach for zip instead.

1335
02:14:16,238 --> 02:14:19,588
It's the clearest and safest way to loop through several lists at once.

1336
02:14:20,784 --> 02:14:26,468
Awesome! Now we know how to use the zip and enumerate functions to improve how for loops work with lists.

1337
02:14:26,784 --> 02:14:35,584
Now let's head to the practice exercises linked in the description to get some practice using these functions. If you want to practice on your own first, pause the video here.

1338
02:14:35,824 --> 02:14:38,596
In the next section, we'll walk through the solutions together.

1339
02:14:39,502 --> 02:14:41,956
You're a music producer who's just finished an album.

1340
02:14:42,304 --> 02:14:48,996
Before the album can be released, you need to take some information about each of the tracks and build a finished track list.

1341
02:14:49,902 --> 02:14:53,092
Every song numbered and paired with its duration.

1342
02:14:53,998 --> 02:14:59,524
The problem is the information you're given sits scattered across four separate Python lists.

1343
02:15:00,526 --> 02:15:08,356
One for the main album titles, another for the durations, and two more with the same details for the bonus tracks.

1344
02:15:09,198 --> 02:15:14,212
So how do you turn these four disconnected lists into the finished track list?

1345
02:15:15,086 --> 02:15:23,204
That's exactly what we're going to build, one step at a time, using two of Python's handiest tools for working with lists, enumerate

1346
02:15:23,470 --> 02:15:24,164
and zip.

1347
02:15:25,006 --> 02:15:30,564
If you tried the practice notebook from our enumerate or zip videos, you can check your work as we go.

1348
02:15:31,374 --> 02:15:34,724
And if you haven't, we link the notebook we're using in the description.

1349
02:15:35,598 --> 02:15:37,476
Okay, let's dive into the code.

1350
02:15:38,318 --> 02:15:45,572
As a first step to our final track list, let's output all the song names in the titles list and their numbered positions.

1351
02:15:46,446 --> 02:15:50,372
To display these titles, we simply loop over the titles list.

1352
02:15:51,214 --> 02:15:54,308
But that doesn't give us access to the item's positions.

1353
02:15:55,278 --> 02:16:00,932
To get those numbers, we wrap the titles list in the enumerate function right in the loop header.

1354
02:16:01,774 --> 02:16:08,516
Now, on each iteration of the loop, the enumerate function generates a tuple containing the position and the title.

1355
02:16:09,422 --> 02:16:17,264
To capture both these values and use them independently in the loop body, we unpack them into two variables number

1356
02:16:17,614 --> 02:16:18,308
and title.

1357
02:16:19,182 --> 02:16:23,332
Inside the body of the loop, we can print those variables using an F string.

1358
02:16:24,238 --> 02:16:28,004
If F strings are new to you, there's a video on them linked in the description.

1359
02:16:28,974 --> 02:16:33,540
Inside the F string, we'll print the number, a dot, and then the title.

1360
02:16:34,446 --> 02:16:38,308
If we run the code, our output is close to what we want.

1361
02:16:39,150 --> 02:16:43,748
But the numbers start from zero, since that's the default behavior of the enumerate function.

1362
02:16:44,750 --> 02:16:50,596
To change this, we can pass the argument start equals 1 to the enumerate function.

1363
02:16:51,502 --> 02:16:56,740
Running the code again, our four songs come out cleanly numbered 1 through 4.

1364
02:16:57,616 --> 02:17:00,964
Okay, that's a good start on the final output we're building towards.

1365
02:17:01,998 --> 02:17:04,196
Next, we'll add each song's duration.

1366
02:17:05,102 --> 02:17:10,596
Those lengths are given to us in a second list called durations, lined up with their titles list.

1367
02:17:11,470 --> 02:17:17,124
To loop through both lists together, we hand them to the zip function right inside the enumerate call.

1368
02:17:17,998 --> 02:17:20,740
And yes, you can nest these functions.

1369
02:17:21,614 --> 02:17:27,780
Enumerate happily works on any sequence we give it, and the result of zip is just a sequence of tuples.

1370
02:17:28,750 --> 02:17:35,296
Zip pairs list items by position, so now enumerate hands us a number and a pair on each pass.

1371
02:17:36,174 --> 02:17:39,332
That means we need to unpack that data in two layers.

1372
02:17:40,256 --> 02:17:43,204
First, the number produced by the enumerate function,

1373
02:17:44,176 --> 02:17:48,388
then the title and duration from inside the tuple.

1374
02:17:49,262 --> 02:17:55,012
Once unpacked, though, we can use all these just like any other regular variable in the loop body.

1375
02:17:55,886 --> 02:18:00,612
So to include the duration in the printed output, we simply add it to the F string.

1376
02:18:01,486 --> 02:18:06,340
Running the code, every song now shows its length right next to its number.

1377
02:18:07,150 --> 02:18:09,508
Great! That's the main four songs done.

1378
02:18:10,350 --> 02:18:13,700
But the full track list we're building has three more bonus tracks.

1379
02:18:14,702 --> 02:18:20,496
These bonus tracks are waiting in their own two lists, bonus titles and bonus durations.

1380
02:18:21,422 --> 02:18:30,020
We want to print them at the end of the track list, numbered to continue the count, 5, 6, and 7, rather than starting over at 1.

1381
02:18:30,862 --> 02:18:36,932
To do this, we can reuse the loop we just wrote, just pointed at the bonus lists instead.

1382
02:18:37,774 --> 02:18:41,316
The only other change is where the enumerate numbering begins.

1383
02:18:42,158 --> 02:18:46,884
The enumerate function's start argument can be any number we like, not just one.

1384
02:18:47,726 --> 02:18:54,500
Since the main album ends at track 4, we pass start equals 5 instead of start equals 1.

1385
02:18:55,534 --> 02:19:03,780
Then to keep the two sections distinct, we print a heading above each, main album before the first loop, and bonus tracks before the second.

1386
02:19:04,718 --> 02:19:10,628
Running the code, the bonus tracks now slot in under their own heading, numbered 5 through 7.

1387
02:19:11,470 --> 02:19:15,044
Awesome! We now have our finished track list nicely formatted.

1388
02:19:15,950 --> 02:19:23,844
The enumerate function gave us the numbering, the zip function paired our lists together, and combining the two let us do both at once.

1389
02:19:24,816 --> 02:19:35,616
By now we're very familiar using for loops to iterate through collections of data. And as we've seen, two of those collections, lists and tuples, store data by position.

1390
02:19:36,016 --> 02:19:39,108
But storing data by position has some limitations.

1391
02:19:39,376 --> 02:19:45,636
In the next section, we'll see a new type of data that allows us to create custom labels to store our data with.

1392
02:19:46,416 --> 02:19:51,140
What if I told you there's a Python data structure that works like a real dictionary?

1393
02:19:52,014 --> 02:19:56,548
When you look up a word or a key, you get its definition or value.

1394
02:19:57,390 --> 02:20:04,004
That's pretty useful when you want to organize data with meaningful labels, such as the email addresses of your best friends.

1395
02:20:05,006 --> 02:20:07,936
This data structure is called a Python dictionary.

1396
02:20:08,174 --> 02:20:09,636
Let's see how to create one.

1397
02:20:10,510 --> 02:20:14,244
To create a Python dictionary, we start with curly brackets.

1398
02:20:15,182 --> 02:20:18,628
In between those brackets, we write the dictionary entries.

1399
02:20:19,470 --> 02:20:22,980
Let's first create an entry to store Bob's email address.

1400
02:20:23,918 --> 02:20:28,420
Each dictionary entry has two parts, a key and a value.

1401
02:20:29,326 --> 02:20:33,220
The key is the label we want to use to access the value.

1402
02:20:34,190 --> 02:20:39,108
In Bob's case, the key is his name and the value is his email address.

1403
02:20:40,110 --> 02:20:45,508
In Python, the syntax we use to connect the key and the value is a colon.

1404
02:20:46,446 --> 02:20:53,188
Entries like this are called key value pairs because each key is linked to a specific value.

1405
02:20:54,190 --> 02:20:58,496
When a dictionary has multiple entries, we separate them with commas.

1406
02:20:59,374 --> 02:21:03,812
We can then similarly create key value pairs for our other friends.

1407
02:21:07,630 --> 02:21:11,140
And just like that, you've created your first Python dictionary.

1408
02:21:12,110 --> 02:21:16,132
Now, let's see how quickly we can look up Bob's email address.

1409
02:21:17,102 --> 02:21:22,256
To do this, let's first assign our dictionary to the variable contacts.

1410
02:21:23,374 --> 02:21:29,860
To retrieve Bob's email address from the dictionary, we follow the name of our dictionary with square brackets.

1411
02:21:30,798 --> 02:21:36,612
Inside those brackets, we put the key we're looking for, in this case, Bob in quotes.

1412
02:21:37,582 --> 02:21:43,556
And when we run this, we get Bob's email address, ilovebunnies at gmail.com.

1413
02:21:44,526 --> 02:21:51,428
In other words, by using the key Bob, we can directly access the corresponding value in the dictionary.

1414
02:21:52,398 --> 02:21:57,636
Awesome! Now you know how to create dictionaries and access values using keys.

1415
02:21:58,606 --> 02:22:04,420
But as you might know, dictionaries are not the only way we can organize collections of items in Python.

1416
02:22:05,358 --> 02:22:12,292
For example, instead of creating a dictionary of our friends' emails, we could have created a list of emails.

1417
02:22:14,510 --> 02:22:16,580
Would using a list be a better idea?

1418
02:22:17,550 --> 02:22:23,492
Before we go any further, if you need to refresh your knowledge of Python lists, we've left a link in the description.

1419
02:22:24,398 --> 02:22:30,852
Now, let's discuss why using a dictionary instead of a list is a better idea in the current example.

1420
02:22:31,694 --> 02:22:37,828
In our example, both the list and dictionary contain the same values, our friends' emails.

1421
02:22:38,798 --> 02:22:42,372
But the difference is how the values are identified.

1422
02:22:43,342 --> 02:22:49,252
In a list, the items are identified or indexed by their position in the list.

1423
02:22:50,222 --> 02:22:56,388
If we want to get Bob's email address, we need to remember that it is the first item in the list

1424
02:22:57,358 --> 02:22:59,428
and index that list with zero.

1425
02:23:00,398 --> 02:23:03,460
That's reasonable with three friends in our contact list.

1426
02:23:03,856 --> 02:23:11,524
But if you're really popular and have dozens of friends, remembering the position of each friend's email in the list will be very hard.

1427
02:23:12,558 --> 02:23:17,476
With a dictionary, we only need to remember our friends' names to get their emails.

1428
02:23:18,446 --> 02:23:19,696
Here's the bottom line.

1429
02:23:20,078 --> 02:23:25,604
Dictionaries make it more simple and intuitive to work with data that have meaningful labels.

1430
02:23:26,446 --> 02:23:34,500
For example, they're great for organizing things like usernames and passwords, products and their prices, or students and their grades.

1431
02:23:35,406 --> 02:23:41,892
In each case, the key serves as a clear label that helps us quickly access the related value.

1432
02:23:42,862 --> 02:23:43,376
Awesome!

1433
02:23:43,630 --> 02:23:51,140
At this point, you understand the differences between dictionaries and lists and know when it is better to use a dictionary.

1434
02:23:52,206 --> 02:24:01,252
But to use dictionaries effectively in your own projects, it is important to understand what kinds of data can be used as keys and values.

1435
02:24:02,286 --> 02:24:06,340
Let's dive into the rules and best practices for keys and values.

1436
02:24:07,278 --> 02:24:11,940
First, the values of a dictionary can be of any data type.

1437
02:24:12,750 --> 02:24:21,540
For example, we could create a siblings dictionary where each value is an integer showing how many siblings each friend has.

1438
02:24:22,510 --> 02:24:31,396
Or we could create a has pet dictionary where the values are Booleans, true if the friend has a pet and false if not.

1439
02:24:32,366 --> 02:24:37,540
Notice that in the has pet dictionary, the value true appears more than once.

1440
02:24:38,382 --> 02:24:39,780
That's totally fine.

1441
02:24:40,782 --> 02:24:44,388
In Python, different keys can share the same value.

1442
02:24:45,230 --> 02:24:48,612
However, dictionary keys must be unique.

1443
02:24:49,486 --> 02:24:53,796
For example, let's try to add a second email address for Wendy.

1444
02:24:54,766 --> 02:25:01,540
To do this, we'll write a second Wendy key in the dictionary definition and pair it with a second email.

1445
02:25:02,510 --> 02:25:09,476
When we run the cell and display our updated dictionary, only one of the entries for Wendy has been added to the dictionary.

1446
02:25:10,510 --> 02:25:13,316
If you think about it, this behavior makes sense.

1447
02:25:14,190 --> 02:25:20,164
If our dictionary had two Wendy keys, how would Python know which email to return?

1448
02:25:23,086 --> 02:25:26,980
Finally, the keys of a dictionary aren't limited to strings.

1449
02:25:27,918 --> 02:25:31,296
They can be integers, floats, or even booleans.

1450
02:25:32,206 --> 02:25:38,436
That said, strings are by far the most common choice, so that's what we'll use in the rest of this video.

1451
02:25:39,406 --> 02:25:43,556
Great! Now you understand the rules for dictionary keys and values.

1452
02:25:44,526 --> 02:25:53,252
That's enough information to create dictionaries, but what if you want to modify an existing dictionary by adding or removing entries?

1453
02:25:54,158 --> 02:26:02,916
For example, let's say we've made a new friend named Alex and want to add their email, coolalex at gmail.com, to our dictionary.

1454
02:26:03,726 --> 02:26:04,932
That's easy to do.

1455
02:26:05,902 --> 02:26:12,548
Simply write the dictionary name, followed by square brackets, with the new key Alex inside.

1456
02:26:13,550 --> 02:26:19,044
Then use the equal sign to assign coolAlex at gmail.com as the value.

1457
02:26:19,982 --> 02:26:20,656
That's it!

1458
02:26:21,006 --> 02:26:22,788
The new pair has been added.

1459
02:26:23,758 --> 02:26:28,932
Now, to remove a key value pair, we can use the pop method.

1460
02:26:29,902 --> 02:26:35,428
For example, let's say Wendy and I had a fight and I want to remove her from my contacts.

1461
02:26:36,430 --> 02:26:42,496
To do that, I can call the pop method on the dictionary and pass in her name as the key.

1462
02:26:43,118 --> 02:26:44,004
Bye Wendy!

1463
02:26:44,974 --> 02:26:51,524
Awesome! So far we focused on operations that only affect a single entry in the dictionary.

1464
02:26:52,558 --> 02:26:58,692
But what if you want to iterate over the whole dictionary, all the names, all the emails, or both together?

1465
02:26:59,662 --> 02:27:02,948
Python has a few methods that make this really easy.

1466
02:27:03,854 --> 02:27:12,356
For example, let's say we want to check if Alex is in our contacts without having to print the entire dictionary and manually search through it.

1467
02:27:13,358 --> 02:27:15,428
We can do this with the keys method.

1468
02:27:16,398 --> 02:27:20,932
As you can probably guess, the keys method returns all the keys in the dictionary.

1469
02:27:21,774 --> 02:27:26,436
Note that the keys here are wrapped inside a dictionary keys object.

1470
02:27:27,406 --> 02:27:32,772
When we use a for loop, we can iterate over this type of object just like we would with a list.

1471
02:27:33,742 --> 02:27:39,492
So, to see if Alex is in our contacts, we can write a for loop that iterates through the keys.

1472
02:27:40,334 --> 02:27:46,176
During each iteration of the loop, we check to see if the key is equal to Alex

1473
02:27:47,118 --> 02:27:48,996
and print a message if it is.

1474
02:27:49,934 --> 02:27:50,336
Awesome!

1475
02:27:50,670 --> 02:27:53,508
Our loop confirms that Alex is in our contacts.

1476
02:27:54,510 --> 02:27:59,364
This works for names, but what if we only care about the email addresses themselves?

1477
02:28:00,302 --> 02:28:10,276
For example, let's say we want to check if a certain email address, iLovebunnies at gmail.com, is in our contact list without printing the entire dictionary.

1478
02:28:11,278 --> 02:28:13,444
We can do this with the values method.

1479
02:28:14,382 --> 02:28:19,780
Similar to the keys method, the values method returns all the values in the dictionary.

1480
02:28:20,782 --> 02:28:27,876
To check if iLovunnies at gmail.com is in our contacts, we write a for loop that iterates through the values.

1481
02:28:28,910 --> 02:28:36,068
During each iteration, we compare the value to ilovunnies at gmail.com and print a message if it matches.

1482
02:28:37,038 --> 02:28:37,376
Great!

1483
02:28:37,742 --> 02:28:43,396
Our loop confirms that iLovunnies at gmail.com is an email in our contacts.

1484
02:28:44,430 --> 02:28:48,324
But what if we need both the key and the value at the same time?

1485
02:28:49,198 --> 02:28:53,668
For example, to figure out whose email is ilovunnies at gmail.com.

1486
02:28:54,670 --> 02:28:58,052
To do this, we use a for loop and the items method.

1487
02:28:58,894 --> 02:29:05,296
The items method returns both the keys and values at the same time as tuples.

1488
02:29:06,286 --> 02:29:14,468
Inside the loop, we can reuse our comparison logic, but update our print statement to output the key associated with the current value.

1489
02:29:15,438 --> 02:29:20,932
Running the cell, our program tells us that Bob's email is ilovebunnies at gmail.com.

1490
02:29:21,870 --> 02:29:28,100
Awesome! You now know how to use Python dictionaries to organize and store information in your own code.

1491
02:29:28,878 --> 02:29:37,952
Now let's head to the practice notebook linked in the description and build the dictionary from scratch. If you want to try the exercises on your own first, pause the video here.

1492
02:29:38,192 --> 02:29:41,872
In the next section, we'll work through the exercises step by step.

1493
02:29:42,414 --> 02:29:44,740
Alright, let's jump into problem one.

1494
02:29:45,550 --> 02:29:52,676
In this problem, we need to create a dictionary called Capitals that maps three countries to their capital cities.

1495
02:29:53,518 --> 02:29:59,300
Once we create the dictionary, we need to print Japan's capital using its country name as the key.

1496
02:30:00,142 --> 02:30:10,084
Using a dictionary here, instead of another type like a list, makes sense because we have named relationships. Each country maps to a specific capital.

1497
02:30:10,926 --> 02:30:15,268
Okay, to get started, we create the dictionary with curly brackets.

1498
02:30:16,110 --> 02:30:24,352
Inside the brackets, we write each entry as a key value pair, the country name as a string, followed by a colon,

1499
02:30:24,672 --> 02:30:27,108
and then the capital city as a string.

1500
02:30:30,094 --> 02:30:32,484
Each pair is separated by a comma.

1501
02:30:33,326 --> 02:30:37,412
To use this dictionary later, we'll sign it to the variable capitals.

1502
02:30:38,254 --> 02:30:47,632
With the dictionary set up, we can search it for the capital city of Japan by writing the name of the dictionary, followed by the key Japan,

1503
02:30:48,032 --> 02:30:49,412
and printing the result.

1504
02:30:50,254 --> 02:30:55,236
When we run the cell, we get Tokyo, the value stored under the key Japan.

1505
02:30:56,078 --> 02:31:04,420
By using a meaningful label as the key, we can easily look up any values. No need to remember positions or search through the whole dictionary.

1506
02:31:05,262 --> 02:31:07,300
Great, let's move on to problem two.

1507
02:31:08,142 --> 02:31:20,324
In this problem, we're given a cafe menu dictionary and need to make two changes. Add a new item called latte with a price of 450, and remove drip coffee since it's been discontinued.

1508
02:31:21,166 --> 02:31:26,724
The starting dictionary is already written for us, so we just need to add our modifications below it.

1509
02:31:27,566 --> 02:31:34,020
To add the new latte item, we write the name of the dictionary menu followed by square brackets.

1510
02:31:34,862 --> 02:31:40,452
Inside the brackets, we specify the key latte and set its value to 450.

1511
02:31:41,262 --> 02:31:45,956
Note that this is the same syntax we use to access a dictionary's value.

1512
02:31:46,798 --> 02:31:52,996
But when we put it on the left side of an equal sign, Python adds or updates that entry in the dictionary.

1513
02:31:53,838 --> 02:32:00,740
Next, to remove drip coffee, we call menu.pop and pass in the string drip coffee.

1514
02:32:01,582 --> 02:32:05,540
To see what our dictionary looks like after those changes, let's print it.

1515
02:32:06,382 --> 02:32:11,012
Running the cell, we see that drip coffee is gone and latte has been added.

1516
02:32:11,854 --> 02:32:14,180
Awesome, let's move on to problem three.

1517
02:32:15,022 --> 02:32:19,364
In this problem, we're given a dictionary of five students and their test scores.

1518
02:32:20,206 --> 02:32:23,972
We need to print all the students' names using the keys method.

1519
02:32:24,782 --> 02:32:30,628
After that, we need to use the items method to print only the students who scored above 80.

1520
02:32:31,822 --> 02:32:33,860
Let's start with printing students' names.

1521
02:32:34,702 --> 02:32:42,980
Since the names are the keys in our dictionary, we can use the keys method to get the names as a list, and use a loop to iterate through them.

1522
02:32:43,822 --> 02:32:47,044
Then, inside the loop, we can print each name.

1523
02:32:48,878 --> 02:32:57,540
For the second task, printing only the students who scored above an 80, we need to output both the name of the student and their score.

1524
02:32:58,382 --> 02:33:07,556
To get both the key and the value for each entry in the dictionary, we need to use the items method, which returns each key value pair as a tuple.

1525
02:33:08,398 --> 02:33:14,020
We can then use a loop to unpack each tuple into separate name and score variables.

1526
02:33:14,862 --> 02:33:18,756
Inside the loop, we check if each score is greater than 80.

1527
02:33:19,566 --> 02:33:23,812
If it is, we print the name and score using an F string.

1528
02:33:24,654 --> 02:33:28,580
If you want to learn more about F strings, check out our video linked in the description.

1529
02:33:30,702 --> 02:33:35,332
Running the cell, we get all the students' names produced by the keys method.

1530
02:33:36,256 --> 02:33:43,300
And, after the names, we get only the students who scored more than 80. Alice, Carol, and Eve.

1531
02:33:44,142 --> 02:33:52,100
Using the items method lets us work with both keys and values in a single loop, which is a common need when you're working with real data.

1532
02:33:52,816 --> 02:33:58,096
Now we can store data in a dictionary and understand how to work with the dictionary's key-value pairs.

1533
02:33:58,256 --> 02:34:05,616
But those key value pairs, along with all the other data our program has worked with, has been defined before the program was run.

1534
02:34:05,936 --> 02:34:13,540
In the next section, we'll learn how to use user input so that we don't need to define every single piece of data before the program runs.

1535
02:34:14,382 --> 02:34:19,748
Want to make your Python programs more interactive? Asking users for input is the key.

1536
02:34:20,398 --> 02:34:26,180
After watching this video, you'll know exactly how to ask for input while avoiding a common beginner mistake.

1537
02:34:27,502 --> 02:34:34,980
To get familiar with the syntax, we'll write the code for this simple program that asks for a user's name and outputs a customized greeting message.

1538
02:34:36,462 --> 02:34:40,452
In Python, the easiest way to get user input is to call the input function.

1539
02:34:41,454 --> 02:34:44,740
To understand what the input function does, let's run the cell.

1540
02:34:45,742 --> 02:34:49,476
As you can see, the function call created an input box under the cell.

1541
02:34:50,382 --> 02:34:54,532
At this point, the program is waiting for the user to enter information in the box.

1542
02:34:55,278 --> 02:34:59,300
This is fine, but without more context, it's hard for a user to know what to input.

1543
02:35:00,238 --> 02:35:05,348
To prompt the user for a specific input, we can provide a message as an argument to the function.

1544
02:35:06,126 --> 02:35:10,308
Since we want the user to enter their name, let's pass the string enter your name.

1545
02:35:11,278 --> 02:35:15,300
Now when we run the cell, our prompt is displayed before the input box.

1546
02:35:16,270 --> 02:35:17,156
Much clearer.

1547
02:35:18,158 --> 02:35:22,916
Once the user inputs their name and presses enter, the input is displayed in the output box.

1548
02:35:23,918 --> 02:35:29,028
That's fine, but remember, our program should greet the user by printing hello followed by their name.

1549
02:35:30,030 --> 02:35:35,620
To create our greeting message, we'll first save the return value of the input function to a variable called name.

1550
02:35:36,686 --> 02:35:40,516
Then we'll write a print statement that outputs hello followed by name.

1551
02:35:41,454 --> 02:35:44,772
Now when we run the cell, the user is prompted to enter their name

1552
02:35:45,422 --> 02:35:47,140
and the custom greeting is printed.

1553
02:35:48,174 --> 02:35:48,576
Great!

1554
02:35:48,878 --> 02:35:54,660
In this program, we ask the user to input text, but we can also use the input function to ask for numbers.

1555
02:35:55,598 --> 02:35:59,908
However, when asking for numeric input, beginners often make a common mistake.

1556
02:36:00,878 --> 02:36:04,356
Let's see what that mistake is and how to avoid it with another example.

1557
02:36:05,358 --> 02:36:08,196
This new program will ask the users for two numbers

1558
02:36:10,656 --> 02:36:11,428
add them up.

1559
02:36:13,390 --> 02:36:21,456
For example, to add 1 and 2, we run the cell and enter 1 and 2 as inputs and get 12?

1560
02:36:22,478 --> 02:36:24,576
Hmm, that's not what we expected.

1561
02:36:25,614 --> 02:36:26,576
Why did this happen?

1562
02:36:27,534 --> 02:36:32,132
We got 12 because the input function always returns the user's input as a string.

1563
02:36:33,134 --> 02:36:37,572
So the plus operator concatenated the two variables instead of adding them.

1564
02:36:38,606 --> 02:36:42,692
We can confirm that these variables are strings by using the type function.

1565
02:36:44,334 --> 02:36:45,860
Number one is a string.

1566
02:36:46,862 --> 02:36:52,004
Luckily, numbers represented as strings can always be converted to integers or floats.

1567
02:36:52,942 --> 02:37:00,036
To avoid working with data of the wrong type, it's good programming practice to immediately cast user inputs to the data type we expect.

1568
02:37:01,038 --> 02:37:06,084
For that reason, we'll cast the user's input to floats using the float operator.

1569
02:37:07,054 --> 02:37:10,628
Now we can safely assume that both of our number variables are floats.

1570
02:37:11,662 --> 02:37:16,484
When we rerun our code and enter 1 and 2 again, we get 3.

1571
02:37:17,838 --> 02:37:18,256
Great!

1572
02:37:18,446 --> 02:37:22,436
Now you know how to collect user input and ensure it's the right data type.

1573
02:37:23,456 --> 02:37:30,096
When our program asks for a number and gets a word instead, Python stops the whole program and shows an error message.

1574
02:37:30,336 --> 02:37:36,496
For the person using our program, all they'd get is this messy error message, which is not a great user experience.

1575
02:37:36,736 --> 02:37:42,176
In the next section, we'll learn how to better handle these errors and improve the user experience.

1576
02:37:42,766 --> 02:37:47,536
Have you ever wondered what the try and accept keywords are and what they do in Python?

1577
02:37:47,856 --> 02:37:54,896
In Python, these two keywords are used to handle unexpected situations gracefully and prevent programs from crashing.

1578
02:37:55,136 --> 02:38:00,976
In this video, you'll learn how to use these keywords to prevent errors and make your program more robust.

1579
02:38:01,806 --> 02:38:07,044
Let's start with a simple example that shows what happens when a program doesn't handle exceptions properly.

1580
02:38:08,014 --> 02:38:11,716
We'll write a division program that asks the user to input a numerator,

1581
02:38:14,318 --> 02:38:15,428
a denominator,

1582
02:38:18,222 --> 02:38:19,588
and calculates the quotient.

1583
02:38:24,558 --> 02:38:32,100
Because the input function returns the user's input as strings, we'll cast both inputs to floats to enforce the correct data type.

1584
02:38:33,198 --> 02:38:38,884
If you'd like to learn more about user input in Python, we've left a link to our video on this topic in the description.

1585
02:38:39,790 --> 02:38:44,004
Our program will work as expected as long as the user provides valid inputs.

1586
02:38:44,782 --> 02:38:49,028
However, if the user enters something unexpected, then the program crashes.

1587
02:38:49,774 --> 02:38:54,084
We can safely assume that, for the average user, the message on the screen is gibberish.

1588
02:38:54,958 --> 02:38:57,764
An output like this is a poor user experience.

1589
02:38:58,734 --> 02:39:04,740
To make our program more user-friendly, we need to handle these errors, also known as exceptions, more gracefully.

1590
02:39:05,742 --> 02:39:10,276
We should let the user know what went wrong and what the user should do to address this issue.

1591
02:39:11,278 --> 02:39:13,956
This is where the try and accept keywords come in.

1592
02:39:14,894 --> 02:39:18,532
We'll use these keywords to help us manage unexpected exceptions.

1593
02:39:19,534 --> 02:39:23,684
Under the try statement, we indent the code that might potentially cause an exception.

1594
02:39:24,686 --> 02:39:32,400
If an exception occurs during the execution of the try block, Python stops executing the try block and moves down to the accept

1595
02:39:32,640 --> 02:39:32,868
block.

1596
02:39:33,870 --> 02:39:39,204
In the accept block, we write code that either addresses the exception or outputs an instruction for the user.

1597
02:39:40,206 --> 02:39:45,252
If the execution of the try block does not generate an exception, the accept block is skipped.

1598
02:39:46,094 --> 02:39:51,652
Let's implement a try accept block in our quotient calculator program to handle invalid user inputs.

1599
02:39:52,494 --> 02:39:58,308
We start by writing the try keyword above the potentially problematic code and indenting the lines of concern.

1600
02:39:59,246 --> 02:40:04,644
Then we write the corresponding accept keyword on the same level of indentation as the try keyword.

1601
02:40:05,646 --> 02:40:09,572
Under this keyword, we'll write the code that we want to execute if an exception occurs.

1602
02:40:10,414 --> 02:40:18,564
In our case, we'll write a simple statement that lets our user know there was an exception and provide clear instructions on how to prevent the error the next time the program is run.

1603
02:40:19,438 --> 02:40:21,124
Great, let's test the code.

1604
02:40:22,126 --> 02:40:25,988
Like before, when the user inputs numbers, the program calculates the quotient.

1605
02:40:26,990 --> 02:40:35,396
However, if the user inputs non-numeric data, the program now handles the exception more gracefully by telling the user that they provided invalid input.

1606
02:40:36,398 --> 02:40:41,444
That's an improvement, but entering non-numeric input is not the only type of mistake the user can make.

1607
02:40:42,478 --> 02:40:50,596
For example, if we rerun our program and enter zero as the denominator, our program outputs the same error message.

1608
02:40:51,566 --> 02:40:55,460
But the two inputs are numbers, so this error message is confusing.

1609
02:40:56,302 --> 02:41:01,860
The issue here is different. We attempted to divide 12 by 0, which of course is impossible.

1610
02:41:02,830 --> 02:41:08,452
This produced an exception, which was handled by our accept block, which then printed the error message on the screen.

1611
02:41:09,454 --> 02:41:17,156
To avoid confusing the user, it would be better if our program could handle the two types of errors, non-numeric data and division by zero separately.

1612
02:41:18,158 --> 02:41:21,604
This way, we could print relevant messages based on the error type.

1613
02:41:22,542 --> 02:41:24,100
Luckily, this is possible.

1614
02:41:25,070 --> 02:41:32,900
To catch and handle these different types of errors separately, we'll use multiple accept blocks and specify the type of error that each block should handle.

1615
02:41:33,838 --> 02:41:38,820
To determine the type of exception we want to catch, we can reproduce the exception in a separate cell.

1616
02:41:41,966 --> 02:41:44,868
The error type is given as the first line of the output.

1617
02:41:45,742 --> 02:41:50,116
When we input a non-numeric value, the exception type is value error.

1618
02:41:51,054 --> 02:41:55,588
So, this is what we must write in the accept statement that handles this exception type.

1619
02:41:56,622 --> 02:42:02,500
Similarly, to determine the exception type associated with division by zero, let's replicate the error.

1620
02:42:03,342 --> 02:42:06,532
We find that the exception type is zero division error.

1621
02:42:07,502 --> 02:42:12,772
Let's add that to the second accept statement and change the message in the block's body to be more informative.

1622
02:42:16,302 --> 02:42:18,116
Great, let's test our code.

1623
02:42:19,054 --> 02:42:24,068
Now, if we enter a non-numeric value, our program gently directs the user to input a number.

1624
02:42:25,038 --> 02:42:30,308
And if we enter zero as a denominator, our program instructs the user to input a non-zero number.

1625
02:42:31,182 --> 02:42:35,652
Finally, if the numerator and denominator are valid, the program calculates the quotient.

1626
02:42:36,526 --> 02:42:38,628
Now that's a much better user experience.

1627
02:42:39,616 --> 02:42:46,816
Awesome. Now let's head to the practice notebook linked in the description to get some experience working with user input and error handling.

1628
02:42:47,056 --> 02:42:56,736
If you want to try the exercises on your own first, pause the video here. Now let's walk through the solution step by step. Alright, let's break down problem one.

1629
02:42:57,216 --> 02:43:06,916
For this problem, we'll write a program that asks the user for a total bill amount and the number of people splitting it, then prints how much each person owes.

1630
02:43:07,406 --> 02:43:14,656
The two things we need to get right are asking for the inputs with clear prompts and casting them to the correct types.

1631
02:43:15,246 --> 02:43:23,876
To collect the bill amount, we call input with the prompt enter the total bill amount and store the result in a bill variable.

1632
02:43:24,398 --> 02:43:36,816
The input function always returns the user's entry as a string, so to do math with it, we wrap it in float and convert it to a number. We do the same for the number of people,

1633
02:43:39,696 --> 02:43:45,136
but this time we use the int function since the number of people is always a whole number.

1634
02:43:45,456 --> 02:43:54,416
After collecting the inputs, to calculate each person's share, we'll divide bill by people and store the result in share.

1635
02:43:55,056 --> 02:44:03,296
To print the result, we use an F string with the colon.2f format specifier, which rounds share to two decimal

1636
02:44:03,456 --> 02:44:04,036
places.

1637
02:44:04,526 --> 02:44:09,136
If you want to learn more about F-strings, check out our video link in the description.

1638
02:44:10,096 --> 02:44:16,576
Running the program with a bill of $60 and 4 people, each person owes $15.

1639
02:44:17,742 --> 02:44:23,376
Now our program works correctly, but only as long as the user enters numbers.

1640
02:44:23,790 --> 02:44:31,204
If someone types ABC instead of a number, the float casting function will raise an error and the program will crash.

1641
02:44:31,662 --> 02:44:42,084
In problem two, we'll add a safety net for that. In this problem, we'll update our program to handle non-numeric inputs gracefully, using a try accept block.

1642
02:44:42,574 --> 02:44:50,976
When we try to convert a non-numeric string to a float, Python raises a value error, so that's the exception type we'll need to catch.

1643
02:44:51,438 --> 02:44:52,656
To do that,

1644
02:44:55,790 --> 02:45:09,456
we indent all four lines from problem one under a try keyword. Then, below the try block, we write accept value error and add a print statement that tells the user to enter valid numbers.

1645
02:45:09,776 --> 02:45:14,736
Running the program with valid inputs, it still calculates the share correctly.

1646
02:45:15,136 --> 02:45:24,496
But now, if we run the program with ABC as the bill, instead of crashing, the program catches the exception and prints please enter valid number.

1647
02:45:24,816 --> 02:45:29,732
That's an improvement, but entering a non-number isn't the only mistake someone could make.

1648
02:45:30,336 --> 02:45:40,336
If someone enters zero for the number of people, the program will attempt to divide by zero, and the value error block won't catch that, because zero is a perfectly valid integer.

1649
02:45:40,656 --> 02:45:43,136
We'll address that issue in problem three.

1650
02:45:45,376 --> 02:45:52,816
Here, we need to handle the two error cases separately so we can give the user a more specific message depending on what's wrong.

1651
02:45:53,056 --> 02:46:05,696
The try block stays exactly the same as in problem two. To catch the division by zero case, we add a second accept clause below the first, this one for zero division error.

1652
02:46:06,656 --> 02:46:11,456
Under it, we print error, the number of people cannot be zero.

1653
02:46:12,110 --> 02:46:21,456
The reason we use two separate blocks is that the two errors mean different things. Value error means the user types something that isn't a number at all.

1654
02:46:21,998 --> 02:46:29,536
Zero division error means the user entered valid numbers, but put zero in a spot that caused a division by zero.

1655
02:46:29,936 --> 02:46:41,124
With separate blocks, we can respond to each mistake in a way that actually helps the user fix it. Running the program with 60 and 4, we get the correct result.

1656
02:46:41,856 --> 02:46:54,724
If we enter ABC as the bill, we get please enter valid numbers. And if we enter 60 and 0, we get error, the number of people cannot be zero.

1657
02:46:55,278 --> 02:46:58,976
Awesome! Those are the solutions to our practice exercises.

1658
02:46:59,456 --> 02:47:08,736
Across these three problems, we've built up a real program step by step, starting with basic user input, adding a safety net for invalid data,

1659
02:47:08,896 --> 02:47:12,976
and then making the error handling specific enough to actually be useful.

1660
02:47:13,840 --> 02:47:23,280
Now with error handling, when the user makes a mistake, they get a helpful message telling them what went wrong. But by the time they get that message, the program has already ended.

1661
02:47:23,520 --> 02:47:27,200
For them to fix their mistake, they have to go back and rerun the code.

1662
02:47:27,440 --> 02:47:34,800
In the next section, we'll learn about a different type of loop that allows the user to keep inputting values until they get the right one.

1663
02:47:35,470 --> 02:47:37,920
Looking to understand wild loops in Python?

1664
02:47:38,160 --> 02:47:41,760
Wild loops are great tools to use when you're looking to repeat a piece of code.

1665
02:47:41,920 --> 02:47:48,868
In this video, you'll learn everything you need to know about wild loops, how they work, when to use them, and how to avoid common mistakes.

1666
02:47:49,966 --> 02:47:53,540
Let's first familiarize ourselves with the syntax of a wild loop.

1667
02:47:54,350 --> 02:47:57,668
We'll start with a simple loop that prints the numbers 1 to 5.

1668
02:47:58,510 --> 02:48:01,440
In Python, a wild loop begins with the keyword while.

1669
02:48:02,478 --> 02:48:07,044
We follow this keyword with a Boolean expression that tells Python whether or not to execute the loop.

1670
02:48:08,014 --> 02:48:10,948
This Boolean expression is also called the wild condition.

1671
02:48:11,918 --> 02:48:17,476
In our example, we'd like the loop to run as long as the count is less than or equal to 5 starting from 1.

1672
02:48:18,478 --> 02:48:22,240
To represent this starting point, we'll create a count variable and set it to

1673
02:48:22,240 --> 02:48:25,380
1. This variable will serve two purposes.

1674
02:48:26,350 --> 02:48:31,236
We'll use the count variable in the wild condition to test whether the count is less than or equal to 5.

1675
02:48:32,174 --> 02:48:34,436
Later, we'll increment it to count to 5.

1676
02:48:35,374 --> 02:48:38,628
Finally, we end the first line of our while loop with a colon.

1677
02:48:39,470 --> 02:48:41,604
This line is called the while loop header.

1678
02:48:42,446 --> 02:48:48,132
Note that when we press enter at the end of the header, our code editor automatically indents the next line of code.

1679
02:48:49,006 --> 02:48:50,820
This is a feature, not a bug.

1680
02:48:51,758 --> 02:48:57,124
Following the header, the lines of code that compose a while loop must be indented by one tab.

1681
02:48:57,966 --> 02:49:01,956
The indented code within the while loop is called the body of the loop.

1682
02:49:02,798 --> 02:49:10,244
The loop body is where we write the operations that should be repeated a number of times and it ends with the last line of indented code.

1683
02:49:11,086 --> 02:49:16,132
In other words, the first line of unindented code that follows is not part of the loop body.

1684
02:49:17,134 --> 02:49:19,460
Awesome. Let's now fill the body of the loop.

1685
02:49:20,462 --> 02:49:26,532
Since our goal is to count from 1 to 5, we'll first print the count variable and then increment it by 1.

1686
02:49:27,502 --> 02:49:30,500
Lastly, we'll print the message to indicate the end of the program.

1687
02:49:31,406 --> 02:49:34,756
And that's it! We have a wild loop that should count from 1 to 5.

1688
02:49:35,598 --> 02:49:37,476
Let's test this by running the code.

1689
02:49:38,446 --> 02:49:41,092
Great! The loop does indeed count from 1 to 5.

1690
02:49:42,062 --> 02:49:47,364
But before we move on, let's make sure we understand what happened behind the scenes when Python executed the loop.

1691
02:49:48,366 --> 02:49:52,516
In the first line of code, Python initializes the count variable and sets it equal to 1.

1692
02:49:53,486 --> 02:49:59,140
Moving to the wild loop header, Python stops to test whether the count variable is less than or equal to 5.

1693
02:50:00,142 --> 02:50:09,060
Since at that point, count equals 1 and 1 is less than 5, the Boolean expression evaluates to true and Python gets the green light to enter the loop body.

1694
02:50:10,062 --> 02:50:16,420
Entering the loop, Python first prints out count equals 1 and then increments the count variable to store the value 2.

1695
02:50:17,262 --> 02:50:23,204
Since we've reached the end of the loop body, Python returns the loop header and again tests whether the condition is true.

1696
02:50:24,046 --> 02:50:30,500
Since 2 is less than 5, the expression evaluates to true, and Python gets the green light to enter the loop body again.

1697
02:50:31,502 --> 02:50:34,564
During this iteration, count equals 2 is printed,

1698
02:50:35,246 --> 02:50:37,636
and the count variable is incremented to 3.

1699
02:50:38,542 --> 02:50:40,964
This process repeats when count equals 3

1700
02:50:43,200 --> 02:50:43,760
4.

1701
02:50:46,382 --> 02:50:47,108
And 5.

1702
02:50:47,950 --> 02:50:52,260
At the end of the fifth iteration, the count variable is incremented to 6.

1703
02:50:53,070 --> 02:51:00,484
When Python returns the loop header and tests the condition, it evaluates to false since count is no longer less than or equal to 5.

1704
02:51:01,454 --> 02:51:04,580
Python gets a red light and is no longer allowed to enter the loop.

1705
02:51:05,550 --> 02:51:10,884
The program then moves to the next unindented line of code and prints the end of the program message.

1706
02:51:11,886 --> 02:51:15,812
Great! Now you know how to write a while loop and understand how they're executed.

1707
02:51:16,814 --> 02:51:19,588
Wild loops are one of two types of loops in Python.

1708
02:51:20,590 --> 02:51:23,556
You may already be familiar with the more common for loop.

1709
02:51:24,590 --> 02:51:29,316
But if not, be sure to check out our for loop video first. The link is in the description.

1710
02:51:30,222 --> 02:51:36,068
Given that Python provides both while loops and for loops, you might be wondering what's the difference between them?

1711
02:51:37,102 --> 02:51:38,436
Are they interchangeable?

1712
02:51:39,438 --> 02:51:42,436
Couldn't we have used a for loop to count from one to five?

1713
02:51:43,246 --> 02:51:48,004
Let's break down the key differences between the two and explore when to use one over the other.

1714
02:51:49,038 --> 02:51:51,840
We could indeed count from one to five using a for loop.

1715
02:51:52,110 --> 02:51:52,772
Here's how.

1716
02:51:53,774 --> 02:51:58,340
We create a for loop with the for keyword, followed by the iteration variable count.

1717
02:51:59,278 --> 02:52:05,988
Then we use the range function to tell Python to generate a sequence of numbers from 1 to 5, which the loop iterates over.

1718
02:52:06,894 --> 02:52:09,348
Lastly, we print the end-of-the-program message.

1719
02:52:10,350 --> 02:52:14,788
As you can see, the for loop produces the same output as the while loop with less code.

1720
02:52:15,758 --> 02:52:20,420
This is generally true for tasks where we know the number of iterations before the loop starts running.

1721
02:52:21,390 --> 02:52:24,356
When we have a task like this, for loops are the better choice.

1722
02:52:25,294 --> 02:52:30,756
However, a limitation of for loops is that we must know the number of iterations in advance to use them.

1723
02:52:31,758 --> 02:52:37,220
In practice, there are plenty of scenarios where we don't know the number of times a loop should run before it starts running.

1724
02:52:38,062 --> 02:52:39,716
This is where wild loops shine.

1725
02:52:40,558 --> 02:52:45,508
For example, imagine you are coding a login system that asks the user to input their password.

1726
02:52:46,542 --> 02:52:53,188
Since the user might enter the wrong password multiple times, you should keep prompting them for their password until they enter the correct one.

1727
02:52:54,158 --> 02:52:58,948
The user might get their password right the first time, or it might take two attempts or ten attempts.

1728
02:52:59,758 --> 02:53:07,076
The point is we don't know in advance the exact number of attempts the user will need before entering the correct password, secret123.

1729
02:53:08,078 --> 02:53:10,756
For that reason, while loops are the right tool for the job.

1730
02:53:11,630 --> 02:53:16,708
Let's write a simple program to see how we can use a while loop to simulate a login system.

1731
02:53:17,550 --> 02:53:22,500
First, we'll create a variable to store the user's input and set it equal to a placeholder value.

1732
02:53:23,534 --> 02:53:29,668
Then we'll write the while loop header, starting with the while loop keyword, followed by the while condition.

1733
02:53:30,574 --> 02:53:38,052
Since we'd like the loop to keep running as long as the user has not entered the correct password, our while loop condition will test whether the user input

1734
02:53:38,990 --> 02:53:40,132
is not equal

1735
02:53:40,846 --> 02:53:43,012
to the correct password secret123.

1736
02:53:44,014 --> 02:53:48,452
In the body of the loop, we'll ask the user to input their password using the input function.

1737
02:53:49,358 --> 02:53:54,180
The input function pauses the execution of the code and prompts the user for input.

1738
02:53:55,214 --> 02:54:00,804
By the way, if you want to learn more about user input in Python, make sure to watch our video linked in the description.

1739
02:54:01,710 --> 02:54:06,320
Finally, once the user inputs the correct password, the loop stops running.

1740
02:54:06,480 --> 02:54:08,580
Let's print access granted.

1741
02:54:09,422 --> 02:54:14,468
When we run the code, we are prompted with an input box asking us to input our password.

1742
02:54:15,438 --> 02:54:21,348
If we enter the correct password on the first try, the loop stops running immediately and we are granted access.

1743
02:54:22,382 --> 02:54:24,996
But if we get our password wrong a few times,

1744
02:54:25,710 --> 02:54:28,420
the loop will keep prompting us until we get it right.

1745
02:54:29,262 --> 02:54:35,716
When taking user input, while loops gives us the flexibility we need since we can't always predict what users will enter.

1746
02:54:36,686 --> 02:54:42,884
Great! Now you understand when a while loop should be used instead of a for loop and how to use a while loop to take user input.

1747
02:54:43,822 --> 02:54:46,372
But our current approach could be slightly improved.

1748
02:54:47,374 --> 02:54:52,772
Right now we test whether user input is correct before we even prompt the user for their password.

1749
02:54:53,646 --> 02:54:55,396
But this is a little backwards.

1750
02:54:56,366 --> 02:55:02,020
It forces us to assign user input a placeholder value before the while loop condition can be evaluated.

1751
02:55:02,862 --> 02:55:04,676
Luckily, there's a way to fix this.

1752
02:55:05,582 --> 02:55:15,172
Instead of creating the user input variable outside the loop and setting it equal to the placeholder value, we can remove this line of code and place the while loop condition with true.

1753
02:55:16,174 --> 02:55:21,732
Now that we remove the wild condition, we need another way for the loop to exit once the correct password is entered.

1754
02:55:22,574 --> 02:55:28,740
To do this, we'll add the conditional block inside the loop to check whether the user input is equal to the correct password.

1755
02:55:29,774 --> 02:55:32,452
Inside this conditional, we'll write the break keyword.

1756
02:55:33,422 --> 02:55:39,588
The break keyword tells Python to stop executing the loop immediately and move on to the next line of code after the loop.

1757
02:55:40,526 --> 02:55:45,348
Nice, now we'll prompt the user for the password before testing whether they enter the correct one.

1758
02:55:46,190 --> 02:55:48,708
This code should work the same as the first version.

1759
02:55:49,678 --> 02:55:51,492
Let's run the cells to confirm this.

1760
02:55:52,238 --> 02:55:58,020
If we enter secret123 as the first attempt, the loop exits and access granted is printed.

1761
02:55:58,862 --> 02:56:04,100
But if we enter few incorrect passwords, we are continuously prompted until we enter the right one.

1762
02:56:05,070 --> 02:56:15,268
Awesome! The while true syntax is great for interactive programs like this, but if you forget to include a break statement, your loop will run forever, creating what's called an infinite loop.

1763
02:56:16,110 --> 02:56:19,844
To see how this works, let's comment out the conditional block and rerun the code.

1764
02:56:20,846 --> 02:56:22,980
We are then prompted to enter a password,

1765
02:56:26,478 --> 02:56:27,780
and another password,

1766
02:56:29,072 --> 02:56:30,852
even if we enter the correct one.

1767
02:56:31,854 --> 02:56:37,220
The program will ask us for passwords forever since there's no value we can input to break out of the loop.

1768
02:56:38,222 --> 02:56:45,956
That's why it's important to always make sure there's a clear way for the while loop to exit, whether that's with while condition or with a break keyword.

1769
02:56:46,926 --> 02:56:47,312
Awesome!

1770
02:56:47,598 --> 02:56:50,468
Let's quick recap what we learned about while loops in Python.

1771
02:56:51,438 --> 02:56:54,820
First, we covered the syntax and execution of a while loop.

1772
02:56:55,822 --> 02:56:59,844
We saw that the wild loop is made of a header and an indented body.

1773
02:57:00,814 --> 02:57:04,292
The loop will run as long as the condition in the header remains true.

1774
02:57:05,294 --> 02:57:07,620
Next, we compare while loops to for loops.

1775
02:57:08,622 --> 02:57:12,292
We learn that for loops are great when we know how many times we need to iterate.

1776
02:57:13,166 --> 02:57:17,796
But while loops are best when we don't know in advance how many times a loop should run.

1777
02:57:18,702 --> 02:57:23,428
Finally, we explored the while true syntax, which helps simplify certain loops.

1778
02:57:24,270 --> 02:57:30,148
We learned that we need to check for an end value that includes a break statement, otherwise the loop will run forever.

1779
02:57:31,312 --> 02:57:38,352
Now let's head to the practice notebook linked in the description and write a few while loops. If you want to practice on your own, pause the video here.

1780
02:57:38,592 --> 02:57:42,052
In the next section, we'll walk through the solutions together step by step.

1781
02:57:42,574 --> 02:57:44,260
Alright, let's jump into problem one.

1782
02:57:45,102 --> 02:57:47,364
In the first problem, we have three scenarios.

1783
02:57:48,206 --> 02:57:54,180
For each scenario, we need to decide whether a for loop or a while loop is a better fit, and then explain why.

1784
02:57:55,022 --> 02:58:00,484
The first scenario describes asking a user for their phone number until they enter it in the correct format.

1785
02:58:01,326 --> 02:58:07,428
Since we don't know how many attempts it'll take for the user to input a valid phone number, we should use a while loop.

1786
02:58:08,270 --> 02:58:18,884
In the second scenario, the task is to print each name in a class roster list. In this case, we have all the names in a list before we need to print them, so it's best to use a for loop.

1787
02:58:19,694 --> 02:58:22,992
The third scenario describes attempting to connect to the internet.

1788
02:58:23,854 --> 02:58:29,924
Like the first scenario, we don't know ahead of time how many attempts will be required, so a while loop would be the best fit here.

1789
02:58:30,766 --> 02:58:33,828
When we compare these answers, we can see a clear rule of thumb.

1790
02:58:34,670 --> 02:58:39,044
If the number of iterations is known ahead of time, the for loop is usually a better fit.

1791
02:58:39,854 --> 02:58:45,028
But if the number of iterations is unknown or variable, a while loop is usually the better fit.

1792
02:58:45,870 --> 02:58:49,860
Great, that gives us a quick decision framework we can reuse in real projects.

1793
02:58:50,702 --> 02:58:52,352
Alright, let's move on to problem two.

1794
02:58:53,198 --> 02:58:55,876
In this problem, we're writing a simple login check.

1795
02:58:56,718 --> 02:59:02,468
We need to keep asking for a password until the user types the correct value, which is sunset42.

1796
02:59:03,310 --> 02:59:04,112
To start,

1797
02:59:05,166 --> 02:59:07,632
we create the correct password variable.

1798
02:59:09,600 --> 02:59:11,300
And the user input variable.

1799
02:59:12,110 --> 02:59:17,796
We set this variable equal to a placeholder so our loop has something to compare to before the first prompt.

1800
02:59:18,638 --> 02:59:26,372
After creating those variables, we write a while loop that runs as long as user input is not equal to the correct password.

1801
02:59:27,182 --> 02:59:30,244
Inside the loop, we prompt the user to enter a password.

1802
02:59:31,790 --> 02:59:35,844
After the user enters a value, we check whether the password is still incorrect.

1803
02:59:36,686 --> 02:59:40,388
If it is still incorrect, we print incorrect password try again.

1804
02:59:41,486 --> 02:59:47,716
But if the user enters Sunset42, the loop condition fails on the next check and the loop ends.

1805
02:59:48,558 --> 02:59:50,788
After the loop, we print access granted.

1806
02:59:54,094 --> 02:59:57,444
When we run this code, wrong passwords keep the loop going.

1807
02:59:58,286 --> 03:00:02,308
But when the correct password is entered, the loop exits and access is granted.

1808
03:00:03,150 --> 03:00:07,908
Awesome! Asking for user input is one of the most common real-world uses for while loops.

1809
03:00:08,718 --> 03:00:15,300
In problem 3, we'll refactor our solution from problem 2 to use the while true syntax and break keyword.

1810
03:00:16,110 --> 03:00:17,860
We'll start with our code from problem 2.

1811
03:00:20,430 --> 03:00:27,428
Instead of defining a placeholder variable and writing the stopping condition in the while header, we simply write while true.

1812
03:00:28,270 --> 03:00:32,292
Next, to avoid creating an infinite loop, we need to add an exit condition.

1813
03:00:33,134 --> 03:00:35,908
To do this, we can simply add an else to our conditional.

1814
03:00:36,750 --> 03:00:41,700
The code inside this else block will only be run if the user inputs the correct password.

1815
03:00:42,510 --> 03:00:46,020
In this case, we print access granted and break out of the while loop.

1816
03:00:46,830 --> 03:00:50,244
When the break keyword is run, the loop exits immediately.

1817
03:00:51,086 --> 03:00:55,076
To confirm this, let's change the final print statement to say end of program.

1818
03:00:55,918 --> 03:01:00,132
When we run this version of the code, the behavior is the same from the user's perspective.

1819
03:01:00,974 --> 03:01:05,508
Wrong attempts keep repeating, and the correct password ends the loop right away.

1820
03:01:06,350 --> 03:01:12,320
Great! Now you're familiar with both common styles for while loops and understand when it's best to use a while loop instead of a for

1821
03:01:12,480 --> 03:01:17,284
loop. Now we can write a loop that keeps going until a condition tells it to stop.

1822
03:01:17,600 --> 03:01:24,800
But if we want to reuse this loop or any other code we've written so far, we'd need to copy and paste it somewhere else in our program.

1823
03:01:25,120 --> 03:01:32,388
In the next section, we'll learn how to write a block of code one time and then reuse it anywhere in our code without copying and pasting.

1824
03:01:33,200 --> 03:01:35,588
Want to create your own Python functions?

1825
03:01:36,000 --> 03:01:39,684
Writing good functions requires more than just knowing the syntax.

1826
03:01:40,206 --> 03:01:43,556
You need to truly understand how they work under the hood.

1827
03:01:44,110 --> 03:01:50,596
In this video, we'll show you how to write functions and explore exactly how Python executes them behind the scenes.

1828
03:01:51,918 --> 03:01:58,404
To get familiar with the syntax, let's define a simple function that calculates a rectangle's area and displays the result.

1829
03:01:59,438 --> 03:02:04,160
In Python, we use the keyword def to define a new function.

1830
03:02:05,038 --> 03:02:07,680
We follow this keyword with a name for our function.

1831
03:02:08,750 --> 03:02:13,840
When naming a function, it is best to choose something descriptive that reflects the function's purpose.

1832
03:02:14,766 --> 03:02:20,160
Since the purpose of our function is to calculate the area of a rectangle, let's name it calc rectarea.

1833
03:02:21,440 --> 03:02:24,480
Next, we follow the function's name with parentheses.

1834
03:02:25,518 --> 03:02:30,240
Inside these parentheses, we specify the inputs our function needs to perform its task.

1835
03:02:31,310 --> 03:02:40,164
Since calculating a rectangle's area requires its length and width, we write the variable names length and width as inputs, separated by a comma.

1836
03:02:41,070 --> 03:02:43,556
Finally, we end the line with a colon.

1837
03:02:44,480 --> 03:02:50,212
This first line, which specifies the function's name and inputs, is known as the function's header.

1838
03:02:51,182 --> 03:02:56,292
After pressing enter at the end of the header, the code editor automatically indents the line.

1839
03:02:57,134 --> 03:02:59,236
This is a feature, not a bug.

1840
03:03:00,174 --> 03:03:04,740
After the header, the lines of code in a function must be indented by one tab.

1841
03:03:05,742 --> 03:03:09,060
The indented code within a function is called the function's body.

1842
03:03:09,934 --> 03:03:16,480
The function's body contains the code that does the heavy lifting, running calculations, and other steps to get the result we want.

1843
03:03:17,518 --> 03:03:22,884
Any unindented code that appears after the function's body is not considered part of the function.

1844
03:03:23,886 --> 03:03:24,512
Awesome!

1845
03:03:24,752 --> 03:03:29,764
Let's now fill in the body of the function to calculate the area of a rectangle using the inputs.

1846
03:03:30,766 --> 03:03:37,444
To calculate the area of a rectangle, we'll multiply length by width and store the result in a variable called area.

1847
03:03:38,414 --> 03:03:42,788
Finally, after calculating the area, we display the result with a print statement.

1848
03:03:43,886 --> 03:03:50,084
Awesome! Now that we've defined our calculate rectangle area function, we can use it in our program.

1849
03:03:50,958 --> 03:03:55,524
To use the function, we write its name, followed by a pair of parentheses.

1850
03:03:56,526 --> 03:03:59,908
Inside the parentheses, we write the inputs to our function.

1851
03:04:00,752 --> 03:04:08,843
For example, to calculate the area of a rectangle with a length of 14 and a width of 10, we write 14 comma 10.

1852
03:04:09,966 --> 03:04:10,432
Great!

1853
03:04:10,734 --> 03:04:13,636
Now you know how to define and use your own functions.

1854
03:04:14,574 --> 03:04:18,308
Running the cell, we get 140 in the output box.

1855
03:04:19,310 --> 03:04:20,452
Our code works.

1856
03:04:21,518 --> 03:04:29,444
But to really understand how Python executes functions, let's trace through the execution of the code line by line.

1857
03:04:30,382 --> 03:04:34,404
When Python executes the code, it first runs our functions header.

1858
03:04:35,406 --> 03:04:41,732
Here, Python memorizes the name of the function, its inputs, and the code in the function's body.

1859
03:04:42,766 --> 03:04:48,516
To emphasize, Python is not calculating the area of any rectangles at this point.

1860
03:04:49,518 --> 03:04:53,924
Python only memorizes the definition of the function so it can be used later.

1861
03:04:54,926 --> 03:05:00,452
The actual execution of the calculate rectangle area function happens on the next line.

1862
03:05:01,486 --> 03:05:10,660
When Python reaches this line, it recognizes that we want to use the calculate rectangle area function with the values 14 and 10 as inputs.

1863
03:05:11,694 --> 03:05:20,356
In programming, we say that this line of code calls the function calculateRectangleArea, passing 14 and 10 as arguments.

1864
03:05:21,262 --> 03:05:24,516
A function call triggers the execution of its code.

1865
03:05:25,486 --> 03:05:31,460
The execution begins by assigning the input values to the variables listed in the function's header.

1866
03:05:32,462 --> 03:05:38,468
For example, this function call assigns 14 to the variable length and 10 to the variable width.

1867
03:05:39,470 --> 03:05:45,188
In programming, the variable names specified in the function's header are called parameters.

1868
03:05:46,318 --> 03:05:51,748
Once the arguments have been assigned to the parameters, Python executes the function's body.

1869
03:05:52,750 --> 03:06:00,612
On the first line, Python calculates the area by multiplying 14 by 10 and assigns the result to the variable area.

1870
03:06:01,582 --> 03:06:05,956
Finally, Python prints the area of the rectangle, 140.

1871
03:06:07,022 --> 03:06:07,312
Great!

1872
03:06:07,502 --> 03:06:10,436
You now understand how Python executes functions.

1873
03:06:11,374 --> 03:06:14,756
As you can see, writing a Python function is straightforward.

1874
03:06:15,726 --> 03:06:20,900
But to write functions that really add value, it's important to follow some best practices.

1875
03:06:21,870 --> 03:06:26,596
One of those best practices is called the single responsibility principle.

1876
03:06:27,534 --> 03:06:35,716
As the name suggests, the single responsibility principle states that each function should have exactly one job or responsibility.

1877
03:06:36,750 --> 03:06:44,112
That's because, when a function tries to do too many things at once, it becomes hard to understand, maintain, and reuse.

1878
03:06:44,878 --> 03:06:49,764
Additionally, each function should have a name that clearly describes what it does.

1879
03:06:50,766 --> 03:06:57,476
In our example, the function name calculateRectangleArea suggests it should only calculate the area.

1880
03:06:58,414 --> 03:07:03,172
It's not immediately clear from the name of the function that it also prints the area.

1881
03:07:04,174 --> 03:07:09,312
To keep our function focused on calculation, let's move the print statement to the main program.

1882
03:07:10,350 --> 03:07:13,924
This way, our function only calculates the area

1883
03:07:14,832 --> 03:07:18,112
and we can control how to print the results in the main program.

1884
03:07:19,214 --> 03:07:23,300
For example, the main program can print the area along with a unit.

1885
03:07:24,334 --> 03:07:28,484
This is a better way to structure our program, but there's a problem.

1886
03:07:29,454 --> 03:07:32,964
If we run the cell, we get an error at the print statement.

1887
03:07:33,582 --> 03:07:36,676
Python says the variable area is not defined.

1888
03:07:37,678 --> 03:07:42,372
But we did create area inside the function, so why can't we use it?

1889
03:07:43,406 --> 03:07:52,068
To answer this question, we need to understand how Python uses something called namespaces to track variables during a program's execution.

1890
03:07:53,006 --> 03:08:00,708
A namespace is like a notebook where Python writes down variable names and their current values during execution.

1891
03:08:01,742 --> 03:08:08,356
When Python runs a line of code like unit equals feet squared, it doesn't just randomly store this information.

1892
03:08:08,608 --> 03:08:11,332
It carefully organizes this data in memory.

1893
03:08:12,334 --> 03:08:21,892
In Python's organization system, variables created outside of any function, like our unit variable, get placed in what is called a global namespace.

1894
03:08:22,766 --> 03:08:26,916
Variables living in the global namespace are called global variables.

1895
03:08:27,950 --> 03:08:31,808
But the global namespace doesn't just store variables.

1896
03:08:32,206 --> 03:08:36,048
It also stores the names of any function we define in our main program.

1897
03:08:37,102 --> 03:08:45,968
For example, during the execution of the next line, Python stores the name of the calculate rectangle area function and its code in the global

1898
03:08:46,128 --> 03:08:46,852
namespace.

1899
03:08:47,886 --> 03:08:53,252
But the global namespace is not the only place where Python can store names and values.

1900
03:08:54,222 --> 03:09:01,572
In particular, during function calls, Python creates a new temporary namespace called a local namespace.

1901
03:09:02,574 --> 03:09:08,836
Python uses this namespace to store the function's parameters and any variables defined inside it.

1902
03:09:09,806 --> 03:09:18,368
For example, when Python calls the calculate rectangleArea function, it assigns the arguments 14, and 10 to the parameters

1903
03:09:18,574 --> 03:09:20,932
length and width in the local namespace.

1904
03:09:21,838 --> 03:09:28,964
And when Python executes the body of the function, the variable area is also stored in the local namespace.

1905
03:09:29,902 --> 03:09:36,548
Variables living in the local namespace, like length, width, and area are called local variables.

1906
03:09:37,486 --> 03:09:41,540
Now, here's the key to understanding our previous error message.

1907
03:09:41,966 --> 03:09:49,124
When a function finishes executing and returns to the calling point, its local namespace is always destroyed.

1908
03:09:50,030 --> 03:09:58,052
This means that the variables stored in the local namespace are no longer available after the program returns to the calling point.

1909
03:09:59,086 --> 03:10:06,368
That's why, when we tried to print the area variable outside the function, we got an error saying that area doesn't exist.

1910
03:10:07,406 --> 03:10:11,088
Unlike global variables, local variables are temporary.

1911
03:10:11,374 --> 03:10:13,476
They only exist during a function call.

1912
03:10:14,702 --> 03:10:15,328
Awesome!

1913
03:10:15,726 --> 03:10:19,492
Now that we understand what went wrong, let's see how to fix this.

1914
03:10:20,462 --> 03:10:28,848
To display the calculated area while following the single responsibility principle, we need to return the area back to the main program

1915
03:10:29,168 --> 03:10:31,748
before the local namespace is destroyed.

1916
03:10:32,654 --> 03:10:35,684
We can do this by using a return statement.

1917
03:10:36,718 --> 03:10:44,208
A return statement starts with the keyword return, followed by the variable whose value we'd like to return to the main program.

1918
03:10:45,326 --> 03:10:48,836
In our example, we want to pass back the value of area.

1919
03:10:49,870 --> 03:10:51,968
During the execution of our program,

1920
03:10:54,222 --> 03:11:02,724
when Python executes the return statement, the value of area is returned back to the calling point before the local namespace is discarded.

1921
03:11:03,662 --> 03:11:10,436
In practice, this means that the function call expression evaluates the return value, 140.

1922
03:11:11,438 --> 03:11:16,452
To keep using the return value in the rest of our program, we need to capture it in a variable.

1923
03:11:16,750 --> 03:11:20,804
Otherwise, the value will be lost once the program continues to the next line.

1924
03:11:21,646 --> 03:11:27,728
For example, we can capture 140 by assigning it to a new variable called area.

1925
03:11:28,782 --> 03:11:32,900
This creates a global variable containing 140.

1926
03:11:33,870 --> 03:11:36,420
Running the cell, we no longer get an error.

1927
03:11:37,422 --> 03:11:41,284
Note that we could capture the return value using any variable name.

1928
03:11:41,552 --> 03:11:45,604
It doesn't need to match the name of the local variable returned by the function.

1929
03:11:46,638 --> 03:11:55,300
By adding a return statement, our program can finally display the area of the rectangle while following the single responsibility principle.

1930
03:11:56,334 --> 03:12:01,220
But to do this, we had to work around the short lifespan of the local namespace.

1931
03:12:02,190 --> 03:12:08,548
If local namespaces make it complicated to access variables, why does Python bother creating them?

1932
03:12:09,550 --> 03:12:14,852
Wouldn't it be simpler if Python stored all names and values in the global namespace?

1933
03:12:15,790 --> 03:12:18,436
At first glance, this might seem better.

1934
03:12:19,438 --> 03:12:27,076
But using a single namespace could quickly lead to naming conflicts and unpredictable behavior as a program gets more complicated.

1935
03:12:28,078 --> 03:12:33,284
To help us see this issue, let's create another area variable at the top of our program.

1936
03:12:34,254 --> 03:12:39,300
Now let's trace the code, assuming Python stores everything in the global namespace.

1937
03:12:40,206 --> 03:12:46,404
Like before, the first two variable assignments and the function definition are stored in the global namespace.

1938
03:12:47,406 --> 03:12:52,996
We first see the problem with using a single namespace when Python executes the function call.

1939
03:12:54,030 --> 03:12:59,940
Without a local namespace, Python must put the parameters and their arguments in the global namespace.

1940
03:13:00,878 --> 03:13:05,380
Since the length and width parameters don't already exist there, this works fine.

1941
03:13:06,352 --> 03:13:11,300
But when Python executes the first line of the body, there's an issue.

1942
03:13:12,270 --> 03:13:20,272
Since the area variable already exists in the namespace, Python would replace the original value, 200, with the new value,

1943
03:13:20,512 --> 03:13:21,444
140.

1944
03:13:22,606 --> 03:13:24,068
But this is bad.

1945
03:13:24,526 --> 03:13:28,036
The function now violates the single responsibility principle.

1946
03:13:29,038 --> 03:13:35,268
In addition to calculating the area, it also changes the value of a variable defined elsewhere in the program.

1947
03:13:36,238 --> 03:13:41,412
If Python worked this way, any function call could have unintended consequences.

1948
03:13:42,574 --> 03:13:47,556
This is why, when a function is called, Python creates a local namespace.

1949
03:13:48,526 --> 03:13:57,860
This way, when the first line of the function is executed, Python creates a new local area variable without modifying the global area variable.

1950
03:13:58,926 --> 03:14:02,020
Finally, Python moves to the return statement.

1951
03:14:03,150 --> 03:14:07,300
But at this point, there are two area variables with different values.

1952
03:14:08,270 --> 03:14:11,236
Which area variable will Python choose to return?

1953
03:14:12,238 --> 03:14:13,796
It's not immediately clear.

1954
03:14:14,830 --> 03:14:19,684
By default, Python uses the local version of the area variable if it exists.

1955
03:14:20,654 --> 03:14:24,292
This is the case here, so Python returns 140.

1956
03:14:25,358 --> 03:14:33,668
But note that if a local area variable did not exist, Python would return the value of the global area variable, 200.

1957
03:14:34,670 --> 03:14:36,644
If we print the value of area

1958
03:14:37,358 --> 03:14:38,468
and run the code,

1959
03:14:39,566 --> 03:14:45,604
we see that the calculate rectangle area function call does not change the original area value.

1960
03:14:46,638 --> 03:14:52,420
The creators of Python designed local namespaces to help us write functions that serve a single purpose.

1961
03:14:53,358 --> 03:14:53,872
Great!

1962
03:14:54,382 --> 03:14:58,724
You now have a comprehensive understanding of how functions work in Python.

1963
03:14:59,552 --> 03:15:06,276
Now let's head to the practice notebook linked in the description where we'll get some hands-on practice creating and using our own functions.

1964
03:15:06,592 --> 03:15:10,660
In the next section, we'll walk through the solutions to these exercises step by step.

1965
03:15:11,310 --> 03:15:12,996
Let's get started with problem one.

1966
03:15:13,998 --> 03:15:19,684
In this problem, we'll explore what happens when a function's return value isn't stored in a variable.

1967
03:15:20,750 --> 03:15:24,964
Before we dive into the solution, pause the video if you want to try this on your own.

1968
03:15:26,990 --> 03:15:31,492
Okay, let's trace the code step by step to try and figure out its output.

1969
03:15:32,334 --> 03:15:38,980
To help with that, we'll use namespace graphics throughout this video to visualize how variables and functions are stored.

1970
03:15:39,822 --> 03:15:45,412
If you're not familiar with how namespaces work in Python, check out our functions video linked in the description.

1971
03:15:46,254 --> 03:15:51,172
When the code is executed, Python first reads the greet function definition.

1972
03:15:52,174 --> 03:16:00,068
Here, Python memorizes the function's name, parameter, and body and stores all of that in the global namespace.

1973
03:16:01,230 --> 03:16:05,188
Now that the function is defined, Python can use it throughout the program.

1974
03:16:06,158 --> 03:16:10,756
On the next line, Python calls the greet function with the argument Alice.

1975
03:16:11,694 --> 03:16:17,440
This creates a local namespace where Python stores the name parameter and its argument Alice.

1976
03:16:18,542 --> 03:16:24,804
In the body of the function, Python builds the return string by concatenating hello with the value of name.

1977
03:16:25,646 --> 03:16:33,316
When the function returns, the string hello Alice is passed back to the calling point, and the local namespace is destroyed.

1978
03:16:34,446 --> 03:16:39,684
At this point, the function call expression evaluates the return value hello Alice.

1979
03:16:40,654 --> 03:16:45,156
Hello Alice is then assigned to the greeting variable in the global namespace.

1980
03:16:46,190 --> 03:16:50,084
When Python moves to the next line, it calls the greet function again.

1981
03:16:51,118 --> 03:16:56,484
A new local namespace is created with the name parameter, which is assigned the value Bob.

1982
03:16:57,454 --> 03:17:04,516
In the function's body, Python again creates the return string by concatenating hello with the value of the name variable.

1983
03:17:05,518 --> 03:17:12,452
When the function returns, the string helloBob is returned to the calling point and the local namespace is destroyed.

1984
03:17:13,294 --> 03:17:18,436
At this point, the function call expression evaluates the return value, hello Bob.

1985
03:17:19,534 --> 03:17:23,972
But in this second call, we didn't assign the return value to a variable.

1986
03:17:24,880 --> 03:17:30,404
So, when Python moves to the final line of the program, we lose access to hello Bob.

1987
03:17:31,406 --> 03:17:36,036
On the final line, Python prints the value of the greeting variable, hello Alice.

1988
03:17:36,880 --> 03:17:42,052
This problem shows why a return value is lost when it is not assigned to a variable.

1989
03:17:42,990 --> 03:17:48,132
If you want to use a return value later in your program, make sure to capture it in a variable.

1990
03:17:49,006 --> 03:17:51,396
Awesome! Let's move on to problem two.

1991
03:17:52,270 --> 03:17:59,524
In this problem, we'll see how a return value from one function call can be reused as an input in another function call.

1992
03:18:00,782 --> 03:18:03,364
Pause the video now if you'd like to try it yourself.

1993
03:18:05,294 --> 03:18:08,292
Alright, let's walk through the code to understand what's happening.

1994
03:18:09,326 --> 03:18:17,060
Just like before, Python begins the execution by storing the add function's name, parameters, and body in the global namespace.

1995
03:18:18,030 --> 03:18:28,868
On the next line, Python calls the add function, creates a local namespace, and assigns the arguments 5 and 10 to the parameters A and B in the local namespace.

1996
03:18:29,806 --> 03:18:34,468
During the function call, Python calculates a sum of A and B to get 15.

1997
03:18:35,470 --> 03:18:43,204
When the function returns, the function call expression evaluates to the return value, 15, and the local namespace is destroyed.

1998
03:18:44,046 --> 03:18:48,196
15 is then assigned to the result variable in the global namespace.

1999
03:18:49,134 --> 03:18:52,324
On the next line of code, Python prints the result, 15.

2000
03:18:53,294 --> 03:18:56,228
On the last line, we have nested function calls.

2001
03:18:57,262 --> 03:19:02,436
Python handles this by first calling the add function, which creates a new local namespace.

2002
03:19:03,406 --> 03:19:10,500
This time, the value of result, 15, is assigned to the parameter A and 20 is assigned to the parameter B.

2003
03:19:11,342 --> 03:19:15,812
Inside the function, Python adds 15 and 20 to get 35.

2004
03:19:16,654 --> 03:19:23,524
When the return statement is executed, 35 is passed back to the calling point and the local namespace is destroyed.

2005
03:19:24,366 --> 03:19:32,548
Since this function call is inside the print function, its return value, 35, is passed into the print function as an argument.

2006
03:19:33,614 --> 03:19:37,604
The print function then displays 35 in the output box.

2007
03:19:38,574 --> 03:19:45,892
This problem shows how a function's return value can be reused as an input in another function call to perform further calculations.

2008
03:19:46,734 --> 03:19:48,996
Great, let's move on to problem 3.

2009
03:19:49,870 --> 03:19:57,700
In this problem, we'll explore how a function can use multiple return statements to output different values depending on specific conditions.

2010
03:19:58,574 --> 03:20:01,924
Feel free to pause the video if you want to try and solve it yourself.

2011
03:20:03,918 --> 03:20:06,308
Okay, let's start tracing the code from the top.

2012
03:20:07,310 --> 03:20:15,780
During execution, Python first memorizes the compute function's name, parameters, and body and stores them in the global namespace.

2013
03:20:16,750 --> 03:20:20,996
On the next line, Python executes the first call to the compute function.

2014
03:20:21,966 --> 03:20:28,100
This creates a local namespace where the arguments 3 and 2 are assigned to the parameters A and B.

2015
03:20:29,070 --> 03:20:33,284
Inside the function, Python calculates the product of A and B to get 6.

2016
03:20:34,222 --> 03:20:37,988
6 is then assigned to the result variable in the local namespace.

2017
03:20:38,958 --> 03:20:43,460
On the next line, Python tests whether the value of result is greater than 10.

2018
03:20:44,462 --> 03:20:52,036
Since 6 is not greater than 10, Python skips the body of the if statement and moves to the final line of the function.

2019
03:20:52,878 --> 03:20:57,252
On this line, Python adds 4 to our result variable to get 10.

2020
03:20:58,254 --> 03:21:04,388
When the function returns, Python passes 10 back to the calling point and the local namespace is destroyed.

2021
03:21:05,390 --> 03:21:10,628
10 is then passed directly into the print function and displayed in the output box.

2022
03:21:11,598 --> 03:21:15,652
On the last line of the program, Python calls the compute function again.

2023
03:21:16,654 --> 03:21:22,948
This creates a new local namespace where the arguments 5 and 3 are assigned to the parameters A and B.

2024
03:21:23,918 --> 03:21:28,516
Inside the function, Python calculates the product of A and B to get 15.

2025
03:21:29,358 --> 03:21:33,124
15 is then assigned to the result variable in the local namespace.

2026
03:21:34,062 --> 03:21:38,244
On the next line, Python tests whether the value of result is greater than 10.

2027
03:21:39,086 --> 03:21:44,228
Since 15 is greater than 10, Python moves into the body of the if statement.

2028
03:21:45,166 --> 03:21:49,380
Here, Python subtracts 3 from our result variable to get 12.

2029
03:21:50,222 --> 03:21:54,500
12 is then returned to the calling point as the local namespace is destroyed.

2030
03:21:55,502 --> 03:22:02,116
It is important to note that, in this case, Python does not execute the final return statement in the function.

2031
03:22:03,086 --> 03:22:07,236
As soon as the return statement is executed, Python exits the function.

2032
03:22:08,142 --> 03:22:12,612
Finally, Python prints the return value of the second function call 12.

2033
03:22:13,582 --> 03:22:20,964
This problem shows how you can combine conditionals with multiple return statements to pass back different values based on conditions.

2034
03:22:22,016 --> 03:22:27,012
Awesome! Now we know how to write our own custom functions and use them anywhere in our code.

2035
03:22:27,376 --> 03:22:38,416
But some of the jobs we'll want to write functions for come up over and over again in programming. Because these types of jobs are so common, someone else has already written the functions for them.

2036
03:22:38,656 --> 03:22:43,236
In the next section, we'll see how to use other people's code in our own programs.

2037
03:22:44,416 --> 03:22:51,588
By default, Python only gives you a small set of built-in tools like SUM, LEN, and Print.

2038
03:22:52,016 --> 03:22:55,652
These tools are useful, but they only take you so far.

2039
03:22:56,750 --> 03:23:02,884
They won't let you draw a chart, analyze a huge spreadsheet, or pull live data from the web.

2040
03:23:04,016 --> 03:23:09,156
And yet, Python is famous for powering through these tasks easily.

2041
03:23:10,606 --> 03:23:12,484
So where does that power come from?

2042
03:23:13,742 --> 03:23:17,316
Much of Python's power comes from the work of programmers like yourself.

2043
03:23:18,446 --> 03:23:22,820
Over the years, thousands of them have written and shared code with the community.

2044
03:23:23,694 --> 03:23:30,212
To make their functions easy to share, programmers have bundled them into collections, called Python libraries.

2045
03:23:31,054 --> 03:23:33,412
The name library is quite appropriate.

2046
03:23:34,254 --> 03:23:41,028
Just like a physical library is filled with books organized into sections like nonfiction, mystery, and history,

2047
03:23:42,190 --> 03:23:46,148
a Python library is filled with functions organized into modules.

2048
03:23:47,342 --> 03:23:52,996
For example, consider the standard library, one of the most important libraries in Python.

2049
03:23:53,838 --> 03:24:02,404
The library is divided into more than 200 modules, including classics like the random, math, and statistics modules.

2050
03:24:03,246 --> 03:24:06,852
In turn, each of these modules holds many functions.

2051
03:24:07,662 --> 03:24:15,524
For example, the random module contains the randint function, which picks a random integer between two numbers.

2052
03:24:16,336 --> 03:24:21,060
And randint is just one of the random module's many functions.

2053
03:24:22,030 --> 03:24:27,076
There's also choice, which picks a random item from a list, and so on.

2054
03:24:27,918 --> 03:24:36,496
Beyond the random module, the math module has functions to find a number's square root, raise a number to a power,

2055
03:24:36,736 --> 03:24:37,376
and more.

2056
03:24:38,254 --> 03:24:42,212
In total, the standard library contains thousands of functions.

2057
03:24:43,054 --> 03:24:45,220
So much power right at our fingertips.

2058
03:24:46,096 --> 03:24:47,588
Well, almost.

2059
03:24:48,430 --> 03:24:54,276
Just like a book, a Python function has to be checked out of its library before we can use it.

2060
03:24:55,086 --> 03:24:59,296
If we skip that checkout and just try to use a function, like randint,

2061
03:25:00,718 --> 03:25:01,604
we get an error.

2062
03:25:02,496 --> 03:25:08,496
To check out the function from the random module, we import

2063
03:25:09,518 --> 03:25:10,096
randint.

2064
03:25:11,118 --> 03:25:16,580
Once the randint function is checked out, we can use it in the rest of our code.

2065
03:25:17,422 --> 03:25:20,096
Running our program again, there's no error.

2066
03:25:20,416 --> 03:25:23,268
Python picked a number between 1 and 6.

2067
03:25:24,078 --> 03:25:27,876
Now that's a neat little function we could use to build a dice game.

2068
03:25:28,846 --> 03:25:33,156
One import line was all it took to check randint out of the library.

2069
03:25:34,016 --> 03:25:38,500
And a similar line could be used to import any function in the standard library.

2070
03:25:39,342 --> 03:25:45,956
However, importing randint was only that easy because we already knew it lives in the random module.

2071
03:25:46,816 --> 03:25:53,220
With hundreds of modules in the standard library, it's impossible to remember where every function is located.

2072
03:25:54,030 --> 03:26:01,444
As a result, it's often hard to tell whether the tool we need already exists, or whether we'd have to write it ourselves.

2073
03:26:02,286 --> 03:26:05,924
That's an issue we may run into as we keep building our dice game.

2074
03:26:06,766 --> 03:26:13,796
For example, say we want to introduce players into our game and assign them a random turn order.

2075
03:26:14,766 --> 03:26:17,508
Is there a function in the standard library that can do that?

2076
03:26:18,478 --> 03:26:20,260
And if so, where is it located?

2077
03:26:21,102 --> 03:26:24,416
We can answer both questions by going to one place.

2078
03:26:24,656 --> 03:26:28,388
Python's official documentation known as the Docs.

2079
03:26:29,230 --> 03:26:36,004
The docs live on a website, and on that website we can find information on everything in the standard library.

2080
03:26:36,846 --> 03:26:41,124
To find the docs, we open our browser and do a Google search.

2081
03:26:43,278 --> 03:26:50,052
Clicking on the first result, we land on a web page that links to every module in the standard library.

2082
03:26:50,896 --> 03:26:55,876
Randomizing the items in a list sounds like a job for the random module.

2083
03:26:56,718 --> 03:26:59,652
To find that module, we can scroll through the list.

2084
03:27:03,022 --> 03:27:04,964
There it is, the random module.

2085
03:27:05,806 --> 03:27:11,652
So let's open the module's documentation page and see if the tool we need is listed there.

2086
03:27:12,496 --> 03:27:13,796
Skimming through,

2087
03:27:16,656 --> 03:27:18,852
one function jumps out, shuffle.

2088
03:27:19,662 --> 03:27:25,444
According to the documentation, that function rearranges the items of a list into a random order.

2089
03:27:26,286 --> 03:27:27,696
That's exactly what we need.

2090
03:27:28,686 --> 03:27:32,256
So let's switch back to our code and import that function.

2091
03:27:33,678 --> 03:27:39,044
From here, let's use the shuffle function to rearrange our list of names into a random order.

2092
03:27:39,886 --> 03:27:43,716
Running the code, we get our list of names back in a new order.

2093
03:27:44,672 --> 03:27:49,604
And if we run the code again, the names usually come back in a different order.

2094
03:27:50,432 --> 03:27:52,228
So we got the job done.

2095
03:27:52,686 --> 03:27:56,196
But finding the right tool took a lot of manual digging.

2096
03:27:57,038 --> 03:28:01,700
And that's despite being able to guess that the tool we needed was in the random module.

2097
03:28:02,510 --> 03:28:04,324
We won't always be that lucky.

2098
03:28:05,166 --> 03:28:12,804
For example, in our dice game, let's say we want to visualize the number of wins each player has using a chart.

2099
03:28:13,614 --> 03:28:17,540
Can the random, math, or statistics modules help us here?

2100
03:28:18,382 --> 03:28:21,572
None of those sound like places graphing functions would live.

2101
03:28:22,414 --> 03:28:26,432
Since we're not sure where to start, let's go back to the standard library docs.

2102
03:28:27,310 --> 03:28:31,748
If we try searching for a chart module, nothing useful turns

2103
03:28:32,558 --> 03:28:38,628
up. And if we kept digging, we'd just be wasting our time. Charting tools aren't part of the standard library.

2104
03:28:39,472 --> 03:28:45,892
But creating a chart is such a common need that the Python community has built several libraries for it.

2105
03:28:46,734 --> 03:28:53,092
To find which community library is best for our needs, we could head to Google and search for it.

2106
03:28:54,592 --> 03:28:57,892
But in 2026, that's often not the most efficient way.

2107
03:28:58,752 --> 03:29:04,676
Instead, we can usually get a more direct answer by asking an AI assistant like Claude.

2108
03:29:05,518 --> 03:29:10,020
In plain English, let's ask Claude which library we should use to draw a chart.

2109
03:29:12,110 --> 03:29:18,820
Claude's answer suggests that Matplotlib is the popular choice for creating charts in Python. So we'll go with that.

2110
03:29:19,632 --> 03:29:24,420
But before we can put Matplotlib to work, we have to get it onto our machine.

2111
03:29:25,230 --> 03:29:29,924
Matplotlib is our first external library built by the community.

2112
03:29:31,472 --> 03:29:37,028
It doesn't come packaged with Python the same way that the rand int and shuffle functions do.

2113
03:29:37,870 --> 03:29:38,752
As a result,

2114
03:29:39,712 --> 03:29:45,540
trying to import the library without installing it first gives us an error.

2115
03:29:46,382 --> 03:29:51,844
Before we import an external library, we need to download it from the internet and install it.

2116
03:29:52,686 --> 03:29:56,580
This process is handled by Python's package manager, Pip.

2117
03:29:57,422 --> 03:30:05,632
To download Matplotlib, Pip turns to one giant catalog, the Python Package Index, or PyPI.

2118
03:30:06,592 --> 03:30:11,012
We can browse the PyPI catalog ourselves by going to its website.

2119
03:30:11,854 --> 03:30:15,684
And if you want to explore the website on your own, we left a link in the description.

2120
03:30:16,592 --> 03:30:21,796
PyPI is where hundreds of thousands of packages live, including Matplotlib.

2121
03:30:22,638 --> 03:30:28,548
Searching for Matplotlib and clicking the link brings us to its page.

2122
03:30:29,422 --> 03:30:35,652
This page provides some information about the package, as well as a link to its documentation.

2123
03:30:36,592 --> 03:30:43,492
And back at the top of the page, we can find the pip command we'll use to install Matplotlib on our machine.

2124
03:30:44,334 --> 03:30:46,788
So let's head back to our notebook to do that.

2125
03:30:47,598 --> 03:30:52,740
Since we're working in a notebook, in a new code cell, we can run the pip command.

2126
03:30:53,632 --> 03:30:59,684
But if we install Matplotlib like this, we need an exclamation point at the beginning of the line.

2127
03:31:00,526 --> 03:31:06,820
That exclamation point tells the notebook to run this line as a terminal command, not as Python code.

2128
03:31:07,662 --> 03:31:16,432
If you were using a regular.py file instead of a notebook, you'd install matplotlib by going to the terminal and writing pip install

2129
03:31:16,592 --> 03:31:17,636
matplotlib.

2130
03:31:18,446 --> 03:31:20,772
Running the cell, pip gets to work.

2131
03:31:21,614 --> 03:31:23,552
It finds matplotlib online,

2132
03:31:24,352 --> 03:31:28,004
downloads it, and adds it to our Python installation.

2133
03:31:28,814 --> 03:31:33,252
Beyond installing libraries, pip can upgrade and remove them too.

2134
03:31:34,062 --> 03:31:39,268
If you want to learn more about pip, we're working on a video, and the link will be in the description once it's ready.

2135
03:31:40,110 --> 03:31:44,996
Now that Matplotlib is installed on our computer, any program we write can import it.

2136
03:31:45,838 --> 03:31:50,756
We won't need to run this command again in the future, so we can delete the cell from our notebook.

2137
03:31:51,566 --> 03:31:58,788
Running the import cell again, the error goes away, which means our program now recognizes that Matplotlib is installed.

2138
03:31:59,630 --> 03:32:04,644
With the import error gone, visualizing the player wins is just a few function calls away.

2139
03:32:05,486 --> 03:32:10,596
To figure out which functions we need, PyPI doesn't offer much help.

2140
03:32:11,438 --> 03:32:16,388
The role of PyPI is simply to catalog available libraries.

2141
03:32:17,230 --> 03:32:21,732
But the page does provide a link to Matplotlib's own documentation website.

2142
03:32:22,544 --> 03:32:25,508
Here, we'll find a lot more information.

2143
03:32:26,350 --> 03:32:32,100
For example, we can navigate to the reference section to see all the available modules.

2144
03:32:32,944 --> 03:32:34,820
That is a lot of options.

2145
03:32:35,662 --> 03:32:39,716
Determining exactly which ones we need could turn into an afternoon's project.

2146
03:32:40,526 --> 03:32:47,396
To speed that process up, let's go back to Claude and ask which functions and modules to use.

2147
03:32:50,094 --> 03:33:00,068
After thinking a bit, Claude points us to two functions in matplotlib's pyplot module, bar to draw the chart, and show to display it.

2148
03:33:01,504 --> 03:33:06,052
So, back in our code, we'll need to import both of those functions.

2149
03:33:06,894 --> 03:33:08,516
Let's start with the bar function.

2150
03:33:09,358 --> 03:33:15,684
The intuitive way to import the bar function might be to use the same syntax as we did with the randon function.

2151
03:33:16,494 --> 03:33:20,384
Simply write from pyplot importbar.

2152
03:33:21,614 --> 03:33:25,252
But if we try to run the cell, we get an error.

2153
03:33:26,094 --> 03:33:28,996
The message says there's no module named pyplot.

2154
03:33:29,838 --> 03:33:33,063
That's strange, since we definitely installed matplotlib.

2155
03:33:34,094 --> 03:33:39,204
Why could Python find the random module earlier, but not the Pyplot module now?

2156
03:33:40,014 --> 03:33:41,636
The error type gives us a clue.

2157
03:33:42,478 --> 03:33:47,588
Module not found means Python went looking for the module and came back empty-handed.

2158
03:33:48,398 --> 03:33:52,324
To understand why, let's discuss what happens when we request a module.

2159
03:33:53,166 --> 03:34:00,036
When running an import statement, Python searches for the module in the library directory of our Python installation.

2160
03:34:00,846 --> 03:34:08,196
Every Python module, whether it's part of the standard library or an external library, is a real file on our computer.

2161
03:34:09,024 --> 03:34:13,508
So to import a module, Python has to first find its file.

2162
03:34:14,350 --> 03:34:20,420
Many standard library modules like random and statistics sit at the top level of that structure.

2163
03:34:21,230 --> 03:34:25,252
Because they are at the top level, Python can find them by name alone.

2164
03:34:26,383 --> 03:34:32,612
Matplotlib, though, is a folder with nested module files, including Pyplot.

2165
03:34:33,454 --> 03:34:37,380
Because of this structure, we can't just name the module we want to import from.

2166
03:34:37,664 --> 03:34:40,132
We have to specify exactly where it lives.

2167
03:34:40,974 --> 03:34:46,544
We do this by writing the library name, followed by a dot, and then the module

2168
03:34:46,784 --> 03:34:50,852
name. Similarly, we can also import the show function.

2169
03:34:51,694 --> 03:34:56,900
Running the code, we no longer get an error, so we've properly imported both functions.

2170
03:34:57,710 --> 03:34:59,556
Now we can put them to work.

2171
03:35:00,398 --> 03:35:05,700
To create the chart, we call bar, passing in both lists as arguments.

2172
03:35:06,542 --> 03:35:11,300
The first list, players, will label the bars along the bottom of the chart.

2173
03:35:12,270 --> 03:35:15,780
The second list, wins, will set each bar's height.

2174
03:35:16,590 --> 03:35:19,076
To display the chart, we'll call show.

2175
03:35:19,918 --> 03:35:25,092
Since its only job is to display what we've just drawn, show needs no arguments.

2176
03:35:25,934 --> 03:35:31,172
Running the code, we get a bar chart that shows the number of wins each player has.

2177
03:35:32,014 --> 03:35:36,516
Back at the top of our cell though, our imports are starting to stack up.

2178
03:35:37,230 --> 03:35:40,388
Bar and show each take a separate import line.

2179
03:35:41,198 --> 03:35:44,096
For two functions, that's not a huge cost.

2180
03:35:44,976 --> 03:35:53,616
But if we wanted to use more functions from Pyplot, we'd need to add more lines at the top of our program, each starting with the same from

2181
03:35:53,936 --> 03:35:56,736
matplotlib.pyplot import.

2182
03:35:57,966 --> 03:35:59,716
That's a lot of repetition.

2183
03:36:00,558 --> 03:36:07,812
To reduce the boilerplate code and keep our program leaner, we can import several functions on a single line.

2184
03:36:08,654 --> 03:36:13,796
After the import keyword, we list all the functions we want, separated by a comma.

2185
03:36:14,638 --> 03:36:21,892
Then, if we want to import more functions from the same module, we add them to the end of the line, separated with a comma.

2186
03:36:22,734 --> 03:36:31,044
For example, if we wanted to add a title and a label to the y-axis, we could add both of those functions at the end of the import line.

2187
03:36:31,886 --> 03:36:35,300
We can then use these functions in our code after the bar call.

2188
03:36:36,270 --> 03:36:38,084
Both take a string as an argument.

2189
03:36:38,894 --> 03:36:40,004
Running the code,

2190
03:36:41,296 --> 03:36:45,124
our chart now has a title, and its y-axis is labeled.

2191
03:36:45,966 --> 03:36:46,948
That's great.

2192
03:36:47,438 --> 03:36:51,492
But we could add an almost endless number of customizations to our chart.

2193
03:36:51,856 --> 03:36:55,556
Grid lines, a legend, a label for the other axis.

2194
03:36:56,558 --> 03:37:03,524
Each of these features would require importing a new function, which would make our import line harder and harder to read.

2195
03:37:04,366 --> 03:37:12,388
To avoid listing all the functions we want individually, we can instead import everything in the PyPlot module at once.

2196
03:37:13,296 --> 03:37:20,900
But when we import the whole module instead of individual functions, we can no longer call the functions the same way.

2197
03:37:21,774 --> 03:37:26,692
Indeed, if we try running our code in a fresh notebook, we get an error.

2198
03:37:27,534 --> 03:37:33,508
The reason is that our updated import line brought in all those functions under a single name.

2199
03:37:34,350 --> 03:37:40,420
To use a specific function out of all the ones we imported, we need to specify its full path.

2200
03:37:41,230 --> 03:37:47,684
For example, to use bar, we need to add matplotlib.pyplot as a prefix.

2201
03:37:48,526 --> 03:37:51,460
We must do the same for all the other functions as well.

2202
03:37:52,302 --> 03:37:54,788
Running the code, we get our chart back.

2203
03:37:55,790 --> 03:38:02,948
But writing out matplotlib.pyplot every time we reach for one of its functions is tedious.

2204
03:38:03,790 --> 03:38:09,924
To solve the tedium, Python lets us define a shorter nickname for the module using the as keyword.

2205
03:38:10,734 --> 03:38:15,748
The nickname convention for Pyplot is PLT, but you can use any name you want.

2206
03:38:16,590 --> 03:38:21,668
Once that nickname is defined, we can use it to call any function in the Pyplot module.

2207
03:38:25,006 --> 03:38:27,716
Using that nickname makes the code easier to read.

2208
03:38:28,576 --> 03:38:31,332
And when we run the code, we still get our chart.

2209
03:38:32,174 --> 03:38:32,964
Awesome!

2210
03:38:33,806 --> 03:38:41,936
By finding, installing, and importing Matplotlib, we pulled an entire charting library into our own program,

2211
03:38:42,176 --> 03:38:44,132
all in just a few lines of code.

2212
03:38:44,974 --> 03:38:49,092
The same steps work for every library the Python community has shared.

2213
03:38:49,936 --> 03:38:54,628
Working with a giant spreadsheet of data, the pandas library is your friend.

2214
03:38:55,470 --> 03:38:57,412
Pulling live data from a website,

2215
03:38:58,222 --> 03:39:00,388
the requests library can help there.

2216
03:39:01,326 --> 03:39:03,876
Other libraries let us edit images,

2217
03:39:04,590 --> 03:39:08,324
build games, or even run scientific simulations.

2218
03:39:09,166 --> 03:39:13,540
Whatever the task, chances are a library already exists for the job.

2219
03:39:14,896 --> 03:39:17,572
That is where Python's real power comes from.

2220
03:39:18,736 --> 03:39:27,856
Now let's head to the practice notebook linked in the description and practice using a few of these libraries ourselves. If you want to try it on your own first, pause the video here.

2221
03:39:28,016 --> 03:39:30,916
Then we'll walk through the solutions together step by step.

2222
03:39:31,566 --> 03:39:36,004
You just crossed the finish line of a 5K race, well behind the time you trained for.

2223
03:39:36,576 --> 03:39:40,868
Before you start your next training block, you want to figure out why you were so slow.

2224
03:39:41,454 --> 03:39:43,460
The evidence is right on your computer.

2225
03:39:43,856 --> 03:39:49,028
Because you're a Python programmer, you logged every run in your training block in a Python dictionary.

2226
03:39:50,158 --> 03:39:55,236
The dictionary stores the run dates as keys and the run distances as values.

2227
03:39:57,710 --> 03:40:07,844
You have several theories about why your race didn't go well. Maybe your average run was too short, maybe the training block was too short, maybe your longest runs never went far enough.

2228
03:40:08,782 --> 03:40:14,596
You could work all that out by hand, averaging the distances and counting the days off a calendar yourself.

2229
03:40:15,598 --> 03:40:16,708
But you don't need to.

2230
03:40:17,104 --> 03:40:24,164
The tools for these jobs already exist in Python's standard library and in libraries built by the Python community.

2231
03:40:25,070 --> 03:40:30,788
So we're going to practice finding and using the right tool for each job, one problem at a time.

2232
03:40:31,662 --> 03:40:35,684
If you want to follow along with us, the notebook we're using is linked in the description.

2233
